// Scenario Lab asset library — Phase 7A: mindset
const _AL_mindset = {"id":"mindset","title":"Phase 7A: mindset","chapters":[{"id":"ch05","title":"Introduction","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"False growth mindset vs. true growth mindset","visual_frame_description":"A leadership-training workshop in an Austin conference room. Trainer Maya Chen stands before a whiteboard divided into three columns, and the wall is covered with sticky notes carrying the things managers say when they claim to embrace growth. Workshop participants watch as the player must drag each sticky note into the correct column on the board.","player_mission":"Sort each spoken claim into the correct column: a fixed-mindset claim, a genuine growth-mindset claim, or a false growth mindset (a well-intentioned but inaccurate implementation). Decide the verdict for the manager's slogan in the question first, then file the rest of the evidence items.","key_concepts":["Dweck's research shows that a simple belief about yourself guides a large part of your life and permeates every part of it.","Much of what people think of as their personality actually grows out of their mindset, according to the book.","Much of what may prevent a person from fulfilling their potential grows out of their mindset.","The tradition of psychology Dweck works in shows that beliefs, whether we are aware of them or not, strongly affect what we want and whether we succeed in getting it.","Changing people's beliefs, even the simplest beliefs, can have profound effects on their lives.","An entire organization can have a mindset; Dweck added a study on organizational mindsets to the business chapter in the updated edition.","A 'false growth mindset' refers to the many creative ways people interpreted and implemented the growth mindset, not always accurately.","Dweck added a section called 'The Journey to a (True) Growth Mindset' because people asked how to actually take that journey.","The book closes each chapter with ways to apply the lessons: recognize the mindset guiding your life, understand how it works, and change it if you wish.","A genuine growth mindset is the belief that basic qualities can be cultivated, not merely a slogan of cheerleading and effort praise."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction between a genuine growth mindset and a false implementation: Dweck notes in the Introduction that people found creative ways to interpret and implement the growth mindset 'not always accurately.' A true growth mindset is the belief that your basic qualities can be cultivated through effort, strategies, and help from others, not mere enthusiasm.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"A manager announces, 'We tell everyone \"You can do it!\" and then keep judging them the same way.' Which column does this claim belong in?","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset claim","column_b_label":"Genuine growth mindset","column_c_label":"False growth mindset (misapplication)","rows":[{"label":"Talent is scarce; only a few people are born with it.","col":"a"},{"label":"Every employee can develop skills through effort, better strategies, and good coaching.","col":"b"},{"label":"We praise everyone's effort so nobody feels bad, even when nobody learns anything.","col":"c"},{"label":"I have to prove my intelligence every single day or people will see I'm not smart.","col":"a"},{"label":"Setbacks are information I can use to improve my next attempt.","col":"b"},{"label":"We tell everyone 'You can do it!' and then keep judging them the same way.","col":"c"},{"label":"If I ask for help, it will only reveal my limitations.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["It belongs in column a because it focuses on fixed traits like talent.","It belongs in column b because cheering people on is the essence of a growth mindset.","It belongs in no column because it is purely about company policy."],"explanation":"The slogan keeps the old judgmental practices in place while only changing the words, so it is a false growth mindset: a well-meaning but inaccurate interpretation of Dweck's idea. A genuine growth mindset changes what people believe about developing ability and what they actually do to cultivate it, which is why the claim cannot be sorted into the fixed column or the true growth column."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Implementing the growth mindset in an organization","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled meeting room at a software firm in Seattle. Victor, a vice president, is surrounded by posters reading 'Effort Wins!' and 'Growth Every Day!' while engineers outside visibly hide a failed prototype review behind a closed door. The player sits across from Victor as a workplace consultant called in to diagnose why the culture has not actually changed.","player_mission":"Interview Victor to reveal how the organization is misusing the growth mindset, then choose the response that turns their approach into a genuine growth-minded one. Pick the single most accurate and effective move to end the dialogue.","key_concepts":["Dweck added a new section on the 'false growth mindset' after learning about the many creative ways people were interpreting and implementing the growth mindset, not always accurately.","A whole organization can have a mindset, as Dweck's study on organizational mindsets showed.","The growth mindset is based on the belief that basic qualities can be cultivated through effort, strategies, and help from others.","People's ideas about risk and effort grow out of their more basic mindset, not out of random personality quirks.","When people are put in a fixed mindset, they quickly fear challenge and devalue effort.","When people are taught the growth mindset, their ideas about challenge and effort follow from it.","Books of tips like 'take more risks!' leave you inspired for a few days but never show how the pieces fit together.","Understanding the two mindsets shows exactly how one thing leads to another, producing what psychologists call an Aha! experience.","Dweck receives letters from coaches and business leaders who recognize themselves and reorient their thinking after reading her work.","The Introduction promises that recognizing the mindset guiding your life is the first step toward changing it."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the false growth mindset in organizations: people can adopt the vocabulary of growth while keeping fixed-mindset judgments and behaviors, which Dweck says happens 'not always accurately.' A genuine growth mindset requires changing the belief that qualities can be cultivated, which then changes what leaders do with failure, effort, and help.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Victor, a vice president at a mid-sized software company, rolled out a growth-mindset campaign with posters, slogans, and an 'Effort Wins' motto. Engineers still hide their bugs and hoard credit, and the last failed prototype produced no learning. As a consultant, your goal is to diagnose whether the implementation is genuine and to guide Victor to a real growth-minded practice before the culture does more damage.","dialogue":["Victor: We rolled out the growth mindset last quarter. T-shirts, posters, the whole thing. Our motto is 'Effort Wins!'","Player: What actually happened when an engineer submitted a risky prototype that failed in review?","Victor: We told the team 'nice effort' and moved on. Nobody learns anything, but nobody feels bad. That is the growth mindset, right? Never punish, always praise the effort.","Player: Not exactly. Growth mindset means believing skills can be cultivated through effort, strategies, and help from others. Praising effort while ignoring whether anyone learned is a false growth mindset.","Victor: Hmm. So you're saying my campaign is all slogan and no substance?","Player: The evidence is in what people do with failure. Do teams analyze what the failure taught them and change their approach, or do they just get comforted?","Victor: They just get comforted. And the same prototypes fail again in three months.","Player: Then let's change that. Turn every failed prototype into a short learning review so the effort is connected to strategy and growth."],"choices":["Treat the failed prototype as data: have the team analyze what it taught them, adjust the strategy, and target coaching where the gaps are.","Keep the current policy, since effort praise protects psychological safety and that is what matters most.","Stop praising entirely and return to judging only the outcome, because the praise is clearly confusing people.","Tell Victor to remove the engineers whose prototypes fail so the team learns to avoid risk."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"False growth mindset versus genuine growth mindset: praising effort without connecting it to learning is an inaccurate implementation of Dweck's idea, whereas a genuine growth mindset believes qualities can be cultivated through effort, strategies, and help from others.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 works because it ties effort to strategy and learning, the actual engine of growth, and directly contradicts the false growth mindset Victor built. Choice 1 keeps the false growth mindset alive by rewarding effort as an end in itself. Choice 2 collapses into pure outcome judgment, which feeds the fixed mindset. Choice 3 punishes failure and forces risk-avoidance, the opposite of the growth mindset."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Beliefs drive organizational outcomes","visual_frame_description":"A strategy room at NovaRail, a struggling transit-equipment firm. The founder, Garrett, has filled the walls with photos of himself and a small circle of 'proven stars.' Team members whisper about a recent derailment report being buried, and a junior engineer quietly hides her analysis that would have caught the fault. The player is a turnaround consultant studying the org before making one decisive move.","player_mission":"Identify the belief that is driving NovaRail's failure culture, then choose the intervention that shifts the organization toward a genuine growth mindset. Beat the boss by seeing the mindset beneath the symptoms.","key_concepts":["A whole organization can have a mindset, according to Dweck's research on organizational mindsets.","Believing your qualities are carved in stone creates an urgency to prove yourself over and over.","In a fixed-mindset culture, every situation is evaluated as 'will I look smart or dumb, accepted or rejected, a winner or a loser.'","The growth mindset treats the hand you are dealt as just the starting point for development.","In the growth mindset, everyone can change and grow through application and experience.","Risk and effort are two things that might reveal your inadequacies if you hold a fixed mindset.","When people are put in a fixed mindset, they quickly fear challenge and devalue effort.","The growth mindset's passion for stretching yourself and sticking to it is what lets people thrive during challenging times.","Changing people's beliefs, even the simplest beliefs, can have profound effects on what they achieve.","Recognizing the mindset guiding your life is the first step to understanding and changing it."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests how a shared belief can become an organizational force: Dweck reports that an entire organization can have a fixed or growth mindset, and that a fixed mindset makes people hide mistakes and fear challenge because every situation feels like a verdict. The theory says beliefs, not just individual talent, determine whether people risk, learn, and correct course.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the organization's fixed mindset: the belief that talent is a scarce, permanent resource that must be proved, which makes employees bury failures, avoid challenge, and protect their reputations instead of learning.","stage_1_observation":"You spend a week at NovaRail watching the culture. Garrett recruits only 'proven stars' and celebrates the same two people at every all-hands. A junior engineer, Priya, built a model that would have predicted last month's derailment, but she told no one because 'rocking the boat gets you labeled.' When you ask about the incident report, three managers change the subject, and one quietly shreds the draft.","stage_1_question":"Which belief is most likely driving NovaRail's culture of hidden failures and self-protection?","stage_1_correct_answer":"That talent is a fixed, scarce resource that must be proved over and over, so every mistake feels like evidence of deficiency and every risk feels like exposure.","stage_2_intervention":"Garrett asks you what single change would turn NovaRail around. You know slogans will fail, and you know pure outcome punishment will deepen the hiding. Decide which intervention actually creates a genuine growth mindset in the organization.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Launch a public failure-review ritual where teams present what went wrong, what it taught them, and what they will change, with Garrett visibly rewarding honest analysis of process rather than blaming people — and seat the 'proven stars' alongside junior engineers so that help and learning flow openly."}]},{"id":"ch06","title":"Chapter 1: The Mindsets","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Fixed vs. growth reactions to the bad-day vignette","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen research playback. On the left, a transcript of Dweck's bad-day vignette scrolls: a C+ midterm, a parking ticket, and a friend brushing you off. On the right, speech bubbles float from real respondents, some reading 'I'm slime' and others reading 'I'll pay the ticket and call my friend.' The player must sort each bubble into the correct reaction column.","player_mission":"Sort each spoken reaction to the C+ vignette into the fixed-mindset column, the growth-mindset column, or the neutral column. Start with the verdict for the question, then classify the remaining responses from the study.","key_concepts":["In the bad-day vignette, the grade was intentionally a C+ and not an F, a midterm and not a final, a ticket and not a car wreck — nothing catastrophic or irreversible happened.","People with the fixed mindset saw the events as a direct measure of their competence and worth.","Fixed-mindset respondents said things like 'I'd feel like a reject,' 'I'm a total failure,' and 'I'm slime.'","Fixed-mindset respondents described their lives as 'pitiful' and said 'the world is out to get me.'","Fixed-mindset people cope by withdrawing: 'Do nothing,' 'Stay in bed,' 'Get drunk,' 'Go into my closet and sit there.'","From this raw material of a grade, a ticket, and a bad phone call, the fixed mindset created the feeling of utter failure and paralysis.","Growth-mindset respondents thought 'I need to try harder in class, be more careful when parking the car, and wonder if my friend had a bad day.'","Growth-mindset respondents coped directly: study differently, pay the ticket, work things out with the friend.","People in the fixed mindset are not simply low in self-esteem or card-carrying pessimists; when not coping with failure, they feel as worthy and optimistic as growth-minded people.","Both mindsets feel distress; the difference is that growth-minded people stay ready to take risks and keep working even while distressed."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests attribution following a setback: people with the fixed mindset interpret a mild failure as proof of permanent incompetence and withdraw, while people with the growth mindset see it as information and cope directly. Dweck built the vignette to show that a fixed mindset can manufacture 'utter failure and paralysis' from events that were deliberately minor and reversible.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"A student who receives a C+ on the midterm thinks, 'I'm a total failure. Everyone's better than me.' Which column does this response belong in?","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset reaction","column_b_label":"Growth-mindset reaction","column_c_label":"Neutral statement (no mindset content)","rows":[{"label":"I'm a total failure. Everyone's better than me.","col":"a"},{"label":"I'd look at what was wrong on my exam, resolve to do better, pay my parking ticket, and call my friend.","col":"b"},{"label":"The professor handed back the midterm on a Thursday afternoon.","col":"c"},{"label":"Stay in bed and eat chocolate.","col":"a"},{"label":"I have the rest of the semester to pull up my grade.","col":"b"},{"label":"I'm the most unlucky person on this earth.","col":"a"},{"label":"I need to try harder in class, be more careful when parking, and wonder if my friend had a bad day.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["A growth-mindset reaction, because it shows honesty about the grade.","A neutral statement, because it simply describes the student's feelings.","A growth-mindset reaction, because it reflects healthy self-criticism."],"explanation":"'I'm a total failure' turns a mild, reversible event into a global judgment of the self, which is the signature of the fixed mindset: every situation becomes a measure of competence and worth. The neutral item only reports a fact, and the 'pull up my grade' item is growth-minded because it treats the grade as information and a starting point for effort."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The nature vs. nurture debate about human qualities","visual_frame_description":"A museum-style exhibit on 'Why Do People Differ?' Three display panels show historical views of intelligence: one panel of skulls labeled phrenology and craniology, one panel of classrooms and training programs, and one panel with a double helix entwined with a schoolyard. A narrator cue asks the player to file historical claims under the panel they belong to.","player_mission":"Sort each historical claim about why people differ into the nature-based column, the experience-based column, or the interactionist column. Decide the verdict for the question first, then file the remaining claims from Binet, Gottlieb, and Sternberg.","key_concepts":["Through the ages, alleged physical bases for human differences have included bumps on the skull (phrenology), the size and shape of the skull (craniology), and, today, genes.","Alfred Binet invented the IQ test to identify children who were not profiting from the Paris public schools so that new educational programs could get them back on track.","Binet did not see IQ as a summary of unchangeable intelligence; he believed education and practice could bring about fundamental changes in intelligence.","Binet protested against what he called 'brutal pessimism,' the claim that an individual's intelligence is a fixed quantity that cannot be increased.","Binet wrote that with practice, training, and above all method, people 'literally become more intelligent than we were before.'","Most experts today agree that it is not either-or; from conception on there is a constant give-and-take between genes and environment.","Gilbert Gottlieb argued that genes require input from the environment to work properly.","Scientists now find that people have more capacity for lifelong learning and brain development than they ever thought.","Robert Sternberg writes that the major factor in whether people achieve expertise is not some fixed prior ability but 'purposeful engagement.'","It is not always the people who start out the smartest who end up the smartest."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the historical debate that the two mindsets grow out of: whether human qualities are fixed physical endowments or things that can be cultivated. The book argues the answer is interactionist, since genes and environment cooperate from conception, which is why no one is locked in by their starting point.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"A neuroscientist states, 'Not only do genes and environment cooperate as we develop, but genes require input from the environment to work properly.' Which column does this claim belong in?","column_a_label":"Nature-based explanation","column_b_label":"Experience-based explanation","column_c_label":"Interactionist view (genes + environment)","rows":[{"label":"Bumps on the skull determine intelligence (phrenology).","col":"a"},{"label":"New educational programs can get struggling children back on track.","col":"b"},{"label":"Not only do genes and environment cooperate as we develop, but genes require input from the environment to work properly.","col":"c"},{"label":"An individual's intelligence is a fixed quantity that cannot be increased.","col":"a"},{"label":"With practice, training, and above all method, we become more intelligent than we were before.","col":"b"},{"label":"From conception on, there is a constant give-and-take between genes and environment.","col":"c"},{"label":"The size and shape of the skull reveal permanent differences between people.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["It is a nature-based explanation because it mentions genes.","It is an experience-based explanation because it downplays the role of genes.","It is a fixed-trait claim because genes cannot change."],"explanation":"Gottlieb's claim is interactionist: it keeps genes central while insisting they need environmental input to operate, so it cannot be filed as a one-sided nature or nurture position. The skull and fixed-intelligence items belong in the nature column, and the training and education items belong in the experience column."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The language-class exercise: ego under evaluation","visual_frame_description":"A brightly lit Spanish classroom in Chicago. Instructor Señora Reyes has called a student, Miguel, to the front of the room and is firing questions at him while the class watches. A classmate, Lena, whispers warnings from her desk, and the instructor's expression hovers between encouragement and judgment. The player chooses Miguel's response as the scene plays out.","player_mission":"Take on the role of Miguel at the board and respond to the instructor's drill. Choose the response that treats the moment as a learning opportunity rather than a test of fixed ability, and avoid the responses driven by ego and the fear of looking dumb.","key_concepts":["When you put yourself in a fixed mindset, your ability is on the line and you can feel your ego bristle and waver.","In the fixed mindset, the instructor's face feels like it is evaluating you and every question is a test of worth.","In the growth mindset, you are a novice and that is why you are here: you are here to learn.","In the growth mindset, the teacher is a resource for learning rather than a judge.","The message of the exercise is that you can change your mindset.","The growth mindset believes basic qualities can be cultivated through your efforts, your strategies, and help from others.","The passion for stretching yourself and sticking to it, even when it is not going well, is the hallmark of the growth mindset.","People with the growth mindset do not hide deficiencies; they want to overcome them.","Fixed-mindset people see every situation as one to confirm their intelligence, personality, or character.","Risk and effort are things a fixed mindset sees as possibly revealing that you are not up to the task."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the fixed-versus-growth mindset under direct evaluation: in the fixed mindset, being questioned in front of a class feels like a verdict on your ability, while in the growth mindset you are a novice whose teacher is a resource. The book's own exercise shows that the same situation produces opposite emotions and behavior depending on which mindset is active.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"You signed up for a Spanish course and, a few sessions in, the instructor calls you to the front of the room and starts throwing questions at you one after another. Your classmate Lena whispers warnings, and everyone is watching. Your goal is to keep learning from the drill, not to protect your ego; the stakes are whether you leave the front of the room wiser or just humiliated.","dialogue":["Señora Reyes: Miguel, front and center. Let's warm up with some quick questions. No pressure — just show me what you remember.","Lena: (whispering) Everyone is watching. You better not freeze up.","Señora Reyes: ¿Dónde está la biblioteca?","Miguel: (hesitating) La biblioteca está... está... al lado del café?","Lena: (whispering) Told you.","Señora Reyes: Almost — it is next to the auditorium. Don't worry. Now, tell me honestly, why did you hesitate there?","Miguel: (the whole class waits for an answer)","Señora Reyes: Take your time. This is practice."],"choices":["Say, 'I hesitated because I am still practicing this tense. Could you show me how you would form that sentence?' and keep trying aloud.","Go quiet, mumble 'I guess I'm just not good at languages,' and sit down so the attention moves away from me.","Bluff a confident 'Está al lado del... teatro' even though I am not sure, hoping the next question is easier.","Snap at Señora Reyes, 'Why are you putting me on the spot? This is humiliating.'"],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The fixed-versus-growth mindset under evaluation: a fixed mindset makes your ego bristle and waver because your ability feels on the line, while a growth mindset lets the tension leave you because you are a novice and the teacher is a resource for learning.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 works because it treats the stumble as data, openly asks for help, and keeps practicing — exactly the growth-minded move the book describes. Choice 1 hides the deficiency and withdraws, confirming failure. Choice 2 bluffs to protect the ego, so no learning occurs. Choice 3 makes the situation adversarial, turning the teacher into an enemy instead of a resource."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Self-insight: accurate views of your assets and limitations","visual_frame_description":"A rooftop cafe at sunset in San Francisco. Priya, a graphic designer, slumps over a rejected portfolio review, and a candid senior colleague, Elena, has just told her the truth about her deadline estimates. The player sits with them as a mentor trying to help Priya find an accurate picture of her skills.","player_mission":"Coach Priya through her reaction to candid feedback. Choose the response that helps her develop accurate self-knowledge rather than distortion or reassurance, and guide her to use the feedback as information for growth.","key_concepts":["Studies show that people are terrible at estimating their abilities.","In Dweck's research, the people with the fixed mindset accounted for almost all the inaccuracy in estimating their performance and ability.","People with the growth mindset were amazingly accurate at estimating their abilities.","If you believe you can develop yourself, you are open to accurate information about your current abilities, even if it is unflattering.","If you are oriented toward learning, you need accurate information about your current abilities in order to learn effectively.","If everything is either good news or bad news about your precious traits, distortion almost inevitably enters the picture.","Fixed-mindset people magnify some outcomes and explain away others, until they no longer know themselves.","Howard Gardner concluded that exceptional individuals have a special talent for identifying their own strengths and weaknesses.","The growth mindset leads people to seek friends who challenge them to grow rather than just shore up their self-esteem.","The fixed mindset makes you concerned with how you will be judged; the growth mindset makes you concerned with improving."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests self-insight and accuracy: fixed-mindset people distort information about their abilities because every outcome is good or bad news about their traits, while growth-mindset people are open to accurate, even unflattering, feedback because they need it to learn. Dweck found that fixed-mindset people accounted for almost all the inaccuracy in estimating their performance.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Priya, a graphic designer, just got her first candid review: her work is strong but her deadline estimates are consistently off, and the client has noticed. She keeps swinging between feeling like a fraud and deciding the reviewers are out to get her. Your goal is to help her get an accurate picture of her current skills so she can actually improve; the stakes are her credibility and her next promotion.","dialogue":["Priya: My review says my deadline estimates are consistently off. Either I feel like a fraud, or I convince myself the reviewers are just out to get me.","Player: What do you actually think is true?","Priya: Honestly? I have no idea anymore. Every piece of feedback either makes me a genius or a failure.","Player: That is the fixed mindset talking. When every outcome is good or bad news about your precious traits, distortion creeps in until you don't know yourself.","Priya: But if I admit my estimates are off, doesn't that prove I'm bad at my job?","Player: Only if your skill is carved in stone. If you believe you can develop, then accurate information about your current abilities is exactly what you need to improve.","Priya: So the honest read is a starting point, not a verdict?","Player: Yes. Dweck found it is fixed-mindset people who account for almost all the inaccuracy in judging their performance. Growth-minded people are amazingly accurate."],"choices":["Help Priya state, out loud, three specific skill gaps she can work on, framed as next steps she can practice and measure.","Reassure her she is actually brilliant and the reviewers are wrong, so her confidence stays intact.","Agree that honest feedback is too risky and suggest she avoid reviews that could label her.","Tell her she should switch careers to something that never evaluates her work."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"Self-insight and accuracy: growth-minded people stay open to accurate information about their current abilities because they need it to learn, whereas fixed-minded people distort outcomes to protect their image of their traits.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 works because it converts the feedback into concrete, measurable development targets, which is what an accurate, growth-oriented view requires. Choice 1 feeds distortion and hides the real gap. Choice 2 avoids the information she needs to improve. Choice 4 treats the feedback as a verdict on her identity rather than data about her current skill."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Cultivating a love of challenge in struggling learners","visual_frame_description":"A quiet testing room in an elementary school. Researcher desk lights glow as a ten-year-old, Marcus, sweats over hard puzzles, then shoves the paper away and slumps in defeat. Across the table, another child grins, rubs her hands together, and announces she loves a challenge. The player studies both children before coaching Marcus through the next puzzle.","player_mission":"Diagnose what distinguishes the delighted child from the defeated one, then choose the coaching move that helps Marcus turn the hard puzzle from a failure into learning. Win by shifting his interpretation of struggle.","key_concepts":["Dweck watched children grapple with hard puzzles and discovered that some of them loved the difficulty.","One ten-year-old boy pulled up his chair, rubbed his hands together, smacked his lips, and cried out 'I love a challenge!'","Another boy, sweating on the puzzles, looked up with a pleased expression and said with authority, 'I was hoping this would be informative!'","These children knew that human qualities, such as intellectual skills, could be cultivated, and they were getting smarter by tackling hard problems.","The puzzle-lovers were not discouraged by failure because they did not think they were failing; they thought they were learning.","Dweck originally believed human qualities were carved in stone, and that failure meant you were not smart.","The growth mindset believes the hand you are dealt is just the starting point for development.","The passion for stretching yourself and sticking to it, even when it is not going well, is the hallmark of the growth mindset.","Turning a failure into a gift is the kind of transformation the growth mindset makes possible.","Exceptional people seem to have a special talent for converting life's setbacks into future successes."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the puzzle-study mechanism: children who believe intellectual skills can be cultivated treat hard problems as learning rather than failure, and even cheer 'I love a challenge!' The intervention goal is to help a struggling child adopt that belief so struggle stops feeling like proof of low ability.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the fixed-mindset belief that hard problems are proof of low ability. It turns a normal struggle into a verdict, and the only way to beat it is to replace that interpretation with the growth mindset's view that struggle is learning.","stage_1_observation":"In the testing room, Marcus completes the first easy puzzles quickly, then freezes on the hard ones. He pushes the paper away and whispers 'I'm just not smart at these.' Next to him, a girl with the same hard puzzles says 'I love a challenge!' and keeps experimenting with new strategies, visibly enjoying each near-miss. Both children face identical problems; only their interpretations differ.","stage_1_question":"What most distinguishes the girl who loves the challenge from Marcus, who gives up?","stage_1_correct_answer":"She believes intellectual skills can be cultivated, so she experiences the hard puzzles as learning and growing rather than as a verdict on fixed intelligence; Marcus believes failure means he is not smart, so he stops.","stage_2_intervention":"The next hard puzzle is placed in front of Marcus and he starts to retreat. You have one coaching move. What do you say and do to shift him into the mindset that turns failure into a gift?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Reframe the struggle as progress: tell Marcus 'This puzzle is supposed to be hard, and struggling on it is how your brain gets smarter — what did you just try, and what could you try next?' then prompt him to test one new strategy and acknowledge the attempt as learning, not as a test of whether he is smart."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Your intelligence and personality mindsets: self-assessment","visual_frame_description":"An interactive self-assessment screen from the book's 'Grow Your Mindset' section. Statements about intelligence scroll on the left, and statements about personality and character scroll on the right. A slider at the bottom notes that the two kinds of mindset can differ, and the player must file each statement under the correct column.","player_mission":"Sort each statement from the book's mindset quiz into the fixed-mindset column, the growth-mindset column, or the domain-dependent column. Determine the verdict for the question first, then classify the remaining intelligence and personality items.","key_concepts":["The fixed-mindset intelligence items are 'Your intelligence is something very basic about you that you can't change very much' and 'You can learn new things, but you can't really change how intelligent you are.'","The growth-mindset intelligence items are 'No matter how much intelligence you have, you can always change it quite a bit' and 'You can always substantially change how intelligent you are.'","The fixed-mindset personality items are 'You are a certain kind of person, and there is not much that can be done to really change that' and 'You can do things differently, but the important parts of who you are can't really be changed.'","The growth-mindset personality items are 'No matter what kind of person you are, you can always change substantially' and 'You can always change basic things about the kind of person you are.'","You can be a mixture of mindsets, but most people lean toward one or the other.","Your intelligence mindset comes into play when situations involve mental ability.","Your personality mindset comes into play in situations that involve your personal qualities, such as how dependable, cooperative, caring, or socially skilled you are.","The fixed mindset makes you concerned with how you will be judged; the growth mindset makes you concerned with improving.","You could substitute 'artistic talent,' 'sports ability,' or 'business skill' for 'intelligence' when assessing your mindset.","The message of the book's self-assessment is that you can change your mindset."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests self-assessment of the two mindsets across domains: the book's own quiz shows that statements about intelligence and statements about personality and character each split into fixed and growth variants, and that a person's intelligence mindset can differ from their personality mindset. The mechanism is that mindset beliefs are domain-specific, not a single global trait.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"A person says, 'You can learn new things, but you can't really change how intelligent you are.' Which column does this statement belong in?","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset statement","column_b_label":"Growth-mindset statement","column_c_label":"Domain-dependent claim (mental ability vs. personal qualities)","rows":[{"label":"You can learn new things, but you can't really change how intelligent you are.","col":"a"},{"label":"You can always substantially change how intelligent you are.","col":"b"},{"label":"A situation involving mental ability calls up your intelligence mindset.","col":"c"},{"label":"You are a certain kind of person, and there is not much that can be done to really change that.","col":"a"},{"label":"No matter what kind of person you are, you can always change substantially.","col":"b"},{"label":"Your intelligence mindset and your personality mindset can differ.","col":"c"},{"label":"You can do things differently, but the important parts of who you are can't really be changed.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["A growth-mindset statement, because it says learning is possible.","A domain-dependent claim, because it is about intelligence only.","A neutral fact about how learning works."],"explanation":"'You can learn new things, but you can't really change how intelligent you are' is one of the book's exact fixed-mindset intelligence items: it admits surface learning while insisting the core quantity is fixed. The growth items assert the quality itself can change, and the domain-dependent items describe when each kind of mindset is triggered rather than endorsing either belief."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Effort and help-seeking after a setback","visual_frame_description":"A late-night study lounge near an exam center. Derek, an engineer, has just failed a certification exam for the second time, four points short of passing. He stares at his score report while a stack of untouched review guides sits nearby, and a peer, Sam, slides into the seat across from him to talk it through.","player_mission":"Talk Derek out of the fixed-mindset conclusion that his failure proves he lacks ability. Choose the response that reconnects effort and help with growth, and that turns his next attempt into a strategic plan rather than blind repetition or withdrawal.","key_concepts":["People with the fixed mindset treat risk and effort as things that might reveal their inadequacies and show they are not up to the task.","For people with the fixed mindset, the sayings become 'Nothing ventured, nothing lost' and 'If at first you don't succeed, you probably don't have the ability.'","Growth-minded people hold 'Nothing ventured, nothing gained' and 'If at first you don't succeed, try, try again.'","It is startling to see how much people with the fixed mindset do not believe in putting in effort or getting help.","People's ideas about risk and effort grow out of their more basic mindset.","When people are put in a fixed mindset, they quickly fear challenge and devalue effort.","The growth mindset is based on the belief that basic qualities can be cultivated through your efforts, your strategies, and help from others.","The passion for stretching yourself and sticking to it, even when it is not going well, is the hallmark of the growth mindset.","Growth-minded people who feel overwhelmed respond by digging in and doing what it takes.","Books with titles like 'The Ten Secrets of the World's Most Successful People' crowd the shelves but offer only unconnected pointers like 'take more risks!'"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the mindset roots of effort and help-seeking: fixed-mindset people devalue effort and avoid help because these could expose a lack of ability, while growth-minded people treat effort, strategy, and coaching as the path to cultivating ability. The theory predicts that the same failure leads a fixed mindset to withdraw and a growth mindset to dig in strategically.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Derek, an engineer, has failed his certification exam twice, this time by four points. He has decided that studying hard and still failing proves he lacks the aptitude, and he refuses the free retraining course. Your goal is to help him see effort and help as growth tools, not as proof of deficiency, and to turn his third attempt into a real plan; the stakes are his certification and his career.","dialogue":["Derek: I'm done with the exam. If I studied that hard and still failed, I clearly don't have the aptitude.","Player: You got 71 percent — four points short. People pass on the third attempt all the time after changing their approach.","Derek: Asking for help would just confirm I'm not smart enough. Risk and effort just reveal your inadequacies.","Player: That is the fixed mindset talking. People with that mindset see effort and help as things that might expose them — it is 'nothing ventured, nothing lost.'","Derek: And what would a growth-minded person say?","Player: 'Nothing ventured, nothing gained.' If your qualities can be cultivated, then studying differently and getting coaching isn't evidence of missing talent — it is how talent grows.","Derek: So you want me to throw more effort at it and hope?","Player: Not blind effort. A new strategy: break the material into themes, get a study partner, and target exactly the sections where you dropped points."],"choices":["Offer to meet twice a week to break the material into themes and practice the exact sections where he lost points, tying effort to strategy.","Respect his decision to stop, since pushing him would only hurt his self-esteem.","Tell him to take the exam again exactly the same way and just keep trying until luck turns his way.","Agree that he probably lacks the aptitude and suggest he find a different line of work."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The mindset basis of effort and help: fixed-mindset people fear effort and help because these could reveal inadequacy, while growth-minded people treat effort, strategy, and coaching as the route to cultivating ability — 'nothing ventured, nothing gained.'","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 works because it converts effort into targeted strategy with help from others, the precise opposite of Derek's 'nothing ventured, nothing lost' logic. Choice 1 reinforces his withdrawal and lets the fixed mindset win. Choice 2 keeps blind effort without strategy, which invites another identical failure. Choice 4 accepts the fixed-mindset verdict and ends his path to growth."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Escaping the proving-self trap: the fixed-mindset ego","visual_frame_description":"An open-plan office late in the evening. Kevin, a manager up for promotion, stares at a dashboard showing his team's project had a small but public forecasting error. In the reflection of the window you can see him rehearse excuses, decide whether to blame a junior teammate, or quietly edit the numbers. His boss walks in holding the corrected report.","player_mission":"Identify the belief that is pushing Kevin to hide his mistake, then choose the response that breaks the proving-self trap and moves him toward growth. Beat the boss by recognizing that every situation feels like a verdict when the fixed mindset is active.","key_concepts":["Believing your qualities are carved in stone creates an urgency to prove yourself over and over.","If you have only a certain amount of intelligence, personality, and moral character, you had better prove you have a healthy dose of them.","In the fixed mindset, every situation is evaluated: will I succeed or fail, look smart or dumb, be accepted or rejected, feel like a winner or a loser.","Dweck's sixth-grade teacher Mrs. Wilson seated the class in IQ order and created one consuming goal: look smart, don't look dumb.","Who cared about or enjoyed learning when your whole being felt at stake on every test?","The growth mindset treats the hand you are dealt as just the starting point for development.","In the growth mindset, you hide no deficiencies and instead work to overcome them.","Why waste time proving over and over how great you are, when you could be getting better?","Fixed-mindset people do not believe in putting in effort or getting help.","The message of the book is that you can change your mindset."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the fixed-mindset ego's urgency to prove itself: Dweck argues that believing your qualities are carved in stone turns every situation into a test of worth, which is why admitting a mistake feels catastrophic. The intervention must show that a mistake is information about process, not a verdict on identity.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the proving-self trap: the fixed-mindset urgency to look smart and never look dumb, which makes Kevin treat a small public error as a threat to his worth and pushes him toward concealment, blame, or data-tampering.","stage_1_observation":"Kevin is one promotion away from the role he has chased for years. His team's forecast was off by nine percent, and the error is on the exec dashboard. He has already drafted three options in his notebook: bury the variance, blame the junior analyst who built the sheet, or quietly revise the assumptions to make the number look right. His hands shake, and he mutters 'If they see this, they'll know I'm not up to it.'","stage_1_question":"Which belief is driving Kevin's urge to hide or recast the forecasting error?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The fixed-mindset belief that his worth is measured by how he looks in every situation, so an error feels like proof he is deficient and must be concealed or blamed away.","stage_2_intervention":"Kevin's boss walks in holding the corrected report and asks him to walk through what went wrong. The room is tense, and Kevin reaches for his notebook full of excuses. Choose the response that breaks the proving trap and moves him onto the growth road.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Kevin should acknowledge the error plainly, walk through what the analysis missed, and propose a revised forecasting process with a peer review built in — treating the mistake as information about the method rather than a verdict on his worth, and using the team as a resource instead of a shield."}]},{"id":"ch07","title":"Chapter 2: Inside the Mindsets","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Meaning of Failure: Identity, Learning, or Excuse","visual_frame_description":"A busy courtroom has just emptied after a verdict. Maya Chen, a 34-year-old attorney, stands alone at the plaintiff's table, six years of case files stacked beside her; her team lost the suit against the bank that their clients said cheated them. On a split screen, three other faces appear in ghosted panels: a football player in a Vikings jersey, a three-star French chef in a kitchen, and a tennis player berating a camera crew. The player sees each figure tagged with a one-line reaction to losing.","player_mission":"Read the reaction of each person in the scene and classify which response pattern each one shows: failure treated as a permanent identity, failure treated as an event to learn from, or failure handled by repairing self-esteem through blame or downward comparison. Sort every evidence row into the correct column before advancing.","key_concepts":["In the fixed mindset, failure is transformed from an action (\"I failed\") into an identity (\"I am a failure\").","Bernard Loiseau, a three-star Michelin chef, was possessed by the idea that a downgrade in a guide had redefined him as a failure and a has-been.","The Martins cooled toward their three-year-old son Robert and stopped treating him with pride after he failed to get into the number one preschool in New York.","Jim Marshall, after running a fumble recovery the wrong way for a touchdown on national television, decided at halftime, \"If you make a mistake, you got to make it right.\"","College students in the fixed mindset, after doing poorly on a test, chose to look at the tests of people who had done really poorly as a way to feel better about themselves.","John McEnroe blamed anything but himself for losses: a fever, a backache, the tabloids, a friend being in love, and an NBC cameraman's headset noise during the 1984 French Open.","John Wooden said you are not a failure until you start to blame, because you can still be learning from your mistakes until you deny them.","Seventh graders with the growth mindset said they would study harder after a poor grade, while those with the fixed mindset said they would study less and would seriously consider cheating.","Jack Welch, after the Kidder, Peabody scandal, phoned fourteen of his top GE colleagues to apologize personally, saying \"I blamed myself for the disaster.\"","In the growth mindset, failure is a painful but nondestructive problem to be faced, dealt with, and learned from."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how mindsets change the meaning of failure: in the fixed mindset a setback becomes a verdict on fixed identity, while in the growth mindset it is an event to face and learn from. Dweck also shows that fixed-mindset people repair self-esteem after failure through blame, excuses, and downward comparison rather than through repairing the underlying skill.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Maya Chen, highlighted at the plaintiff's table, just lost the six-year case she brought for clients who felt cheated by a bank. Which column best classifies Maya's response to losing?","column_a_label":"Failure as identity (fixed mindset)","column_b_label":"Failure as an event to learn from (growth mindset)","column_c_label":"Self-esteem repair via blame or downward comparison (fixed mindset)","rows":[{"label":"Maya tells a colleague, \"Who am I to say that just because I spent seven years on something I am entitled to success? I do not regret it. I had to do it.\"","col":"b"},{"label":"Jim Marshall pulls himself together at halftime and plays some of his best football, contributing to his team's victory.","col":"b"},{"label":"John McEnroe explains his 1984 French Open loss to Ivan Lendl by blaming noise from an NBC cameraman's headset.","col":"c"},{"label":"College students, after failing a test, choose to review the test papers of students who did far worse than they did.","col":"c"},{"label":"Bernard Loiseau, the three-star chef, becomes possessed by the idea that losing two points in a guide has redefined him as a has-been.","col":"a"},{"label":"The Martins stop treating three-year-old Robert with pride and affection after he failed to get into the number one preschool.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Failure as identity: the loss permanently brands Maya as someone who could never win.","Self-esteem repair: Maya is blaming the bank or the judge to protect her ego.","Maya's reaction is best explained by temperament, since sensitive people can never let go of a loss."],"explanation":"Maya separates the outcome from her worth: she asks whether she fought for success or for the validity of the effort itself, refuses regret, and does not let the loss define her - the growth-mindset pattern. Bernard Loiseau and the Martins sort to column a because for them failure redefines the person permanently. McEnroe's headset excuse and the students reviewing worse test papers sort to column c because both repair self-esteem through blame or downward comparison rather than through learning."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Feeling Smart: Flawless Now vs. Learning Over Time","visual_frame_description":"Ms. Alvarez's fifth-grade classroom is finishing a set of challenging math puzzles. In one corner, six students are captured in thought bubbles as they answer the question, \"When do you feel smart?\" Amara, a girl with her hand raised mid-answer, is outlined in gold as the target of the task. Each student's spoken line floats above their head in a labeled bubble the player can tap to classify.","player_mission":"Classify each student's answer about when they feel smart into one of three categories: feeling smart from being flawless right now, feeling smart from learning over time, or feeling smart from being special and better than others. Sort all six answers into the correct columns.","key_concepts":["When Dweck asked people when they feel smart, those with the fixed mindset said \"It's when I don't make any mistakes.\"","Fixed-mindset people said they felt smart \"When I finish something fast and it's perfect.\"","Fixed-mindset people also said they felt smart \"When something is easy for me, but other people can't do it.\"","Growth-mindset people said they felt smart \"When it's really hard, and I try really hard, and I can do something I couldn't do before.\"","Growth-mindset people felt smart when they worked on something a long time and started to figure it out.","For the growth mindset it is not about immediate perfection but about confronting a challenge and making progress over time.","In the fixed mindset you have to be pretty much flawless, and you have to be flawless right away.","People with the fixed mindset described feeling smart as times they felt like a special person, different from and better than other people.","The difference between the mindsets shows up from grade schoolers to young adults when they are asked when they feel smart.","When the fixed-mindset person asks \"If you're somebody when you're successful, what are you when you're unsuccessful?\", the fragility behind the self-esteem is revealed."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests what the two mindsets define as feeling smart: the fixed mindset requires flawless performance right now and superiority over others, while the growth mindset defines intelligence as learning something over time through effort. Dweck found striking, consistent differences in how people from grade school to young adulthood answered the same question.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Amara, the highlighted student, says, \"When it's really hard, and I try really hard, and I can do something I couldn't do before.\" Which column best classifies Amara's answer?","column_a_label":"Feeling smart = being flawless right now (fixed mindset)","column_b_label":"Feeling smart = learning over time (growth mindset)","column_c_label":"Feeling smart = being better than other people (fixed mindset)","rows":[{"label":"\"When I don't make any mistakes.\"","col":"a"},{"label":"\"When it's really hard, and I try really hard, and I can do something I couldn't do before.\"","col":"b"},{"label":"\"When something is easy for me, but other people can't do it.\"","col":"c"},{"label":"\"When I finish something fast and it's perfect.\"","col":"a"},{"label":"\"When I work on something a long time and I start to figure it out.\"","col":"b"},{"label":"\"When I feel like a special person, someone different from and better than other people.\"","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Column a - feeling smart means Amara made no mistakes and finished perfectly and fast.","Column c - Amara feels smart because the puzzle made her feel more special than her classmates.","Amara's answer cannot be classified because it reflects a temporary mood rather than a mindset."],"explanation":"Amara describes stretching against difficulty and making new progress, which is exactly the growth-mindset definition of feeling smart. The answers about not making mistakes and finishing fast and perfect sort to column a, because they demand immediate flawlessness. The answers about doing something easy that others cannot do, or feeling like a special person, sort to column c, because they hinge on superiority over other people."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Effort: A Threat or an Igniter","visual_frame_description":"A violin studio at Juilliard, late afternoon. Nadia, a former child prodigy now in her late teens, sits in a chair with no violin case in sight while her teacher stands by an open music stand with a bow in hand. Around the room, ghosted vignettes illustrate other attitudes toward effort: a tortoise and a hare on a finish line, a prodigy sweating through a competition rehearsal, and a professor working late in a lit office building. The player's cursor hovers over labeled evidence cards.","player_mission":"Study how each person in the scene treats effort and sort each evidence card into the column that names the belief behind it: effort signals low ability, effort ignites ability into accomplishment, or effort robs you of your excuses. Classify all six cards correctly.","key_concepts":["People with the fixed mindset tell you, \"If you have to work at something, you must not be good at it.\"","In the fixed mindset, effort is for people who don't have the ability; things come easily to people who are true geniuses.","The tortoise-and-hare fable and stories like The Little Engine That Could gave effort a bad name, reinforcing the image that effort is for plodders who lack endowment.","People with the growth mindset believe that even geniuses have to work hard, and that no matter what your ability is, effort is what ignites that ability and turns it into accomplishment.","Nadja Salerno-Sonnenberg, a violin prodigy with a crisis of confidence, was afraid of trying because real effort would leave her without excuses.","\"Nothing is harder than saying, 'I gave it my all and it wasn't good enough'\" - the deepest fear within the fixed mindset.","Dweck realized that \"I could have been Yo-Yo Ma\" is a claim people can only make if they never really tried.","The fixed mindset makes effort terrifying partly because great geniuses are not supposed to need it, so needing it casts a shadow on your ability.","When Salerno-Sonnenberg finally trained all-out for a competition under Dorothy DeLay, she won it.","Effort can be feared in relationships too: Amanda kept choosing unreliable boyfriends because she could not risk really trying with a genuinely good partner and having it fail."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the mindset-dependent meaning of effort: the fixed mindset reads effort as a sign of low ability and as a threat that destroys the \"I could have been\" excuse, while the growth mindset treats effort as the force that ignites ability into accomplishment. Dweck grounds this in the fear that paralyzed violinist Nadja Salerno-Sonnenberg and in the culture's reverence for effortless genius.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Nadia, the highlighted prodigy, has stopped bringing her violin to lessons and refuses to fix her awkward fingerings and bowings. Which column best names the belief driving her refusal?","column_a_label":"Effort signals low ability (fixed mindset)","column_b_label":"Effort ignites ability into accomplishment (growth mindset)","column_c_label":"Effort robs you of your excuses (fixed mindset)","rows":[{"label":"Nadia refuses to correct her bowing and fingerings because changing them would admit her habits were wrong.","col":"a"},{"label":"Nadia admits, \"If you go to an audition and don't really try, if you're not really prepared, you have an excuse.\"","col":"c"},{"label":"The fable of the tortoise and the hare reinforces that effort is for plodders, not for the talented.","col":"a"},{"label":"Dweck's colleagues working late at the University of Illinois struck her as \"not as smart as I am\" instead of more hardworking.","col":"a"},{"label":"\"Even geniuses have to work hard for their achievements... effort is what ignites that ability and turns it into accomplishment.\"","col":"b"},{"label":"Once Salerno-Sonnenberg began training all-out for a competition, she won it - and said you have to work hardest for the things you love most.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Column a - Nadia is simply lazy and unwilling to practice, regardless of what it would mean.","Column b - Nadia is wisely conserving her energy for the music she truly loves.","Nadia's refusal is best explained by her temperament, not by any belief about effort."],"explanation":"Nadia's own words reveal the excuse-robbery mechanism: if she does not really try, she keeps the out of \"I could have been.\" Column a captures the belief that needing effort proves you lack talent, which is what makes the fingerings so hard to change; column c captures the even sharper fear that honest effort destroys all excuses. The tortoise-and-hare lesson and Dweck's late-night colleagues sort to column a, while the quotes about effort igniting ability and Salerno-Sonnenberg's winning competition sort to column b."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Challenge-Seeking in Learning and Sports","visual_frame_description":"An admissions advising office at a large university. A wall-mounted screen cycles through six cases: a four-year-old at a puzzle table, Hong Kong freshmen registering for classes, a seventh-grade girl raising her hand, a pre-med student hunched over a chemistry textbook, a teenage golfer on a PGA fairway, and a cabdriver listening to opera. A counselor points at the golfer's case, which is outlined in gold as the target the player must classify.","player_mission":"For each case on the screen, decide whether the person's choice reflects a fixed mindset, a growth mindset, or a factor other than mindset such as temperament or resources. Sort all six evidence cards into the correct columns.","key_concepts":["Four-year-olds with the fixed mindset chose to redo an easy jigsaw puzzle rather than try a harder one, because kids who are born smart \"don't do mistakes.\"","Growth-minded four-year-olds found the choice strange and chose one hard puzzle after another; one girl said \"I'm dying to figure them out!\"","At the University of Hong Kong, freshmen who were not fluent in English and had a fixed mindset were not very interested in a faculty-offered remedial English course.","The Hong Kong students with the growth mindset said an emphatic yes to the remedial English course, seizing the chance to learn.","The seventh-grade girl raised her hand even when unsure of the answer so her mistakes could be corrected and she could increase her intelligence.","In Dweck's brain-wave lab at Columbia, only people with the growth mindset paid close attention to information that could help them learn.","Fixed-mindset pre-med students showed a big drop in interest as chemistry got harder; growth-mindset students kept the same high level of interest, and challenge and interest went hand in hand.","Fifth graders with the growth mindset wanted to take the hard puzzles home, and one asked the researcher to write down the names so his mom could buy more.","Michelle Wie entered the Sony Open against the best male golfers to prepare for the future, saying the learning experience was what she was after.","After the Sony Open, Wie's confidence had not suffered one bit: \"I think I learned that I can play here.\""],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how the two mindsets drive challenge-seeking: the fixed mindset avoids challenges that might expose deficiency, while the growth mindset actively seizes challenges as opportunities to stretch. Dweck demonstrates this across jigsaw puzzles, remedial English courses, pre-med chemistry, and a teenage golfer entering a PGA tournament.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"The highlighted case is Michelle Wie, the teenage golfer who entered the Sony Open against the best male professionals. Which column best classifies her choice?","column_a_label":"Fixed mindset - avoid exposing deficiency","column_b_label":"Growth mindset - seize the chance to stretch and learn","column_c_label":"Neither - temperament or resources explain it","rows":[{"label":"A four-year-old with the fixed mindset chooses to redo the easy jigsaw puzzle instead of attempting a harder one.","col":"a"},{"label":"Hong Kong freshmen who lack English fluency decline a faculty course that would improve their English skills.","col":"a"},{"label":"The seventh-grade girl raises her hand even when unsure so her mistakes can be corrected and she can get smarter.","col":"b"},{"label":"Growth-minded pre-med students keep their high interest in chemistry even when the exams stay very hard.","col":"b"},{"label":"Michelle Wie enters the Sony Open against the world's best male golfers, seeking a learning experience rather than a protected win.","col":"b"},{"label":"A cabdriver spent years trying to cultivate a love of opera because his father loved it, and finally accepts that he never will.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Column a - Wie was avoiding a real challenge by choosing easier opponents in junior tournaments.","Column c - Wie's choice is fully explained by her fearless temperament, not by any mindset.","Column b only applies to academic settings, never to sports."],"explanation":"Wie chose the hardest possible field not to protect her record but to learn what playing at that level felt like, and her confidence survived the loss - a growth-mindset choice. The easy-puzzle choice and the Hong Kong students' refusal sort to column a because they protect against exposing deficiency. The cabdriver case sorts to column c because Dweck treats his acceptance of not loving opera as a legitimate limit of taste and temperament, not a mindset failure."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"CEO Disease and Growth Leadership","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled boardroom in a downtown office tower. A wall of screens shows six corporate leaders in portrait: a CEO repeating the same car design, a turnaround artist waving at a soaring stock chart, an executive apologizing into a telephone, an engineer under a lamp overhauling a tech giant, a paper-company chief standing at a mill, and a disgraced energy boss pointing at the world outside the window. The player must classify each leader's strategy.","player_mission":"Review each corporate case on the boardroom wall and classify the strategy as protecting the hero image, confronting shortcomings for long-term growth, or assigning blame outward after failure. Sort every case into the correct column.","key_concepts":["\"CEO disease\" describes leaders who create a world where they have no shortcomings by surrounding themselves with worshipers and exiling critics.","Lee Iacocca, after his early success at Chrysler, kept bringing out the same car models with only superficial changes and quickly lost touch with where his field was going.","Albert Dunlap, a self-professed fixed mindsetter at Sunbeam, chose the short-term strategy of looking like a hero to Wall Street; the stock soared and the company fell apart.","Lou Gerstner, an avowed growth mindsetter at IBM, accepted stagnant stock and Wall Street sneers while overhauling IBM's culture and policies, and years later IBM led its field again.","Darwin Smith, looking back on his extraordinary performance at Kimberly-Clark, declared, \"I never stopped trying to be qualified for the job.\"","Jack Welch chose GE executives on the basis of \"runway,\" their capacity for growth.","NASA rejected applicants with pure histories of success and instead selected people who had significant failures and bounced back from them.","Jeffrey Skilling insisted that Enron's failure was the world's fault and dismissed the Justice Department investigation into corporate deception as a witch hunt.","After the Kidder, Peabody scandal, Jack Welch phoned fourteen of his top colleagues to apologize personally, saying \"I blamed myself for the disaster.\"","In the fixed mindset people are always in danger of being measured by a failure, and it can define them in a permanent way."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how mindsets shape leadership: the fixed mindset pushes executives toward \"CEO disease\" - protecting the perfect image and choosing short-term heroics - while the growth mindset lets leaders confront shortcomings and invest in long-term development. Dweck contrasts Iacocca and Dunlap with Gerstner, Smith, and Welch, and contrasts Skilling's outward blame with Welch's personal responsibility.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"The highlighted case is Albert Dunlap at Sunbeam, who chose a strategy to make himself look like a hero to Wall Street. Which column best classifies his strategy?","column_a_label":"Protect the hero image - CEO disease (fixed mindset)","column_b_label":"Confront shortcomings for long-term growth (growth mindset)","column_c_label":"Assign blame outward after failure (fixed mindset)","rows":[{"label":"Lee Iacocca keeps releasing the same car models with superficial changes while surrounding himself with worshipers and exiling critics.","col":"a"},{"label":"Albert Dunlap chooses a short-term strategy at Sunbeam: the stock soars but the company falls apart.","col":"a"},{"label":"Lou Gerstner overhauls IBM's culture while stock prices stagnate and Wall Street sneers, then IBM leads its field again.","col":"b"},{"label":"Darwin Smith of Kimberly-Clark says, \"I never stopped trying to be qualified for the job.\"","col":"b"},{"label":"Jeffrey Skilling insists Enron's failure was the world's fault and calls the Justice Department investigation a witch hunt.","col":"c"},{"label":"Jack Welch apologizes personally to fourteen GE colleagues after the Kidder, Peabody scandal, saying \"I blamed myself for the disaster.\"","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Column b - Dunlap was investing in the long-term foundation of the company for its health and growth.","Column c - Dunlap blamed regulators and short sellers for the company's collapse.","Dunlap's strategy is best explained by his industry's temperament, not by his mindset."],"explanation":"Dunlap chose the short-term strategy of looking like a hero to Wall Street, the same image-protecting pattern as Iacocca's worshipers-and-critics world, so he sorts to column a. Gerstner, Smith, and Welch sort to column b because they confronted shortcomings and worked for long-term development even under criticism. Skilling sorts to column c because he blamed the world rather than taking responsibility, in direct contrast to Welch's personal apology."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Reframing Failure After a Lost Pitch","visual_frame_description":"A design studio called Lumina, mid-morning. Priya, a senior product designer, sits across from her account lead Maya at a long table covered in sticky notes and a rejected pitch deck. Maya is slumped back, arms crossed, scrolling a phone showing the rival agency's announcement. Sunlight cuts across the room; a whiteboard behind them reads \"Next client: April.\"","player_mission":"Guide Priya through the conversation with Maya, who lost a major pitch and is spiraling. Choose the responses that help Maya treat the loss as an event to learn from rather than a verdict on her identity, and that steer her away from blame and downward comparison.","key_concepts":["In the fixed mindset, failure shifts from an action (\"I failed\") to an identity (\"I am a failure\").","In the growth mindset, failure is a painful but nondestructive problem to be faced, dealt with, and learned from.","Seventh graders with the growth mindset said they would study harder for the next test after a poor grade.","Seventh graders with the fixed mindset said they would study less and seriously consider cheating, reasoning that without ability effort is wasted.","College students in the fixed mindset repaired their self-esteem after a bad test by looking at the tests of people who did far worse.","People with the fixed mindset may assign blame or make excuses instead of repairing the actual deficiency.","John Wooden said you are not a failure until you start to blame, because you can still learn from mistakes until you deny them.","A growth-mindset lawyer spent seven years fighting a bank on behalf of cheated clients, lost, and said he did not regret it because the effort itself was valid.","The growth mindset allows people to value what they are doing regardless of the outcome.","People with the fixed mindset may try to repair their self-esteem by going looking for people who are even worse off than they are."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the mindset-dependent response to failure: the fixed mindset turns a lost pitch into proof of a permanent flaw and reaches for blame or downward comparison, while the growth mindset treats it as a problem to study and learn from. Dweck shows fixed-mindset students repairing self-esteem through cheating, blame, and comparing with worse-off others instead of repairing the skill.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Maya Chen and Priya are senior product designers at Lumina, a mid-sized design studio. They just lost a six-week pitch for a major health-tech client to a rival agency. A week later, Maya is still devastated: she keeps rehashing the loss, muttering that the client's committee \"never understands good design,\" saying the loss \"proves I was never really senior material,\" and comparing herself favorably to a junior colleague named Tomas who is struggling. Priya, who wants to win the next pitch and keep Maya's morale intact, must help Maya shift out of the spiral. Stakes: Maya's confidence, the studio's next opportunity, and the health of the team.","dialogue":["Maya: I can't believe it. Twelve weeks of work and they picked the other team. I feel like I've been exposed as a fraud.","Priya: You've been sitting with this all week. What's the story you're telling yourself about what this means?","Maya: It means I'm not the designer I thought I was. Everyone in the studio knows it now.","Priya: Do you actually believe one lost pitch measures who you are as a designer?","Maya: Maybe not. But it's easier to say the client's committee is clueless. And honestly, at least I'm not floundering like Tomas.","Priya: Tomas is working through his own learning curve. Comparing yourself to his struggles isn't going to make you a better designer.","Maya: So what am I supposed to do?","Priya: Let's take the pitch apart. What did we learn about how they evaluate, and what would we do differently next time?","Maya: ...You're right. I'd rather lose the excuse than the lesson."],"choices":["You're one of the best designers I know. Don't let one setback shake that - you have nothing to prove to anyone.","Let's book a client debrief, pull the competitor's deck, and map what we'd change. This loss is data for the next pitch.","Honestly, the committee was biased from the start. It's not fair to blame yourself.","Maybe design isn't the right field for you if a loss hits this hard."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Failure as an event to be faced and learned from (growth mindset) versus failure as a verdict on fixed identity with self-esteem repaired through blame or downward comparison.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 works because it reframes the loss from identity to problem and turns it into a learning plan, exactly how the growth-mindset seventh graders responded by studying harder and how the growth-minded lawyer valued the validity of his effort. Choice 0 is talent reassurance that feeds the fixed mindset's ego without teaching anything. Choice 2 is outward blame, the McEnroe-style self-esteem repair that short-circuits learning. Choice 3 delivers the identity verdict - \"you are a failure\" - which is precisely the meaning the fixed mindset attaches to a setback."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The 'Yet' Moment for a New Hire","visual_frame_description":"A bright corner office at a boutique investment firm. Rosa, a managing director, sits across from Devon, a new financial analyst who joined two weeks ago. Devon's desk across the hall is stacked with deal memos and valuation templates; he looks pale and keeps glancing at a senior analyst's finished report pinned to the wall. Rosa has just put down her coffee to lean in and listen.","player_mission":"Play Rosa, the new analyst's manager, and steer the conversation so Devon sees that his current inability is a state of \"not yet\" rather than a permanent verdict. Choose the response that gives him a concrete path to learn while holding the job's real standards.","key_concepts":["Columbia graduate students with stellar records and test scores felt like complete imposters within a day, looking at the faculty and thinking \"Oh my God, I can't do that.\"","The new graduate students knew how to take tests and get A's, but they did not know how to do the research work - yet - and they forgot the yet.","School (and a job) is there to teach; people are there to learn how to do these things, not because they already know everything.","People with the fixed mindset expect ability to show up on its own, before any learning takes place.","\"Becoming is better than being,\" and the fixed mindset does not allow people the luxury of becoming; they have to already be.","Janet Cooke and Stephen Glass were talented young reporters who fabricated stories, possibly because they felt they should already be big-time reporters before doing the hard work of learning how.","Stephen Glass wrote, \"We were stars - precocious stars - and that was what mattered.\"","An assessment at one point in time has little value for understanding someone's ability, let alone their future potential.","In the growth mindset, you don't always need confidence: you can plunge into something wholeheartedly even when you think you're not good at it.","Dweck says people can change mindsets just by knowing about the two mindsets and catching themselves in the fixed mindset."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the \"yet\": the fixed mindset reads current inability as a permanent verdict on talent, while the growth mindset treats skills as developable through learning. Dweck describes Columbia graduate students who \"forgot the yet\" within one day, reading their unfamiliar research demands as proof of inadequacy.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Devon, 24, is a new financial analyst at a boutique investment firm. He aced every test in college, had near-perfect grades, and was aggressively recruited, but two weeks into the job he freezes: senior analysts draft deal memos, run valuation models, and write investment committee notes he cannot yet produce. He tells his manager, Rosa, that he must have fooled everyone in the interview. Rosa knows this is the classic \"yet\" moment and wants to keep a talented analyst from spiraling into imposter thinking. Stakes: Devon's development, the quality of the work he will soon own, and the team's trust in the hiring decision.","dialogue":["Devon: Honestly, Rosa, I think I got this job by mistake. Everyone here writes memos in their sleep and I can't even start one.","Rosa: You've been here two weeks. What exactly can't you do yet?","Devon: Everything that matters. I don't know how to value a deal, I don't know the models, and I'm not like the others.","Rosa: Were you like the others on day one? Nobody walks in knowing this. You know how to take tests and get A's - you just don't know this yet.","Devon: But my grades and my interview answers were all I had. If I can't do the work, it means I never really had the talent.","Rosa: Or it means you're in a room full of people who've practiced for years. The talent got you here; the practice carries you from here.","Devon: So you're not going to write me off?","Rosa: I'm going to pair you with Dana on the next deal memo and give you the model templates. Your job is to ask every question you have. That's how you learn."],"choices":["Don't worry about it, Devon. You were hired for a reason - you're clearly brilliant. Just be patient.","You're not ready yet - and that's why you're here. Here's the deal memo template; work through it with Dana and bring me your questions.","I need to be honest: if you can't keep up by Friday, we'll need to reconsider the fit.","Feeling like an imposter means you don't belong here. Maybe you should have taken that offer from the other firm."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"The \"yet\": the fixed mindset treats current inability as a permanent verdict on talent, while the growth mindset treats skills as developable and learning as the very purpose of the role.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 names the missing yet, reframes the gap as a learning stage, and gives Devon a concrete structure, matching Dweck's point that people are hired to learn how, not because they already know everything. Choice 0 is empty talent praise that reinforces the fixed mindset and leaves Devon with no skill-building plan. Choice 2 turns a one-point-in-time assessment into a threat, the fixed mindset's \"a test score is forever\" logic. Choice 3 confirms the imposter verdict and drives him out."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Responding to Praise Versus Correction","visual_frame_description":"A polished dance studio with mirrored walls and a barre. Ms. Alvarez, the director of a competitive youth ballet program, stands beside Elena, a 17-year-old dancer who has just finished a fouetté sequence. A few younger students watch from the floor, and Elena's expression hardens the moment Ms. Alvarez opens a notebook that contains her corrections. The scene is quiet except for the sound of a metronome app on a phone.","player_mission":"Play Ms. Alvarez in a conversation with Elena, a gifted student who thrives on praise but wilts under correction. Choose the response that teaches Elena to treat correction as information for growth rather than proof she lacks talent, and decide how to run the rehearsal accordingly.","key_concepts":["Marina Semyonova, a great Russian dancer and teacher, made students survive a trial period while she watched how they reacted to praise and to correction.","Semyonova's students had to be more responsive to correction to be deemed worthy - she separated those who get their thrill from what's easy from those who get their thrill from what's hard.","\"This is hard. This is fun\" was the moment Dweck knew she was changing mindsets.","In the fixed mindset it is not enough to succeed - you have to be pretty much flawless, and you have to be flawless right away.","Fixed-mindset people said they feel smart when they don't make any mistakes and when they finish something fast and perfect.","Growth-mindset people said they feel smart when they work on something a long time and start to figure it out.","Even the best puzzle-solvers among fifth graders showed a plunge in enjoyment when the puzzles got harder, so puzzle talent did not prevent the decline.","The self-esteem movement encourages people to confirm their superiority, as with the \"I LOVE ME\" mirror, instead of building their skills.","Effort is what ignites ability and turns it into accomplishment, no matter what your ability is.","In the fixed mindset, needing effort casts a shadow on your ability, because great geniuses are not supposed to need it."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the Semyonova principle: response to correction, not response to praise, reveals which mindset a learner has and which learners will grow. Dweck recounts how Marina Semyonova selected students by watching whether they got their thrill from what was easy or from what was hard, and how the fixed mindset requires flawless performance right now.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Ms. Alvarez runs a competitive youth ballet program with a demanding performance season ahead. Elena, 17, is her most naturally gifted dancer, but she only absorbs praise: any correction makes her sulk, withdraw, and threaten to quit. Elena's parents press Ms. Alvarez to praise her constantly to protect her confidence. Ms. Alvarez knows the program's culture is at stake and wants to model how a growth-minded studio treats correction. Stakes: Elena's development, the example she sets for younger students, and the quality of the season's company.","dialogue":["Elena: Did you see my fouettés today? Everyone said they were perfect.","Ms. Alvarez: I saw them. Now let's talk about the extension in your arabesque - it collapsed on the landing.","Elena: (turns away) Fine. I guess I'm just not cut out for this.","Ms. Alvarez: Why does a correction make you want to quit?","Elena: Because if I was actually talented, I wouldn't need to be fixed all the time.","Ms. Alvarez: Here's the truth about this studio. I don't pick dancers who are already perfect. I pick dancers who get energized by correction.","Elena: That's backwards. Everyone else praises me.","Ms. Alvarez: Praise tells you where you already are. Correction tells you where you could go. Which one is going to make you the dancer you want to be?","Elena: ...Nobody's ever said it like that. So what do I work on first?"],"choices":["You're the most naturally gifted dancer here, Elena. Don't let one correction shake your confidence.","From now on I'll only critique the students who ask for it. You just keep dancing and enjoy it.","Let's start with the arabesque. Here's the drill - you'll be off balance for a week, and that's exactly what growing feels like. I'm watching your process, not your perfection.","If corrections make you this upset, maybe competitive dance isn't for you."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Marina Semyonova's selection test: separate students who get their thrill from the easy from those who get their thrill from the hard, judging them by their response to correction rather than to praise.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 2 reframes correction as the path of growth, demands engagement with the hard part, and makes process visible, which is exactly how a growth-minded teacher selects for learners. Choice 0 is talent praise that keeps Elena's confidence contingent on never being wrong. Choice 1 removes the challenge and protects her ego at the cost of development. Choice 3 delivers the fixed-mindset identity verdict, confirming her fear that correction means she is not cut out for dance."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Choosing a Partner: Pedestal or Growth","visual_frame_description":"A café on a rainy Saturday afternoon. Lena, a 29-year-old event planner, wraps both hands around a mug across from her friend Sam. A wedding invitation lies on the table between them. Lena looks tired and keeps glancing at her phone, which shows a string of missed calls from Marcus. The screen displays Sam's and Lena's thoughts as the conversation unfolds.","player_mission":"Play Sam, Lena's trusted friend, as she describes a boyfriend who adores her but crumbles at any criticism. Choose the advice that helps Lena see what she actually wants from a partner - someone who fosters her development rather than a worshiper who needs her to be flawless.","key_concepts":["People with the fixed mindset said their ideal mate would put them on a pedestal, make them feel perfect, and worship them.","People with the growth mindset hoped for a partner who would see their faults and help them to work on them.","Growth-minded people wanted someone who would challenge them to become a better person and encourage them to learn new things.","Growth-minded people did not want people who would pick on them or undermine their self-esteem, but they did want people who would foster their development.","A growth-mindset woman told of her marriage to a fixed-mindset man who was devastated by any request and then ran to his mother for the constant adoration he needed.","The fixed-mindset husband's idea of success - total, uncritical acceptance - was not the wife's, and her idea - confronting problems - was not his.","One person's growth can be the other person's nightmare when the mindsets clash.","People with the growth mindset did not assume they were fully evolved, flawless beings who had nothing more to learn.","In the fixed mindset, if you're somebody when you're successful, what are you when you're unsuccessful?","The self-esteem movement invents devices like the \"I LOVE ME\" mirror to help people confirm their superiority instead of fostering development."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the ideal-mate question: the fixed mindset wants a partner who enshrines fixed qualities through pedestals and worship, while the growth mindset wants a partner who challenges and fosters development. Dweck's young-adult surveys showed these two sharply different pictures of the ideal mate, and a fixed-mindset partner's need for uncritical adoration can make one person's growth the other's nightmare.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Lena, 29, an event planner, has been dating Marcus for five months. Marcus showers her with compliments and cancels his own plans for her, but he collapses at the smallest criticism. When Lena asked him to consult her before booking weekend plans, he was devastated, then spent an hour on the phone with his mother so she could reassure him he was wonderful. Lena is wondering whether she is being too picky and asks her friend Sam for a read on the relationship. Stakes: Lena's judgment about what a good relationship actually is.","dialogue":["Lena: I feel like I have to walk on eggshells. I asked Marcus to run weekend plans by me, and he acted like I'd insulted him to his core.","Sam: What happened after that?","Lena: I spent an hour rebuilding his mood, and then he called his mom so she could tell him he's wonderful. We never even talked about my actual request.","Sam: What do you want a partner for - to feel perfect, or to grow?","Lena: I want someone who sees my faults and helps me work on them. Who challenges me to be better. Not a cheerleader for everything I do.","Sam: Then Marcus is giving you the opposite of what you want. He needs total, uncritical acceptance.","Lena: Is that really so bad? He adores me.","Sam: Adoration that breaks the second you disagree isn't adoration. It's a pedestal, and it doesn't foster development.","Lena: So I should end it?","Sam: I can't answer that. But ask yourself: does this relationship make you grow, or does it exist to make you feel flawless?"],"choices":["Marcus sounds great. Someone who worships you is what everyone really wants - don't overthink it.","Ask yourself what you're actually getting here. You want a partner who fosters your development; he wants a relationship where nobody raises an issue. Those two things don't coexist.","You're the problem, Lena. You're too sensitive - most people would love being adored.","Dump him immediately. Anyone who calls his mother that often is a red flag."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"The ideal-mate question: fixed-mindset people seek a partner who enshrines their fixed qualities, while growth-mindset people seek a partner who fosters their development and challenges them to become better.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 holds Lena's own stated values (development, challenge, working on faults) up against what Marcus needs (uncritical adoration), letting her see the mismatch herself - the growth-minded picture of a partner. Choice 0 validates the pedestal and the fixed mindset's idea of love. Choice 2 turns the critique onto Lena's identity, the fixed-mindset verdict. Choice 3 is an outcome verdict that skips the deeper question about what fosters growth."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Mindset and the February Slump","visual_frame_description":"A shared grad-student apartment in late February; gray light through the window, a pile of unopened mail, and a lecture hall timetable stuck to the fridge. Priya sits on the edge of Jonah's bed while he stays under a blanket, laptop open to a blank document. A three-week calendar on the wall is dotted with X marks over missed deadlines, and a sticky note reads \"Lab hours: Mon-Sat.\"","player_mission":"Play Priya, Jonah's roommate, as he sinks into a depressive slump. Choose the responses that help him keep his life intact by staying in motion, even while feeling wretched, instead of letting the setbacks define him or letting everything slide.","key_concepts":["In Dweck's three-week online diary study during February and March, students with the fixed mindset had higher levels of depression.","Fixed-mindset depression came from rumination: tormenting themselves with the idea that setbacks meant they were incompetent or unworthy.","The more depressed fixed-mindset students felt, the more they let things go and the less they took action to solve their problems.","Plenty of students with the growth mindset felt miserable in peak depression season, but the more depressed they felt, the more they took action to confront their problems.","Growth-minded students, when depressed, made sure to keep up with their schoolwork and their lives.","A depressed freshman kept going to class and doing the reading; his teaching assistant told him, \"then you're not depressed.\"","He was depressed, but he was coping the way people in the growth mindset tend to cope - with determination.","Temperament certainly plays a role in how people react, but mindset is an important part of the story.","When Dweck's team taught people the growth mindset, it changed the way they reacted to their depressed mood.","When people believe in fixed traits, they are always in danger of being measured by a failure that can define them permanently."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests mindset and depression: fixed-mindset students who felt depressed ruminated on setbacks as evidence of incompetence and let everything slide, while growth-minded students, the more depressed they felt, took more action and kept up their lives. Dweck found that teaching people the growth mindset changed how they reacted to a depressed mood, independent of temperament.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"It is late February, the peak of the campus depression season. Priya's roommate Jonah, a first-year graduate student, has been sliding for three weeks: he sleeps till noon, has stopped going to lab, missed two assignment deadlines, and keeps muttering that the setbacks prove he is not cut out for grad school. Priya worries that letting everything slide is the fixed-mindset pattern she learned about in her psych seminar. Stakes: Jonah's semester and mental health, and the routines that would keep his life intact until he feels better.","dialogue":["Jonah: I'm a dope. I failed that midterm, and now the paper's late and I can barely get out of bed. This just keeps circling: I'm not cut out for grad school.","Priya: You've been beating yourself up for two weeks. What's the story you're holding onto?","Jonah: That the last two weeks prove I'm worthless. What's the point of trying?","Priya: In my psych seminar we talked about this - people who see setbacks as evidence they're broken tend to let everything slide. People who see them as problems keep their routine.","Jonah: So I should pretend I'm fine?","Priya: No. You're not fine. But you can be wretched and still shower, go to lab, and turn things in - so that when you feel better, your life is still intact.","Jonah: That sounds like a lot of effort for nothing.","Priya: The worse you feel, the more determined you get - that's the research finding. Drag yourself in tomorrow, and I'll sit with you while you write the first draft.","Jonah: ...Okay. Meet me at the lab at nine."],"choices":["You're clearly depressed. Take a break from everything - your assignments can wait until you feel better.","I get it, you're a dope, I felt that way last semester too. You'll be fine in a few weeks.","You're not a failure - you're just sensitive by temperament, and some people can't handle this kind of pressure.","Keep the routine anyway. Shower, show up, hand things in, even if you feel awful - when you feel better your life will still be intact. I'll go with you."],"optimal_choice":3,"theoretical_principle":"Mindset and depression: fixed-mindset students who felt depressed ruminated on setbacks as proof of incompetence and let everything slide, while growth-minded students, the more depressed they felt, took more action and kept up their lives.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 3 matches the growth-mindset coping style Dweck documented: feeling wretched but still showing up, so that life remains intact when the mood lifts. Choice 0 is the fixed-mindset slide - letting things go because the setback feels like a verdict. Choice 1 labels Jonah with an identity verdict and offers empty reassurance. Choice 2 writes the whole thing off to temperament, which Dweck says plays a role but is not the whole story, since teaching the growth mindset changed people's reaction to a depressed mood."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Perfect-on-Paper Reporter","visual_frame_description":"The city desk of a metro newspaper, late evening. A young reporter named Eric, 26, types furiously at a terminal while a stack of front-page stories sits beside him. His editor leans over a printout with a red marker circling one detail. On the wall, framed front pages show the paper's best work, and in the corner a veteran reporter watches Eric with concern. The scene glows with the tension of a deadline.","player_mission":"Study Eric's behavior and diagnose the mechanism driving it, then choose the intervention that holds him accountable while refusing to treat one mistake as a permanent verdict on his identity. Defeat the 'prove-it-right-now' urgency before it destroys a talented career.","key_concepts":["Janet Cooke won a Pulitzer Prize for Washington Post articles about an eight-year-old drug addict, but the boy did not exist and she was stripped of the prize.","Stephen Glass was the whiz kid of The New Republic whose stories and sources turned out not to exist.","Dweck understands them as talented, desperate young people who succumbed to the pressures of the fixed mindset.","Stephen Glass wrote, \"We were stars - precocious stars - and that was what mattered.\"","\"Becoming is better than being,\" and the fixed mindset does not allow people the luxury of becoming; they have to already be.","People with the fixed mindset expect ability to show up on its own, before any learning takes place.","In the fixed mindset one test or one evaluation can measure you forever, which is why success must be perfect and immediate.","School and the workplace are there to teach; people are there to learn how to do these things, not because they already know everything.","In the fixed mindset failure shifts from an action to an identity: \"I am a failure.\"","People with the fixed mindset may repair self-esteem after a failure through blame and excuses rather than by repairing the skill."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the 'prove-it-right-now' urgency: in the fixed mindset, one evaluation can measure you forever, so success must be flawless and immediate, and admitting ignorance feels like disqualification. Dweck reads the fabrications of Janet Cooke and Stephen Glass through this lens - talented young people who felt they had to already be big-time reporters before doing the hard work of learning how.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central mechanism is the fixed-mindset urgency to be flawless right away: if one evaluation can measure you forever, then every correction or moment of not-knowing feels like proof of permanent inadequacy, pushing a person to fake success rather than learn. This battle tests whether you can spot that urgency behind a rising reporter's fabrications and replace it with a learning orientation before it costs him his career.","stage_1_observation":"You are the city editor of a metro newspaper. A new reporter, Eric, 26, has delivered three splashy front-page stories in six weeks - unusually fast for a rookie. You notice he panics whenever anyone asks for a source's phone number, changes details under questioning, and has started calling older reporters to check how real journalists handle pushback. Yesterday you asked him to rewrite a small corrections note and he looked physically ill. You study a scene of high talent combined with terror: a young man who behaves as if admitting ignorance would discredit him, who feels he must already be a big-time reporter before doing the hard work of learning how.","stage_1_question":"Which mechanism best explains Eric's pattern of behavior?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The fixed-mindset urgency to be flawless right away: Eric reads any correction or request for a source as evidence that he is not truly talented, so instead of learning he performs success - the same pressure Dweck sees behind Janet Cooke's fabricated Pulitzer-winning story and Stephen Glass's invented sources at The New Republic, where feeling he had to already be a star outweighed doing the hard work of learning how.","stage_2_intervention":"You catch one embellished detail before it reaches print: a quote Eric attributed to a source who never said it. The publisher wants to fire him immediately, and the union representative wants you to issue a public reprimand. Before anyone acts, you pull Eric aside. He finally admits, in a low voice, \"I can't be seen learning on the job.\" You have one move that sets the culture of the newsroom. How do you respond?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Hold Eric accountable for the fabrication as a behavior to correct while refusing to frame him as a permanent failure: walk him through exactly what he did wrong, reassign him for a month to a veteran reporter's beat where learning on the job is the explicit and normal expectation, and praise his corrections and process rather than his splash. This mirrors the growth mindset's treatment of a mistake as something to be faced, dealt with, and learned from, and it treats \"not yet\" as a stage rather than a verdict. Firing him or publicly shaming him would confirm the fixed-mindset belief that one failure defines him forever - the exact belief that drove the fabrication."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Fear of Effort in a Star Performer","visual_frame_description":"An engineering team's open office at sprint planning. Theo, 31, a celebrated coder who wrote his first app at twelve, leans back with his chair tipped, arms behind his head, telling the team he \"could have built the new payment system in a weekend if he'd wanted to.\" Around him, teammates exchange glances, and a whiteboard lists the sprint's stories with Theo's name next to a row of question marks. The manager watches from the doorway.","player_mission":"Diagnose the fear driving Theo's pattern of never committing fully, then choose an intervention that makes real effort the explicit and safe expectation. Defeat the 'I could have been' shield before it lets a star performer coast into mediocrity.","key_concepts":["Nadja Salerno-Sonnenberg, a violin prodigy, was paralyzed by fear of trying and had stopped bringing her violin to her lessons.","She said everything she was going through boiled down to fear: \"Fear of trying and failing.\"","Without effort you can always say \"I could have been...\"; once you really try, you can't say that anymore.","\"Nothing is harder than saying, 'I gave it my all and it wasn't good enough'\" - the deepest fear within the fixed mindset.","Dorothy DeLay confronted the prodigy: \"If you don't bring your violin next week, I'm throwing you out of my class.\"","When Salerno-Sonnenberg finally trained all-out for a competition, she won it.","\"You have to work hardest for the things you love most\" is something Salerno-Sonnenberg says she knows for a fact.","In the fixed mindset, great geniuses are not supposed to need effort, so just needing it casts a shadow on your ability.","Fear of effort can happen in relationships too: Amanda kept choosing unreliable boyfriends because she could not risk genuinely trying with a good partner and having it fail.","High effort is a big risk when your claim to fame is having no deficiencies, because effort can reduce you."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the fear of effort in the fixed mindset: needing to try casts a shadow on talent, and actually trying robs a person of the \"I could have been\" excuse, so the highest-stakes risk is an honest failure. Dweck traces this fear through violin prodigy Nadja Salerno-Sonnenberg and shows how a confrontation that demanded real effort - Dorothy DeLay's ultimatum - freed her to win.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central mechanism is the fixed mindset's fear of effort: because geniuses are not supposed to need effort, trying casts a shadow on your ability, and because a real attempt leaves no excuses, an honest failure is the scariest possible outcome. This battle tests whether you can see that fear behind a talented employee's self-sabotage and intervene so he actually puts in effort instead of preserving his excuses.","stage_1_observation":"You manage a team of software engineers. Theo, 31, is a coding prodigy who has been called a genius his whole career. But he dodges every high-stakes project: he forgets the sprint planning meeting, turns in partial work, and always has an excuse ready - \"the ticket was ambiguous,\" \"my machine kept crashing,\" \"the deadline was unrealistic.\" He has never actually failed, because he has never actually tried. In this morning's stand-up he bragged that he could have built the new payment system in a weekend if he'd wanted to. You watch a very talented man who is terrified of an honest failure.","stage_1_question":"Which mechanism best explains Theo's pattern?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Fear of effort and honest failure: Theo protects his \"natural genius\" identity by never committing fully, so any setback can be blamed on circumstance rather than ability - the fixed-mindset logic that trying and still failing is the worst possible outcome because it leaves no excuses, the same fear that stopped Nadja Salerno-Sonnenberg from bringing her violin to class.","stage_2_intervention":"You assign Theo the new payment system project - the one he bragged he could do in a weekend. It is a six-week build. On day three he turns in a skeleton that does not compile and says he \"had other priorities.\" His mentor suggests you reassign him to easy tickets to keep his confidence intact. You pull Theo into your office. What do you do?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Refuse to reassign him, and make real effort the explicit, safe expectation: tell Theo the project is his, that you expect him to work hard on it, that struggling is a normal part of the job, and that you will measure him on what he learns and how he improves rather than on flawless genius - then meet weekly to review his process. This mirrors Dorothy DeLay's confrontation of Salerno-Sonnenberg, which demanded genuine effort and led to her winning the competition, and it treats \"working hardest for the things you love\" as a virtue. Reassigning him to easy tickets would confirm the fixed-mindset bargain that effort signals low ability and that his excuses must be preserved."}]},{"id":"ch08","title":"Chapter 3: The Truth About Ability and Accomplishment","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Mindset and the Junior High Transition","visual_frame_description":"A middle-school hallway outside a counselor's office. A student named Priya slumps on a bench clutching a report card with a low grade, while three thought bubbles float above her head showing different ways she could explain the result. A timeline strip across the wall charts two years of report-card grades rising and falling under different mindsets.","player_mission":"Sort each of the student's possible explanations into the column that best captures it, distinguishing a fixed-mindset verdict from a growth-mindset response and from the low-effort syndrome. Then answer which mindset's students raised their grades across the two-year junior high transition in Dweck's study.","key_concepts":["The transition to junior high brings harder work, tougher grading, and less personalized teaching at a time of adolescent change.","In Dweck's two-year junior high study, only students with the fixed mindset showed a decline in grades; growth-mindset students showed an increase.","Before junior high, the two groups had earned the same grades and achievement test scores; the gap appeared only under challenge.","Fixed-mindset students explained poor grades by attacking their own ability (\"I am the stupidest\") or by blaming the teacher.","Growth-mindset students felt overwhelmed too, but their response was to mobilize their resources and dig in.","For fixed-mindset adolescents, school is one big test in which a loser is forever.","Fixed-mindset students reported that their main goal in school, besides looking smart, was to exert as little effort as possible.","The low-effort syndrome lets students with a fixed mindset protect themselves from being measured.","Growth-mindset students treat adolescence as an opportunity to learn new subjects and find out what they want to become.","George Danzig solved two famously unsolved math problems because he treated them as demanding homework he had to crack.","In the fixed mindset, effort is seen as a threat that could unmask flaws and turn a winner into a loser."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how students interpret a setback under each mindset: fixed-mindset students treat a poor grade as a verdict on their fixed ability, while growth-mindset students treat it as a signal to dig in. Dweck's junior high transition study showed that only fixed-mindset students' grades declined while growth-mindset students' grades rose over two years.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"A student just brought home poor grades from the first term of junior high. Sort each of the student's statements into the column that best captures it, then answer: in Dweck's two-year transition study, which mindset's students raised their grades?","column_a_label":"Fixed mindset: the grade is a verdict on ability","column_b_label":"Growth mindset: the grade is a signal to learn","column_c_label":"Low-effort syndrome: refusing to be measured","rows":[{"label":"\"I am the stupidest\"","col":"a"},{"label":"\"The math teacher is a fat male slut\" - blaming the teacher instead of the learning","col":"a"},{"label":"\"Sometimes I feel overwhelmed, but my response is to dig in and do what it takes\"","col":"b"},{"label":"\"In school my main goal is to do things as easily as possible so I don't have to work very hard\"","col":"c"},{"label":"\"I suck in math\"","col":"a"},{"label":"\"Adolescence is a time to find out what I want to become\"","col":"b"},{"label":"Answering the adults' challenge of \"Now we will measure you\" with \"No you won't\"","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The growth-mindset students showed the decline because they stopped caring about grades.","Both groups had equal grades before and after junior high, so mindset made no difference.","Students who blamed their teachers usually studied harder and recovered."],"explanation":"Dweck's study found that only fixed-mindset students showed a grade decline across the junior high transition, while growth-mindset students, whose entry records were indistinguishable, increased their grades. Fixed-mindset explanations blamed fixed ability or other people, growth-mindset students mobilized resources for learning, and the low-effort syndrome was a way of protecting a fragile self-image from being measured."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Ability Praise versus Effort Praise","visual_frame_description":"A bright kitchen table where a mother holds a graded math worksheet with an eight-out-of-ten score while her twelve-year-old daughter looks up expectantly. Three labeled speech bubbles float between them, each offering a different way to praise the result, and a small thermometer on the side shows each message's effect on the child's willingness to take on a harder task.","player_mission":"Sort each piece of feedback into the column for the effect Dweck's research found it produces on the child's mindset. Then answer which kind of praise kept 90 percent of students eager to choose a challenging new task.","key_concepts":["More than 80 percent of parents reported believing it was necessary to praise children's ability to foster confidence and achievement.","Dweck's praise studies used ten fairly difficult problems from a nonverbal IQ test and then praised students for either ability or effort.","Ability-praised students were told \"You must be smart at this\"; effort-praised students were told \"You must have worked really hard.\"","Right after the praise, ability-praised students rejected a challenging new task they could learn from.","Ninety percent of effort-praised students wanted the challenging new task.","After hard problems, ability-praised students concluded they were not smart after all; effort-praised students thought difficulty meant applying more effort or new strategies.","After difficulty, the performance of ability-praised students plummeted even on easier problems, while effort-praised students got better and better.","Almost 40 percent of ability-praised students lied about their scores, always in the direction of hiding imperfection.","In the fixed mindset, imperfections are shameful, especially for the talented, so students lie them away.","Praising children's ability, in the end, made them feel dumber and act dumber but claim they were smarter.","Positive labels such as \"gifted\" and \"talented\" can rob people of their zest for challenge and their recipes for success."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the effect of praise content: praising ability (\"you're so smart\") pushes recipients into the fixed mindset, while praising effort and process (\"you worked really hard\") cultivates the growth mindset. Dweck's studies showed that ability praise led students to avoid challenge and to lie about poor scores, whereas effort praise kept 90 percent eager for a challenging learning task.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"A parent wants to praise a child who scored well on a hard test. Sort each piece of feedback by the effect Dweck's research found it produces, then answer: which kind of praise kept 90 percent of students wanting a challenging new task?","column_a_label":"Ability praise (pushes the fixed mindset)","column_b_label":"Effort praise (supports the growth mindset)","column_c_label":"Vague praise with no process information","rows":[{"label":"\"You must be smart at this\"","col":"a"},{"label":"\"You must have worked really hard\"","col":"b"},{"label":"\"You're a natural\"","col":"a"},{"label":"\"I can see you kept trying different strategies until it clicked\"","col":"b"},{"label":"\"Good job\" with no explanation","col":"c"},{"label":"\"You're so talented\"","col":"a"},{"label":"\"You really stuck with it and figured out the pattern\"","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Ability praise boosts confidence so students take on harder tasks.","Effort praise makes students feel they lack talent.","Vague praise is the most effective because it avoids pressure."],"explanation":"In Dweck's studies, ability praise (\"you must be smart at this\") pushed students into the fixed mindset, so they rejected challenging tasks and showed plummeting performance after difficulty, while effort praise (\"you must have worked really hard\") led 90 percent to choose a challenging task they could learn from and to keep improving. Vague praise carries no process information and gives the child nothing to transfer."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Stereotype Threat and Critical Feedback","visual_frame_description":"A college dorm room where a student named Marcus holds an essay covered in red ink from a demanding professor. The screen behind him shows three reaction panels: one of angry dismissal, one of calm note-taking, and one of a worried mind filled with whispering thoughts about what his grade says about his group.","player_mission":"Sort each reaction by the mechanism it reflects, distinguishing the fixed-mindset rejection of feedback from the growth-mindset use of it and from stereotype threat itself. Then answer which mindset allowed students to recruit a critical professor's feedback for their own learning goals.","key_concepts":["Steele and Aronson's research showed that even checking a box for race or sex can trigger a stereotype and lower test performance.","When no stereotype is evoked, Black and white students perform equally, and female and male students perform equally.","Putting more men in the room with a woman before a math test can lower her score.","Evoked stereotypes fill the mind with distracting thoughts and secret worries about confirming the stereotype.","Stereotype threat mainly harms people in a fixed mindset, because negative stereotypes resonate with the belief in permanent inferiority.","In the fixed mindset, a positive label makes you afraid of losing it and a negative label makes you afraid of deserving it.","The growth mindset takes the teeth out of the stereotype and lets people fight back rather than feel permanently inferior.","Fixed-mindset students rejected Edward Caldwell III's critical-but-helpful feedback as biased or unfair.","Growth-mindset students saw Caldwell's evaluation as \"seemingly honest and specific\" and a stimulus to produce better work.","The growth mindset allowed students to recruit even a representative of the white establishment for their own educational goals."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests stereotype threat and the attribution of feedback: fixed-mindset students interpret criticism from an out-group authority as an attack, while growth-mindset students treat it as usable information. Dweck's Caldwell study showed that only the growth mindset lets people take what they need from a threatening environment and keep their learning in their own hands.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"African American students submitted essays to be evaluated by Edward Caldwell III, a distinguished professor with an Ivy League pedigree. Sort each reaction by the mechanism it reflects, then answer: which mindset allowed students to recruit Caldwell's criticism for their own goals?","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset reaction: threat, insult, rejection","column_b_label":"Growth-mindset reaction: useful stimulus to improve","column_c_label":"Stereotype threat mechanism: distracting worries","rows":[{"label":"\"He's mean, he doesn't grade right, or he's obviously biased. He doesn't like me\"","col":"a"},{"label":"\"The evaluation was seemingly honest and specific... a stimulus to produce better work\"","col":"b"},{"label":"\"He is a pompous asshole\" - with the feedback rejected","col":"a"},{"label":"\"His comments were helpful and clear. I feel I will learn much from him\"","col":"b"},{"label":"Secret worries about confirming the stereotype drain mental power during a test","col":"c"},{"label":"Merely checking a box for race or sex can lower a test score by evoking the stereotype","col":"c"},{"label":"\"He doesn't understand the conciseness of my points... He dislikes creativity\"","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Stereotype threat lowers scores equally for everyone regardless of mindset.","Growth-mindset students ignored the feedback because the professor was arrogant.","Fixed-mindset students used the criticism to improve their next essays."],"explanation":"Students with a fixed mindset read Caldwell's critical-but-helpful feedback as a threat, an insult, or bias and rejected it, so they learned nothing. Students with a growth mindset, even while finding him arrogant, described the evaluation as \"honest and specific\" and recruited it as a stimulus to produce better work, while stereotype threat describes the distracting worry mechanism that stereotypes trigger."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Teachers' Mindsets and Ability Tracking","visual_frame_description":"Two sixth-grade classrooms side by side. In the left room, the high-ability reading group and the low-ability reading group stay in their assigned rows all year. In the right room, the same two groups have converged at the same top shelf of the class library. A German research clipboard in the foreground notes the year-end outcomes under each teacher.","player_mission":"Sort each teacher's belief and classroom result into the column that best captures it, distinguishing fixed-mindset teaching from growth-mindset teaching and from the talent-as-gift belief. Then answer under which approach the low-ability group ended the year \"way up high.\"","key_concepts":["Falko Rheinberg studied German schoolteachers with different mindsets about their students' abilities.","Fixed-mindset teachers believed students' achievement mostly remains constant during a year.","Fixed-mindset teachers believed intelligence could predict a student's entire school career.","Fixed-mindset teachers reported having no influence on students' intellectual ability.","In fixed-mindset classrooms, students who started in the high-ability group ended there, and low-ability students stayed low.","Growth-mindset teachers focused on the idea that all children could develop their skills.","In growth-mindset classrooms, both the high- and the low-ability groups ended the year way up high.","Test scores and measures of achievement tell you where a student is, not where a student could end up.","Marva Collins told Freddie on his first day: \"I promise, you are going to do, and you are going to produce. I am not going to let you fail.\"","Alfred Binet believed you could change the quality of someone's mind, and Dweck argues the evidence shows you can.","In the growth mindset, teachers act as allies in learning rather than judges of fixed worth."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests teachers' implicit theories of ability: fixed-mindset teachers see student ability as permanent and act as judges, while growth-mindset teachers see it as developable and teach for improvement. Rheinberg's study showed that group differences in achievement disappeared entirely in the classrooms of growth-mindset teachers.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"A school sorts students into high- and low-ability groups at the start of the year. Sort each teacher's belief and classroom result by mindset, then answer: under which approach did the low-ability group end the year \"way up high\"?","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset teaching: ability is permanent","column_b_label":"Growth-mindset teaching: all children can develop","column_c_label":"The gift myth: talent is born, not made","rows":[{"label":"\"According to my experience students' achievement mostly remains constant in the course of a year\"","col":"a"},{"label":"\"If I know students' intelligence I can predict their school career quite well\"","col":"a"},{"label":"\"As a teacher I have no influence on students' intellectual ability\"","col":"a"},{"label":"Teachers who focused on the idea that all children could develop their skills","col":"b"},{"label":"In this classroom, high- and low-ability groups both ended the year way up high","col":"b"},{"label":"The belief that the \"gift\" of prodigies is the ability itself","col":"c"},{"label":"The belief that what feeds the gift is constant, endless curiosity and challenge seeking","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Fixed-mindset teachers closed the gap between groups by the end of the year.","Test scores show where a student will end up as well as where they are.","Growth-mindset classrooms kept the initial group differences intact."],"explanation":"Falko Rheinberg's German study found that fixed-mindset teachers produced classrooms where the high-ability group stayed high and the low-ability group stayed low, because they believed ability was fixed and beyond their influence. Growth-mindset teachers, focused on development, made the group differences disappear, with both groups finishing the year high."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Myth of the Lone Genius versus the Record of Achievement","visual_frame_description":"A dim museum gallery with two adjacent exhibits. The first shows a solitary cartoon inventor alone in a shed with a single lightbulb flashing on. The second is a timeline collage of Edison's bustling corporate laboratory, Darwin's notebooks full of drafts, and Mozart's practice scores, each labeled with years and teams.","player_mission":"Sort each claim into the column where it belongs, separating the lone-genius myth from the documented record of achievement and from the curiosity-driven drive that feeds real ability. Then answer which category best describes what the historical record actually shows about achievement.","key_concepts":["People picture Edison as a reclusive tinkerer, but he had thirty assistants, including well-trained scientists, working around the clock in a corporate-funded laboratory.","The lightbulb was not one invention but a network of time-consuming inventions requiring chemists, mathematicians, physicists, engineers, and glassblowers.","Edison was a savvy entrepreneur who knew how to publicize himself, and his biographer Paul Israel sees him as a regular boy set apart by mindset and drive.","Darwin's The Origin of Species took years of teamwork in the field, hundreds of discussions with colleagues and mentors, and several preliminary drafts.","Mozart labored for more than ten years until he produced any work we admire today; his earlier pieces were often patched-together chunks from other composers.","The belief that the \"gift\" is the ability itself is common, but what feeds it is constant, endless curiosity and challenge seeking.","Benjamin Bloom's study of 120 outstanding achievers found most were not remarkable as children and their future was not predictable from early ability.","Bloom concluded that almost any person in the world can learn almost anything if provided the appropriate prior and current conditions of learning.","Experts agree Jackson Pollock had little native talent for art, yet he became one of the greatest American painters of the twentieth century.","Twyla Tharp argues in The Creative Habit that creativity is not magical inspiration but hard work and dedication: \"There are no natural geniuses.\""],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the myth of innate genius versus the evidence about how achievement actually accumulates. Dweck uses Edison, Darwin, Mozart, Bloom's achievers, and Pollock to show that mastery comes from teamwork, time, and relentless curiosity rather than sudden solitary inspiration.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"People picture Edison alone in a flash of insight. Sort each claim by where it belongs, then answer: which category best describes what the historical record actually shows about achievement?","column_a_label":"Lone-genius myth: sudden solitary inspiration","column_b_label":"The actual record: teamwork, time, and sustained effort","column_c_label":"The curious, challenge-seeking drive","rows":[{"label":"Edison invented the lightbulb alone in a single moment of brilliance","col":"a"},{"label":"Edison had thirty assistants, including well-trained scientists, working around the clock in a corporate-funded lab","col":"b"},{"label":"The lightbulb was a network of time-consuming inventions needing chemists, mathematicians, physicists, engineers, and glassblowers","col":"b"},{"label":"Mozart labored more than ten years before producing any work we admire today","col":"b"},{"label":"Darwin's Origin of Species took years of teamwork, hundreds of discussions, several drafts, and half a lifetime","col":"b"},{"label":"Prodigies are fueled by relentless curiosity and challenge seeking","col":"c"},{"label":"Michael, at three, pulled his father toward math books saying \"Dad, let's go do work\"","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Most prodigies were unremarkable in early childhood.","The lightbulb was a single invention delivered in one insight.","Mozart's early compositions were original masterpieces."],"explanation":"The chapter's central correction is that achievement stories are rarely lone, sudden flashes: Edison ran a corporate-funded lab with thirty assistants, Darwin's masterpiece took years of teamwork and drafts, and Mozart produced nothing we admire until after ten years of labor. The prodigy cases show that relentless curiosity and challenge seeking, not the gift alone, feed achievement."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Praising Effort after a Test Score","visual_frame_description":"A warm kitchen in the late afternoon. Nora, a twelve-year-old, sits at the table with a math worksheet marked eight out of ten, while her mother Priya stands beside her holding a spatula. A sticky note on the fridge shows the upcoming optional advanced problem set, and Nora's face is caught between pride and fear of the harder challenge.","player_mission":"Choose what Priya says next so that Nora enters the next unit ready to take on the challenging problem set rather than retreating from it. Your words can push Nora toward the fixed mindset or teach her the growth-mindset lesson the chapter's research supports.","key_concepts":["Praising a child's intelligence (\"you must be smart at this\") pushes them into the fixed mindset.","Praising effort (\"you must have worked really hard\") supports the growth mindset.","In Dweck's studies, 90 percent of effort-praised students wanted a challenging new task they could learn from.","Ability-praised students rejected challenging tasks out of fear of exposing their flaws.","More than 80 percent of parents believed praising ability was necessary for children's confidence and achievement.","After difficulty, effort-praised students thought \"apply more effort or try new strategies\" rather than \"I'm not smart.\"","Ability-praised students' performance plummeted after difficulty, even on easier problems.","Effort-praised students said the hard problems were the most fun.","Almost 40 percent of ability-praised students lied about their scores to hide imperfection.","Positive labels can rob people of their zest for challenge and their recipes for success."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the content of praise: ability praise (\"you're so smart\") teaches the fixed mindset, while effort praise (\"you worked really hard\") teaches that success comes from process and strategy. Dweck's praise studies showed that effort-praised students embraced challenge while ability-praised students avoided it and lost performance after difficulty.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Nora, a 12-year-old, just came home with a strong score on a hard math test. Her mother, Priya, is delighted and wants to encourage her daughter's confidence for the future. The stakes are the next semester: will Nora chase a challenging new unit, or will she start avoiding anything that might expose a flaw? Priya has one moment to choose how she praises.","dialogue":["Priya: \"Nora, look at this - eight out of ten on the hardest test of the year! I'm so proud of you.\"","Nora: \"Thanks, Mom. It was actually pretty hard.\"","Priya: \"It must feel great to get a score like that.\"","Nora: \"Yeah. I was nervous before the test, but I did it.\"","Priya: \"And your teacher says there's an optional advanced problem set next week for anyone who wants a challenge. Are you going to try it?\"","Nora: \"I don't know. What if I get it wrong and everyone sees I can't do it?\"","Priya: (player chooses what Priya says)"],"choices":["You're so smart at this, honey. Of course you'll ace it - don't even worry.","You must have worked really hard to get that score. Think about what you did that worked, and you can use it again on the challenge.","You got eight right, so you have nothing to be afraid of. Just take the easy path and keep your record safe.","I knew you were a natural the moment you were born. Genius runs in this family."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"The danger of ability praise versus effort praise, from Dweck's praise studies with early adolescents.","psychological_breakdown":"The second option praises the process (\"worked really hard\") and points to a transferable strategy, which in Dweck's studies kept 90 percent of students eager for a challenging task. The first and fourth options praise ability and talent, pushing Nora into the fixed mindset where a challenge threatens her label, and the third encourages her to protect her record by avoiding growth, exactly the low-effort trap the research documents."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Recruiting Harsh Feedback for Learning","visual_frame_description":"A cramped college dorm room at night. Marcus, a junior, holds a paper covered in red ink and a C-minus at the top, while his roommate Alonzo leans in pointing at the professor's name. A draft of the revised essay sits open on the desk beside a printed critique checklist.","player_mission":"Choose Marcus's next move so that he converts the blunt but specific critique into an improvement plan instead of letting a harsh professor's personality decide his trajectory. The right response applies the chapter's lesson about how growth-mindset students treat critical feedback.","key_concepts":["African American students in the Caldwell study were told their essays would be evaluated by a distinguished professor with an Ivy League pedigree.","Caldwell's feedback was quite critical but also helpful.","Fixed-mindset students viewed the critical feedback as a threat, an insult, or an attack and rejected it.","One fixed-mindset student dismissed the feedback with blame: \"He doesn't understand the conciseness of my points.\"","Growth-mindset students called the evaluation \"seemingly honest and specific\" and saw it as \"a stimulus to produce better work.\"","Another growth-mindset student said the professor's comments were \"helpful and clear\" and that he would learn much from him.","The growth mindset allowed students to recruit even a representative of the establishment for their own educational goals.","People in a fixed mindset treat other people as judges; people in a growth mindset can treat them as allies.","Growth-mindset students were in college to get an education, and they were going to get it regardless of the professor's personality."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how a person attributes critical feedback: as an attack on fixed worth or as usable information for improvement. In the Caldwell study, growth-mindset students converted blunt, critical comments into a roadmap, while fixed-mindset students rejected them and learned nothing.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Marcus, a college junior, just got his essay back from Dr. Okonkwo, a demanding professor, with heavy red ink and a C-minus. The comments are blunt, even brutal, but specific and technical. His roommate Alonzo is already spinning the rejection as persecution. Marcus must decide what to do with the feedback before next week's revision deadline.","dialogue":["Marcus: \"Look at this thing. He tore it apart. 'Argument collapses here,' 'unearned conclusion.' It's a massacre.\"","Alonzo: \"That guy's obviously out to get you. He grades on his ego.\"","Marcus: \"Maybe. But some of it... I mean, he circled exactly where my reasoning falls apart.\"","Alonzo: \"Don't give him the satisfaction. He's just some pompous professor who likes to humiliate people.\"","Marcus: \"I could rewrite the whole thing and try again. Or I could just take the C and move on.\"","Alonzo: \"Take the C. He's not worth it.\"","Marcus: (player chooses Marcus's next move)"],"choices":["You're right. He doesn't grade fairly, and I don't want his feedback poisoning my confidence. I'll take the C.","Actually, his comments are specific and clear. Arrogant or not, I can learn from him - I'm going to revise with his notes as a map.","I'll rewrite it, but only to prove to him he's wrong. My original argument was right, and I'm not changing it.","I'll drop the class and blame the grading. No professor should get to define my ability."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"The growth-mindset response to critical feedback, demonstrated in the Edward Caldwell III essay-evaluation study.","psychological_breakdown":"The growth-mindset students in the Caldwell study called the evaluation \"seemingly honest and specific\" and \"a stimulus to produce better work,\" recruiting the criticism for their own goals, which the second option does. The first option is the fixed-mindset rejection that guarantees no learning, the third uses the feedback only to defend the ego rather than to learn, and the fourth lets another person's evaluation drive Marcus out of the field."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Pre-Med Chemistry: Studying to Learn versus Studying to Ace","visual_frame_description":"A hushed campus library at midnight. Jordan stares at a graded chemistry exam with a failing score, surrounded by a stack of notes that have clearly been reread many times. Priya sits across the table with a clean list of themes and a notebook of corrected mistakes, and a whiteboard behind them shows two contrasting study paths.","player_mission":"Choose Priya's reply so that Jordan changes study strategy rather than concluding that chemistry is simply not their subject. The best response relies on what the chapter found about how growth-mindset students in the pre-med chemistry course actually earned better grades.","key_concepts":["In the pre-med chemistry study, growth-mindset students earned better grades in the course.","When growth-mindset students did poorly on a test, they bounced back on the next ones; fixed-mindset students often did not make a comeback.","Fixed-mindset students studied by reading the textbook and notes and rereading hard material, \"like a vacuum cleaner.\"","Growth-mindset students said \"I looked for themes and underlying principles across lectures.\"","Growth-mindset students said \"I went over mistakes until I was certain I understood them.\"","Fixed-mindset students who did poorly concluded chemistry was not their subject, saying \"I did everything possible, didn't I?\"","Growth-mindset students maintained their interest and kept themselves motivated even when the course was dry or the instructor was a stiff.","One undergraduate reported that before learning the growth mindset she read tough material over and over; afterward she used better strategies.","People with the growth mindset are clued into all the different ways to create learning.","Fixed-mindset pre-med students would do almost anything for a good grade except take charge of the process to make sure it happens."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how study strategy follows mindset: fixed-mindset students memorize and conclude \"chemistry is not my subject,\" while growth-mindset students study to learn by seeking principles and reviewing mistakes. In Dweck's chemistry study, these process-oriented strategies were the reason growth-mindset students got higher grades.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Jordan and Priya are pre-med students in a notoriously hard introductory chemistry course that gates admission to the medical track. Both just failed the first midterm. Jordan studied by rereading the textbook and class notes over and over, and now wants to conclude the course is not for them. Priya, who scored better, is trying to help Jordan change strategy before the next exam.","dialogue":["Jordan: \"I did everything possible. Read the textbook, read my notes, read them again. I still failed. Chemistry just isn't my subject.\"","Priya: \"You memorized a lot of facts. Did you ever look for the big ideas connecting them?\"","Jordan: \"There's no time for big ideas. I just need to survive the test.\"","Priya: \"When you got a question wrong in your practice, what did you do with it?\"","Jordan: \"I moved on. There were fifty more to get through.\"","Priya: \"What if instead you went over every mistake until you were certain you understood why it was wrong?\"","Jordan: \"That sounds like I'd have to slow down. I'd fall further behind.\"","Priya: (player chooses Priya's reply)"],"choices":["Forget the big ideas - just keep rereading until the material sticks. That's what everyone does.","The students who did best in this course looked for themes and underlying principles, and they went over their mistakes until they understood them. Slow down; that's what actually works.","Honestly, if chemistry isn't your subject, you should quit now before you waste more time.","Just pick the section with the most points and memorize that one cold before the exam."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Growth-mindset study strategies in the pre-med chemistry study: seeking themes and underlying principles and reviewing mistakes.","psychological_breakdown":"In the chemistry study, growth-mindset students earned better grades not because they were smarter but because they studied to learn, reporting \"I looked for themes and underlying principles across lectures\" and \"I went over mistakes until I was certain I understood them,\" which the second option offers. The first option reproduces the fixed-mindset vacuum-cleaner approach, the third confirms a premature verdict instead of fixing strategy, and the fourth is tactical cramming that treats the test, not learning, as the goal."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Low-Effort Syndrome and the Moment Everything Changes","visual_frame_description":"A small after-school workshop room with a circle of desks. Fourteen-year-old Jimmy slouches in a hoodie, doodling on his desk, while Mr. Alvarez, a patient teacher, sits beside him. The blackboard behind them reads \"Intelligence can be developed\" in chalk, and Jimmy's face is suddenly wet-eyed and alert, as though hearing something for the first time.","player_mission":"Choose Mr. Alvarez's response in the crucial moment after Jimmy asks whether he really doesn't have to be dumb. Your words will either confirm the fixed mindset's verdict or deliver the message that, in the chapter, unleashed a hard-core low-effort student's effort for good.","key_concepts":["Fixed-mindset adolescents see adolescence as one big test with a verdict: smart or dumb, winner or loser.","In the fixed mindset, a loser is forever.","Fixed-mindset students said their main goal in school, aside from looking smart, was to exert as little effort as possible.","The low-effort syndrome lets students answer the adults' challenge of measurement with \"No you won't.\"","John Holt described students whose intelligence had become disconnected from their schooling when they were sitting under judgment.","Teaching the growth mindset to junior high students unleashed their effort.","Jimmy, the most hard-core low-effort kid, said \"You mean I don't have to be dumb?\" and began working from that day.","Jimmy started staying up late to do homework and handing in assignments early so he could get feedback and revise.","Jimmy came to believe that working hard was not something that made you vulnerable but something that made you smarter.","For growth-mindset students, adolescence is a time of opportunity to learn new subjects and find out what they want to become."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the low-effort syndrome of the fixed mindset, in which students withhold effort to avoid being measured, against the growth-mindset message that effort builds intelligence. Dweck reports that a single growth-mindset message transformed a hard-core low-effort student, Jimmy, who then began working and revising eagerly.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Mr. Alvarez is a middle-school teacher running a mindset workshop for a small group of students who have stopped trying. Fourteen-year-old Jimmy is the most hard-core turned-off kid in the room: he refuses homework, doodles through class, and insists that trying hard is for suckers. Alvarez has one conversation that could change how Jimmy sees his own intelligence.","dialogue":["Mr. Alvarez: \"Jimmy, you've been pretty clear that you don't care about school. What's the point of it all, in your view?\"","Jimmy: \"The point is to look smart and not get measured. If I try and fail, everyone knows I'm stupid. If I don't try, nobody can prove anything.\"","Mr. Alvarez: \"So what would it mean if trying actually made you smarter, not more vulnerable?\"","Jimmy: \"Everybody knows you're either born smart or you're not. My dad says I've got more mouth than brains.\"","Mr. Alvarez: \"What if I told you that intelligence is something you can develop - that your brain grows with effort and learning?\"","Jimmy: (his voice drops) \"You mean I don't have to be dumb?\"","Mr. Alvarez: (player chooses Mr. Alvarez's response)"],"choices":["Well, some kids are just born with it, and you have to accept where you stand. Don't set yourself up for disappointment.","That's right. Working hard isn't something that makes you vulnerable - it's something that makes you smarter. Your effort is how you build your brain.","Maybe. But honestly, with your record, college is probably off the table. Let's just get you through the year.","It depends on your genes. I can't promise anything."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Teaching the growth mindset unleashes effort, as in Dweck's junior high intervention and Jimmy's turning point.","psychological_breakdown":"When Dweck's workshop told turned-off students that intelligence can be developed, Jimmy looked up with tears in his eyes and said \"You mean I don't have to be dumb?\" and from that day he did homework, handed it in early for feedback, and revised. The second option delivers that message. The others reaffirm the fixed mindset's verdict, the low-effort protection, or genetic determinism, which the chapter argues leads students to protect their egos instead of learning."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Belonging and Other People's Verdicts","visual_frame_description":"A quiet coffee shop at noon. Amara, a computer science student, stirs her latte without drinking it, while her mentor Dr. Elena Ruiz, a senior engineer, leans forward across the small table. Amara's laptop screen shows a completed algorithm, and a folded lab name-tag sits beside her coffee cup.","player_mission":"Choose Dr. Ruiz's response so that Amara keeps her confidence and sense of belonging in a field where her supervisor's condescending remarks are chipping away at her. The right reply applies the chapter's findings on stereotype threat and on trusting other people's assessments.","key_concepts":["Steele and Aronson's research shows that evoking a stereotype fills the mind with distracting worries and lowers test scores.","Stereotype threat mainly harms people in a fixed mindset, who believe in permanent inferiority.","The growth mindset takes the teeth out of the stereotype and lets people fight back.","In a calculus study, growth-mindset women felt a fairly strong and stable sense of belonging even when they sensed a lot of negative stereotyping.","Fixed-mindset women felt a shrinking sense of belonging as the semester wore on, and stereotyping made their comfort with math wither.","One fixed-mindset student's sense of belonging fell because a professor said \"that was a good guess\" whenever she gave a correct answer.","Women learn to trust other people's estimates of them: \"If they criticize me, it must be true.\"","Frances Conley, the first woman tenured in neurosurgery at an American medical school, questioned herself after a man condescendingly called her \"honey\" during surgery.","Julie Lynch, a budding techie who loved programming, lost interest after a teacher criticized a shortcut in a program that ran fine.","The fixed mindset, plus stereotyping, plus women's trust in others' assessments all contribute to the gender gap in math and science.","A growth mindset helps people see prejudice for what it is - someone else's view of them - and confront it with their confidence intact."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how stereotype threat and excessive trust in others' opinions erode belonging and performance in stereotyped fields. Dweck's calculus study showed that growth-mindset women kept a stable sense of belonging amid stereotyping, while fixed-mindset women's comfort withered under comments like \"that was a good guess.\"","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Amara is a gifted computer science student who aced her algorithms course. A supervisor on a summer research project keeps saying \"that was a good guess\" whenever she gives a correct answer, and today another teammate called her \"honey.\" Amara is starting to doubt whether she belongs in a male-dominated field. Her mentor, Dr. Elena Ruiz, a senior engineer, is meeting her for coffee.","dialogue":["Amara: \"I keep second-guessing myself in the lab. The supervisor says 'good guess' every time I'm right, and one of the guys called me 'honey' while I was debugging.\"","Elena: \"How does that land with you?\"","Amara: \"I start wondering if my answers are actually mine, or if I'm fooling everyone. Maybe I just got lucky to be here.\"","Elena: \"Do you think you got lucky?\"","Amara: \"No. But I trust what they say about me. Everyone always told me I was good, so if they say I'm not, maybe they're right.\"","Elena: (player chooses Elena's reply)"],"choices":["If they think you're not good enough, you should trust them. They know the field better than you do.","Their comments are about them, not about you. Other people's opinions are not a reliable measure of your ability - you have evidence you belong, so use their skepticism as fuel and keep learning.","You're right, tech just isn't friendly to people like you. Maybe you should pick another major.","Just ignore it and never speak up again. Stay quiet and nobody will notice you."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Stereotype threat, women's tendency to trust others' assessments, and the growth-mindset protection of belonging.","psychological_breakdown":"Dweck argues that stereotype threat, plus women's trust in others' assessments, helps create the gender gap in math and science: neurosurgeon Frances Conley questioned herself after a condescending \"honey,\" and a college woman's sense of belonging shrank after a professor's \"that was a good guess.\" Growth-mindset women kept a stable sense of belonging even amid stereotyping by seeing prejudice as someone else's view. The second option conveys that; the others validate others' verdicts, confirm the stereotype, or counsel withdrawal."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Fixed Mindset's Grip on a Failing Student","visual_frame_description":"A seventh-grade classroom the morning after a hard midterm. Priya, once the top student, stares at her desk over a paper marked B-minus, whispering to her friend; across the room Tom, with the same grade, already stands at the teacher's desk asking where he lost points. A split-screen overlay highlights Priya's reread workbook and Tom's margin notes and redo-list.","player_mission":"Study the scene to identify the fixed-mindset mechanisms gripping Priya, then choose the intervention that will turn her toward learning before she sinks into the low-effort syndrome. The battle tests your command of the chapter's account of how the fixed mindset limits achievement.","key_concepts":["The fixed mindset limits achievement by filling minds with interfering thoughts, making effort disagreeable, and producing inferior learning strategies.","The fixed mindset makes other people into judges instead of allies.","Fixed-mindset students explained poor junior high grades by maligning their ability (\"I am the stupidest\") or blaming the teacher.","Growth-mindset students responded to feeling overwhelmed by digging in and doing what it takes.","George Danzig solved two famous unsolved math problems because he treated them as demanding homework he had to crack.","Fixed-mindset students studied like a vacuum cleaner, rereading without diagnosing mistakes.","Growth-mindset students went over mistakes until they were certain they understood them.","Praising ability pushed students into the fixed mindset and made them reject challenge.","Praising effort kept 90 percent of students eager for a challenging task they could learn from.","Jimmy's turning point, \"You mean I don't have to be dumb?\", showed that teaching the growth mindset unleashes effort.","Important achievements require clear focus, all-out effort, a bottomless trunk of strategies, and allies in learning."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle integrates the chapter's account of the fixed mindset's mechanisms - self-judging thoughts, effort-avoidance, and passive strategies - against the growth-mindset alternative. Dweck's junior high study and praise experiments show that growth-mindset messages and process praise, not ability praise, restore effort and achievement.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests your command of the chapter's core claim about school achievement: the fixed mindset limits achievement through interfering self-judgments, effort-avoidance, and inferior learning strategies, while the growth mindset keeps the student in charge of learning. You will study a student in crisis, identify the fixed-mindset mechanisms at work, then choose the intervention that turns the tide.","stage_1_observation":"You are observing Mrs. Vega's classroom the day after a hard midterm. Priya, once the top student in the class, got a B-minus and has gone quiet. She stares at her desk. When Mrs. Vega hands back papers, Priya whispers to her friend: \"I'm the stupidest one in here now.\" Across the room, Tom, who scored the same grade, is already asking the teacher for the rubric and where he lost points. Priya's workbook shows the same page reread four times; Tom's shows margin notes and a list of problems to redo.","stage_1_question":"Which behaviors in the scene reveal that Priya is in a fixed-mindset response to her grade, as the chapter describes?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Priya labels her whole ability (\"I'm the stupidest\"), reacts to the grade as a permanent verdict on her worth, and shows the passive vacuum-cleaner habit of rereading without diagnosing mistakes, while Tom's targeted questions and redo-list are growth-mindset responses. Fixed-mindset students malign their ability or blame others, while growth-mindset students dig in and go over their mistakes.","stage_2_intervention":"Mrs. Vega now has a brief window to intervene before Priya sinks into the low-effort syndrome - protecting her ego by not trying so she cannot be measured again. Which intervention is most consistent with the chapter's evidence about praise, feedback, and teaching the growth mindset?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Mrs. Vega should praise the process and give strategy-rich feedback, for example \"You worked hard, and here is exactly how to fix the two places you lost points; let's go over your mistakes until you understand them,\" and teach that intelligence develops with effort. Effort praise and growth-mindset messages keep students engaged with challenge, while ability praise pushes them into avoidance and confirms the fixed mindset."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Positive-Label Trap at Work","visual_frame_description":"A modern architecture studio late at night. Marcus, a junior designer, sits frozen in front of a rejected proposal while a stack of old praise emails glows on his screen. His senior partner Dana watches from the doorway, and a crumpled critique sheet lies on the floor where Marcus dropped it.","player_mission":"Observe how the \"you're a natural\" label changed Marcus's behavior after his first setback, then choose the feedback that restores his learning in the one meeting Dana has. The battle tests the chapter's warning that praise and positive labels can rob people of their zest for challenge and their recipes for success.","key_concepts":["Ability praise, such as \"You must be smart at this,\" pushed students right into the fixed mindset.","After difficulty, ability-praised students concluded they were not smart after all.","Effort-praised students read difficulty as a signal to apply more effort or try new strategies.","After success, everyone loved the problems; after difficulty, ability-praised students said it wasn't fun anymore.","The burden of talent was killing the enjoyment of ability-praised students.","Almost 40 percent of ability-praised students lied about their scores to hide imperfection.","Adam Guettel, hailed as the crown prince of musical theater, bowed out of a Metropolitan Opera broadcast at thirteen, faking a voice change because he didn't want to handle the pressure.","Guettel said, \"To be good is to fail. To be very good is to fail... The only thing not a failure is to be great.\"","Seth Abrams, praised for intelligence as a child, wrote at 49 that he was learning to see failure as lack of experience and skill rather than stupidity.","Positive labels such as \"gifted,\" \"talented,\" and \"brilliant\" can rob people of their zest for challenge.","Growth-mindset students saw critical feedback as \"a stimulus to produce better work.\""],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the mechanism behind positive labels: ability praise makes people stake their worth on performance, so a setback reads as a permanent verdict on their talent. Dweck's praise studies and the Guettel case show this reduces enjoyment, honesty, and performance, while process praise keeps learning intact.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the danger of praise and positive labels, which the chapter says can rob people of their zest for challenge and their recipes for success. You will observe how a \"you're a natural\" label changes a rising employee's behavior after a setback, then pick the feedback that keeps their learning alive.","stage_1_observation":"At an architecture firm, partner Dana has always told new hire Marcus, \"You're a natural - you've got real talent,\" and Marcus has thrived on it. Today, Marcus's first big proposal was shredded in review. Dana finds Marcus staring at his screen: he has stopped working and is scrolling old praise emails. When a senior associate offers to walk him through the critique, Marcus says, \"If I was really talented, I wouldn't need help.\" He has quietly scheduled the rest of the week to avoid the project.","stage_1_question":"Which of Marcus's reactions shows the chapter's documented effect of ability praise after difficulty?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Marcus treats the setback as evidence that he was never talented after all. The chapter found that students praised for ability concluded that failure meant they were deficient, lost their enjoyment, and avoided anything that could expose flaws, while effort-praised people read difficulty as a cue to apply more effort or new strategies.","stage_2_intervention":"Dana has one meeting to repair the damage before Marcus abandons the proposal and stops learning. Which response best applies the chapter's findings about praise and feedback?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Dana should retrain the attribution: reframe the review as useful information and praise process rather than talent, for example \"Your instincts are not what carried you; the way you worked the brief did. Here is exactly where to improve, and going over this critique is how you get better.\" This mirrors the growth-mindset response of using critical feedback as \"a stimulus to produce better work.\""}]},{"id":"ch09","title":"Chapter 4: Sports: The Mindset of a Champion","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"What Counts as Success in Sport","visual_frame_description":"A digital sorting board fills the screen, divided into three labeled columns with block-letter headers. Seven glossy evidence cards float beneath, each stamped with an athlete's name and a quoted line. A timer ticks softly as the player drags cards toward the column that matches the mindset behind each quote. The card for Jackie Joyner-Kersee glows faintly, marking it as the featured evidence to classify first.","player_mission":"Drag each athlete's quote onto the column that reflects the mindset behind it: growth-mindset success, fixed-mindset superiority, or the fixed belief that real talent needs no effort. Classify the featured Joyner-Kersee card first, then sort the remaining six quotes into the same three categories.","key_concepts":["In Dweck's account, people with a growth mindset find success in doing their best, in learning, and in improving rather than in the scoreboard alone.","Jackie Joyner-Kersee says the joy of athletics never resided in winning for her and that she derives just as much happiness from the process as from the results.","John Wooden said many non-championship games gave him as much pleasure as his ten national titles because the team prepared fully and played near its highest level.","Tiger Woods was disappointed not to repeat at the 1998 Masters but was proud of his top-ten finish because he squeezed the towel dry and hung in there.","Mia Hamm says that if you walk off the field knowing you gave everything you had, you will always be a winner.","For people with the fixed mindset, success is about establishing their superiority, being the somebody worthier than the nobodies.","John McEnroe admitted that at one time his head was so big it could barely fit through the door, and his account contains no talk of effort or personal best.","In the fixed mindset, effort is not a cause for pride; it casts doubt on your talent, so a fixed-mindset athlete prefers to perform without rehearsal.","Iciss Tillis exemplified the somebody-nobody syndrome when she said that if she won she would be somebody and if she lost she would be a nobody.","The chapter treats the belief that you need to be naturally gifted to be good at sports as a fixed-mindset conviction, in contrast to practice-based improvement."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests how each mindset defines success: the growth mindset defines it as doing your best, learning, and improving, while the fixed mindset defines it as proving superiority over others and protecting the belief that talent needs no effort. Dweck grounds the distinction in athletes like Joyner-Kersee and Woods, who take pride in effort, versus McEnroe and Tillis, who measure themselves by being somebody rather than by the process.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Each evidence card quotes an athlete describing what success means to them. Sort each card into the column that best matches the mindset it reflects, starting with the featured card for Jackie Joyner-Kersee.","column_a_label":"Growth mindset: success is doing your best, learning, and improving","column_b_label":"Fixed mindset: success is proving superiority over others","column_c_label":"Fixed mindset: real talent needs no effort","rows":[{"label":"Jackie Joyner-Kersee: 'I derive just as much happiness from the process as from the results.'","col":"a"},{"label":"John McEnroe: 'There was a time when my head was so big it could barely fit through the door.'","col":"b"},{"label":"Tiger Woods after the 1998 Masters: 'I squeezed the towel dry this week. I'm very proud of the way I hung in there.'","col":"a"},{"label":"John McEnroe: 'Some people don't want to rehearse; they just want to perform. I'm in the former group.'","col":"c"},{"label":"Mia Hamm: 'If you walk off the field knowing that you gave everything you had, you will always be a winner.'","col":"a"},{"label":"Iciss Tillis: 'If I win, I'll be somebody; if I lose, I'll be nobody.'","col":"b"},{"label":"A fixed-mindset athlete, per Stuart Biddle's survey: 'To be good at sports you need to be naturally gifted.'","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Placing Joyner-Kersee under Column B because she won Olympic medals and therefore must be proving superiority.","Placing her under Column C because she competed at an elite level, implying she never needed to work.","Placing her under Column B because she says she does not mind losing, which is read as a boast about being above her rivals."],"explanation":"Joyner-Kersee explicitly says the joy of athletics never resided in winning for her and that she gets as much happiness from the process as from the results, which is the growth mindset's definition of success. The distractors fail because they read her Olympic achievements as superiority claims or effortless talent, ignoring her own words about the process, practice, and seeing herself improve."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"How Champions Respond to Failure","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen training room appears: on one side a video wall loops moments of athletes after defeats, and on the other sit three column bins labeled with response styles. Cards drop one at a time carrying scenes: a golfer kicking his shoe, a runner muttering to herself mid-race, a pitcher hurling his helmet. A narrator's console shows the featured card for Jackie Joyner-Kersee's asthma comeback, highlighted for first classification.","player_mission":"Classify each reaction to a setback into one of three response styles: growth mindset treating setbacks as information, fixed mindset blaming outside forces, or self-protective avoidance that quits rather than faces failure. Begin with the featured Joyner-Kersee card, then sort the remaining six scenes.","key_concepts":["Finding #2 in the chapter: those with a growth mindset found setbacks motivating, informative, and a wake-up call.","After the Bulls were eliminated in the play-offs in his comeback year, Michael Jordan said he would be physically and mentally prepared from then on, and the Bulls won the title the next three years.","Jackie Joyner-Kersee talked herself through an asthma attack in the 800-meter race, instructing herself to keep pumping her arms and telling herself she could make it.","In the fixed mindset, setbacks label you rather than inform you.","John McEnroe did not play mixed doubles again for twenty years after one loss at Wimbledon, calling it the ultimate embarrassment.","John McEnroe smashed a new black Les Paul guitar to pieces instead of practicing after seeing Buddy Guy play.","Sergio Garcia fired caddie after caddie, blamed them for everything that went wrong, and once punished his shoe for a missed shot by throwing and kicking it.","When college basketball outlawed the dunk, Kareem Abdul-Jabbar worked twice as hard on his bank shot off the glass, his skyhook, and his turnaround jumper.","Michael Jordan embraced his failures, noting he had missed more than nine thousand shots and lost almost three hundred games, and went back to practice each time.","The chapter says the growth mindset allows you to dig down and turn an important setback into an important win, which Pedro Martinez could not do."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the fixed-versus-growth attribution of failure: in the growth mindset a setback is information that motivates renewed work, while in the fixed mindset it labels the person and triggers blame or escape. Dweck contrasts Jordan and Joyner-Kersee, who converted setbacks into effort, with McEnroe and Garcia, who blamed outside forces or avoided the situation entirely.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Each card shows an athlete's reaction to a setback. Sort it into the response style it best exemplifies, starting with the featured card for Jackie Joyner-Kersee's asthma attack.","column_a_label":"Growth mindset: setbacks are information and a wake-up call","column_b_label":"Fixed mindset: blame outside forces and other people","column_c_label":"Self-protective avoidance: quit or escape rather than face failure","rows":[{"label":"Jackie Joyner-Kersee during an asthma attack in the 800m: 'Just keep pumping your arms. It's not that bad, so keep going. You can make it.'","col":"a"},{"label":"Michael Jordan after the Bulls' play-off elimination: 'You can't leave and think you can come back and dominate this game. I will be physically and mentally prepared from now on.'","col":"a"},{"label":"Kareem Abdul-Jabbar after the dunk was outlawed: he worked twice as hard on his bank shot, skyhook, and turnaround jumper.","col":"a"},{"label":"John McEnroe after losing mixed doubles at Wimbledon: 'That was the ultimate embarrassment. I said, that's it. I'm never playing again. I can't handle this.'","col":"c"},{"label":"Sergio Garcia after a missed shot: he blamed his shoe and punished it by throwing and kicking it.","col":"b"},{"label":"John McEnroe when he bought a new Les Paul guitar: he smashed it to pieces instead of practicing.","col":"c"},{"label":"McEnroe on a stretch of poor play: 'little things happened that kept me off my game for weeks at a time.'","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Placing Joyner-Kersee in Column C because she was having an asthma attack and could reasonably have stopped racing.","Placing her in Column B because she blamed her lungs and wind for the difficulty.","Placing her in Column C because talking to yourself is a sign of giving up."],"explanation":"Joyner-Kersee kept racing and won, instructing herself to pump her arms and telling herself she could make it, which is exactly the growth mindset's treatment of a setback as a hurdle to work through rather than a label. The distractors fail because she neither quit, blamed others, nor treated the attack as a verdict on her ability; she treated it as a temporary obstacle to push through."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Effort and the Myth of the Natural","visual_frame_description":"A crowded sports-desk scene renders as a classification board: three columns labeled with beliefs about effort and talent, and cards showing scouts in the stands, physical-therapy rooms, and interview clips. One card pictures Marshall Faulk with the words savant's gift glowing, marking it as the featured evidence. A banner reads 'The Myth of the Natural' across the top of the screen.","player_mission":"Sort each card into the column it represents: the fixed mindset about effort, the growth mindset about effort and training, or the observer tendency to revere the natural. Classify the featured Faulk card first, then the remaining six cards.","key_concepts":["Sports is where the idea of a natural comes from, and many scouts and coaches search only for naturals and pay exorbitant sums to recruit them.","Billy Beane, the quintessential natural, believed that natural talent should not need effort, that effort is for the less endowed, and that asking for help is an admission of weakness.","In Stuart Biddle's research, people with a fixed mindset about sports believed you cannot really do much to change your level of ability and that you need to be naturally gifted.","People with a growth mindset about sports agreed that how good you are will always improve if you work harder, and that you need to learn techniques and practice them regularly.","Wilma Rudolph, paralyzed on the left side as a child, pursued physical therapy for eight years and then won three Olympic golds, a visible lesson that physical skills can be developed.","Jackie Joyner-Kersee attributes her transformation to all those hours of work on the bridle path, the neighborhood sidewalks, and the schoolhouse corridors.","Malcolm Gladwell argues that deep down we revere the naturals and prize natural endowment over earned ability.","Marshall Faulk sees his uncanny football knowledge as the product of insatiable curiosity and years of study, but players and coaches call it a savant's gift or innate football intelligence.","Babe Ruth, hardly a specimen of physical perfection, made himself a great hitter through off-season discipline, rising like a rocket from the ashes of 1925.","Stephen Jay Gould described the common view that ballplayers are hunks of meat naturally and effortlessly displaying the talents that nature provided."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the beliefs that surround effort: the fixed mindset holds that real talent should not need effort, while the growth mindset holds that skill develops through training and practice. Dweck adds the cultural layer that observers themselves revere naturals and credit innate endowment even when the athlete's own history is one of work, as with Faulk's football IQ being called a gift.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Each card presents a belief or a reaction about talent and effort. Sort it into the column it best represents, starting with the featured card about Marshall Faulk.","column_a_label":"Fixed mindset about effort: natural talent should not need effort","column_b_label":"Growth mindset about effort: training and practice develop skill","column_c_label":"Revering the natural: observers credit innate endowment over earned ability","rows":[{"label":"Teammates describe Faulk's ability to read defensive alignments as a savant's gift, and a coach calls it very innate football intelligence.","col":"c"},{"label":"'Natural talent does not ask for help. It is an admission of weakness.' (Billy Beane's fixed-mindset conviction)","col":"a"},{"label":"'How good you are at sports will always improve if you work harder at it.' (Stuart Biddle's growth-mindset respondents)","col":"b"},{"label":"'You have a certain level of ability in sports and you cannot really do much to change that level.' (Stuart Biddle's fixed-mindset respondents)","col":"a"},{"label":"Wilma Rudolph's eight years of physical therapy rebuilt her paralyzed left leg and preceded three Olympic golds.","col":"b"},{"label":"Malcolm Gladwell's point that we prize natural endowment over earned ability.","col":"c"},{"label":"'To be successful in sports, you need to learn techniques and skills and practice them regularly.' (Stuart Biddle's growth-mindset respondents)","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Placing the Faulk card in Column A because Faulk is a superstar who is assumed to need no practice.","Placing it in Column B because Faulk clearly worked hard at football.","Placing it in Column A because calling his skill a gift implies he rejects coaching."],"explanation":"The card sorts to Column C because the observation is about how others explain Faulk's skill, not about his own beliefs: he attributes his football IQ to insatiable curiosity and study, while his teammates and coach call it a gift and innate intelligence. The distractors fail because they describe Faulk's own work ethic rather than the observers' tendency to revere the natural, which is exactly the cultural habit the chapter highlights."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Taking Charge of the Process","visual_frame_description":"A control-room console displays three columns for how athletes manage their careers, with surveillance clips of practices and press rooms feeding the screen. One clip shows Mark O'Meara choosing to study Tiger Woods instead of sulking, marked as the featured evidence. Readouts labeled Jordan, Woods, O'Meara, and McEnroe scroll beneath the columns as the player sorts each scene.","player_mission":"Classify each card by how the athlete manages performance over time: taking charge of the process, treating talent as a finished product, or demanding that someone else fix things. Start with the featured O'Meara card, then sort the remaining six.","key_concepts":["Finding #3 in the chapter: people with the growth mindset in sports took charge of the processes that bring success and that maintain it.","As Michael Jordan aged and lost some stamina and agility, he worked even harder on conditioning and on moves like the turnaround and fallaway jumpers.","Tiger Woods took charge of the process: he managed his attention and course strategy, and his father trained him to stay undistracted from the age of three.","Woods managed his motivation by making practice fun and by imagining a rival somewhere out there who would challenge him.","Mark O'Meara chose to learn from playing beside Woods rather than feel jealous of and diminished by him, and his game turned around.","In the fixed mindset you do not take control of your abilities and motivation; you look for your talent to carry you through.","The chapter says people in the fixed mindset treat themselves as finished products that have to protect themselves, lament, and blame.","McEnroe blamed little things for keeping him off his game for weeks and wished he played a team sport so he could hide his flaws.","McEnroe said that when you cannot control yourself, you want someone to do it for you, and that he acutely missed being part of a team sport.","Woods said, 'I know my game. I know what I want to achieve, I know how to get there,' and even the most reliable golf swing stays a work in progress."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the third finding of the chapter: the growth mindset takes charge of the processes that create and sustain success, while the fixed mindset casts the self as a finished product that protects, laments, and blames. Dweck contrasts Jordan and Woods, who deliberately managed conditioning, attention, and motivation, with McEnroe, who blamed outside forces and wished a team would handle his self-control for him.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Each card describes how an athlete managed his career over time. Sort it into the column that best matches his behavior, starting with the featured card about Mark O'Meara.","column_a_label":"Growth mindset: takes charge of the process of success","column_b_label":"Fixed mindset: a finished product that protects, laments, and blames","column_c_label":"Fixed mindset: demands someone else fix the problem","rows":[{"label":"Mark O'Meara, playing beside a superior Tiger Woods, chose to learn from him rather than feel diminished by him.","col":"a"},{"label":"Michael Jordan, losing stamina with age, worked even harder on conditioning and on his turnaround and fallaway jumpers.","col":"a"},{"label":"Tiger Woods managed his motivation by making practice fun and picturing a twelve-year-old rival working hard somewhere out there.","col":"a"},{"label":"The fixed-mindset athlete: 'You are not a work in progress, you're a finished product. And finished products have to protect themselves, lament, and blame.'","col":"b"},{"label":"McEnroe on a weak stretch: 'little things happened that kept me off my game for weeks at a time.'","col":"b"},{"label":"McEnroe wishing he played a team sport so that when he was not at his peak he could hide it more easily.","col":"c"},{"label":"McEnroe: 'When you can't control yourself, you want someone to do it for you, that's where I acutely missed being part of a team sport.'","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Placing O'Meara in Column B because being jealous of a superior player is a natural fixed-mindset reaction.","Placing him in Column C because he relied on Woods to coach him.","Placing him in Column B because he admitted his game had never fulfilled its potential."],"explanation":"O'Meara sorts to Column A because his defining choice was to take charge of his own game by learning from Woods rather than protecting his ego, which the chapter credits with turning his career around. The distractors fail because he neither blamed others, demanded rescue, nor treated his talent as fixed; he deliberately chose the learning path over the diminishing, jealous one."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Star and the Team","visual_frame_description":"A basketball court renders in the background of a classification board with three columns about team responsibility. Evidence cards show a seven-foot center pouting at a coaching whiteboard, a receiver calling himself a star in the sky, and a swimmer crossing a channel trailed by a flotilla of support boats. The card for Michael Jordan, captioned with his teamwork quote, glows as the featured item.","player_mission":"Sort each card into the column it best represents: filling a role for the team, validating your own superstar status, or the truth that every sport is a team sport. Classify the featured Jordan card first, then the remaining six.","key_concepts":["Michael Jordan: a superstar's talent can win games, but it is teamwork that wins championships.","Patrick Ewing was unhappy about playing power forward because center is the star position, and his Knicks never won a championship.","Alex Rodriguez, then the best shortstop in baseball, agreed to retrain as third base when he joined the Yankees.","John Wooden said he could have made Kareem the greatest scorer in college history, but the team would never have won three national championships.","Keyshawn Johnson, comparing himself to an opposing star, said you are trying to compare a flashlight to a star, and declared he was an individual first.","When some star players are interviewed they say we, and when others are interviewed they say I and treat teammates as privileged to participate in their greatness.","Diana Nyad's 102.5-mile open-water record swim required a team of guides, divers, NASA experts, and trainers, fifty-one other people.","The chapter argues that just about every sport is in some sense a team sport, because even individual sports rest on coaches, trainers, and mentors.","Eddie Pawelski, started by John Wooden against Fort Wayne Central, took apart the toughest player in the state and never sat out again.","Coach Wooden said ability can get you to the top but it takes character to keep you there, and reminded readers that champions have character."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the chapter's argument that the growth mindset embraces filling a role within a team, while the fixed mindset drives athletes to validate their own superstar identity. Dweck uses Ewing's refusal to play power forward and Keyshawn Johnson's flashlight-to-star boast as cautionary tales, set against Jordan's teamwork quote, Wooden's handling of Kareem, and the fifty-one-person team behind Diana Nyad's swim.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Each card describes an attitude or a fact about stardom and the team. Sort it into the column it best represents, starting with the featured card about Michael Jordan.","column_a_label":"Growth mindset: fill a role, teamwork wins championships","column_b_label":"Fixed mindset: validate your talent, be the superstar","column_c_label":"Every sport is a team sport","rows":[{"label":"Michael Jordan: 'A superstar's talent can win games, but it's teamwork that wins championships.'","col":"a"},{"label":"Patrick Ewing, asked to play power forward for the Knicks, was unhappy because center is the star position, and his Knicks never won a title.","col":"b"},{"label":"John Wooden on Kareem Abdul-Jabbar: 'I could have made Kareem the greatest scorer in college history... Would we have won three national championships? Never.'","col":"a"},{"label":"Keyshawn Johnson: 'I am a team player, but I'm an individual first... If I'm not the No. 1 guy, I'm no good to you.'","col":"b"},{"label":"Diana Nyad's 102.5-mile record swim was supported by guides, divers, NASA experts, and trainers, fifty-one other people.","col":"c"},{"label":"Alex Rodriguez agreed to retrain as third base when he joined the Yankees, even though he was then the best shortstop in baseball.","col":"a"},{"label":"After a game, some star players say we while others say I and treat teammates as people privileged to participate in their greatness.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Placing the Jordan card in Column B because Jordan is the greatest superstar in basketball.","Placing it in Column C because basketball is obviously a team sport.","Placing it in Column B because Jordan is famous for demanding the ball in clutch moments."],"explanation":"The Jordan card sorts to Column A because it is his explicit philosophy that while a superstar's talent can win individual games, championships come from teamwork, the same growth-mindset value Wooden applied to Kareem and A-Rod applied to third base. The distractors fail because they judge by Jordan's fame or role rather than by the content of his claim about winning championships through teamwork."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Coaching a Talented Teen Through a Bad Game","visual_frame_description":"In an emptying stadium at dusk, sixteen-year-old Maya sits alone on the bench in full soccer kit, head in her hands. Coach Delgado, a middle-aged woman in a team windbreaker, walks over and crouches in front of her. The scoreboard behind them still reads the district final score, and the player's dialogue choices appear as speech bubbles over Maya's head.","player_mission":"Play Coach Delgado and respond to Maya after her scoreless district final loss. Choose the reply that keeps her learning from the setback instead of labeling herself a failure, and that avoids the trap of praising her natural talent.","key_concepts":["Michael Jordan, cut from his high school varsity team, was devastated and responded by leaving the house at six in the morning to practice.","Jordan constantly worked on his weaknesses, and coach John Bach called him a genius who constantly wants to upgrade his genius.","Jackie Joyner-Kersee says there is something about seeing herself improve that motivates and excites her.","Finding #1 in the chapter: those with a growth mindset found success in doing their best, in learning, and in improving.","Mia Hamm says mental toughness is the most important thing for a soccer player and that it has to be learned.","Jordan said the mental toughness and the heart are a lot stronger than some of the physical advantages you might have.","The chapter says the growth mindset helps people cope well with setbacks and leads them to act in their best interest.","Fixed-mindset naturals, carried away with their superiority, do not learn how to work hard or cope with setbacks.","Finding #2 in the chapter: those with a growth mindset found setbacks motivating and informative, a wake-up call.","After a disappointing Masters, Tiger Woods was proud of hanging in there, showing pride in effort rather than outcome."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the growth-mindset coaching move of treating a poor performance as information and a wake-up call rather than as a label on ability. Dweck grounds it in champions like Jordan and Joyner-Kersee, who responded to setbacks with renewed process-focused work, and warns that praised naturals can fall into the fixed mindset that leaves them unable to cope with failure.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Maya, the most naturally gifted midfielder on the district-champion varsity soccer team, just lost the final without scoring. Coach Delgado finds her alone on the bench in the emptying stadium. The goal is to stop her spiral into self-labeling and keep her learning from the loss; the stakes are her motivation and whether she trains over the summer.","dialogue":["Coach Delgado: Maya, sit down. You have mud on your cleats and a look that says the world ended.","Maya: Coach, I choked. Everyone expected me to score and I did not touch the net. I let the team down.","Coach Delgado: You did not choke. You took three shots on frame and tracked back to defend every time. What did you see when the ball was at your feet on the top of the box?","Maya: Just the goalkeeper. I panicked and rushed the shot.","Coach Delgado: So what do you do with that?","Maya: I do not know. I just know I should have been better."],"choices":["Maya, their center back scouted you. Let's watch tonight's film together and find the two runs where the keeper left the near post open. That is where your next goal lives.","Maya, do not worry. You are the most talented player on this team, and talent like yours shows up next time on its own. Just relax.","Maya, you need to own this. A real star finishes those chances. Pull yourself together.","Maya, some players are born clutch and some are not. Let's just keep you healthy for next season."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The growth mindset and taking charge of the process: failure is treated as information and a wake-up call, not a label on your ability.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 works because it converts the missed chances into concrete, learnable material, film study and specific runs, which is the growth-mindset move of taking charge of the processes that bring success. Choice 1 backfires because it praises fixed talent and tells her her ability will rescue her without effort, the very mindset that leaves naturals unable to cope with setbacks. Choice 2 labels her a failure for choking, which is the fixed mindset's verdict style. Choice 3 treats clutch performance as an innate trait, denying Mia Hamm's lesson that mental toughness must be learned."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Natural Who Hits a Wall","visual_frame_description":"In a dim tunnel beneath a minor-league ballpark, Andre, a 24-year-old shortstop in full uniform, sits on a folding chair still in his batting gloves. Veteran utility player Rosa stands against the cinder-block wall, arms crossed, watching him. Empty Gatorade cups and a broken bat lie nearby, and the player's response options glow as dialogue bubbles.","player_mission":"Play Rosa and talk the slumping prodigy Andre out of the fixed-mindset conviction that asking for help would prove he is not special. Choose the reply that opens him to the process the way watching Lenny Dykstra opened Billy Beane's eyes.","key_concepts":["Billy Beane was a natural that everyone agreed was the next Babe Ruth, but he lacked the mindset of a champion.","Beane did not like to fail and, as one scout said, was of the opinion that he should never make an out.","In the fixed mindset, natural talent should not need effort; asking for help is an admission of weakness.","Beane watched Lenny Dykstra, who had no concept of failure, and realized that mindset matters more than talent.","Beane came to believe that scoring runs was much more about process than about talent.","As general manager of the 2002 Oakland Athletics, Beane led the team to 103 victories with the second-lowest payroll in baseball.","The naturals, carried away with their superiority, do not learn how to work hard or how to cope with setbacks.","As it was for Billy Beane and John McEnroe, praise for talent can be a curse that pushes athletes into a fixed mindset.","Pedro Martinez, like Beane, could not tolerate frustration and could not admit his faults and learn from them.","The chapter notes that in the fixed mindset the very idea of deficiencies is terrifying."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the fixed-mindset belief that a natural should not need effort and that asking for help is weakness, the belief that trapped Billy Beane the player. Dweck shows the turnaround came when Beane watched Lenny Dykstra, a lesser talent with no concept of failure, and concluded that mindset matters more than talent and that baseball is about process.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Andre, a double-A shortstop called a natural since high school, is spiraling after his first slump. Veteran teammate Rosa pulls him aside after a three-strikeout night. The goal is to break his conviction that working hard would prove he is not special; the stakes are his spot on the roster and his career.","dialogue":["Rosa: You struck out three times in the fifth and then fell apart after that double play you booted. What was going through your head?","Andre: I should never make an out. The scouts said I am the next big thing. If I need to drill like these grinders, what does that say about me?","Rosa: It says you are a player. I remember a guy, Lenny, who had a tenth of your gifts. He would walk off the field after a terrible at-bat and be laughing. He had no concept of failure.","Andre: Lenny was not judged the way I am. Everyone watches me. If I ask for help, it is an admission that I am not special.","Rosa: So you would rather get sent down than ask for help?","Andre: Maybe. Or maybe I quit first, so nobody gets to watch me fall."],"choices":["I would rather get sent down than ask for help. At least nobody sees me beg.","Maybe you are right. What does a player actually do the week after a season like this?","If I am not special, then I am nobody. I cannot accept that.","You do not get it. Grinders practice; naturals just play."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"The growth-mindset shift that watching Lenny Dykstra triggered in Billy Beane: mindset matters more than talent, and failure is not a verdict.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 works because it is the first step out of the trap, opening Andre to the idea that there is a process a real player uses, the way Beane came to see that baseball was about process more than talent. Choice 0 clings to the fixed belief that asking for help is weakness, the belief that kept Beane the player trapped. Choice 2 is the somebody-nobody syndrome, measuring worth by specialness rather than effort. Choice 3 preserves the myth that naturals need no work, exactly the belief the chapter argues destroys careers."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"A Parent's Words After a Youth Game","visual_frame_description":"On the drive home from a rec-league basketball game, ten-year-old Liam slumps in the passenger seat, knee pads still on, staring out the window. His mother Dana keeps her eyes on the road but glances over at him as the dialogue bubbles appear. A booster club banner for the Lions team flashes past the window as she decides what to say.","player_mission":"Play Dana and respond to Liam after he scored only four points. Choose the reply that supports learning and improvement instead of the talent praise that pushes kids into a fixed mindset.","key_concepts":["The chapter warns that with all the praise for their talent and with how little they have needed to work, naturals can easily fall into a fixed mindset.","Praising talent teaches children that effort is something that casts doubt on their ability rather than a cause for pride.","In the fixed mindset, effort casts doubt on your talent; in the growth mindset, effort is how you improve.","Stuart Biddle's growth-mindset respondents agreed that you need to learn techniques and practice them regularly.","The chapter's growth-mindset champions worked on specific weaknesses, like Jordan working on his defensive game and ball handling.","Candace Parker's father taught her that if you work hard at something, you get out what you put in.","Michael Jordan was cut from his high school varsity team and used the setback as fuel to practice before school.","Wilma Rudolph's recovery from paralysis was a lesson that physical skills could be developed.","The growth mindset leads people to good strategies and to act in their best interest.","Coaches and parents can already hear the mindsets in young athletes, so the message adults send matters."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the difference between talent praise and process feedback in a parent's words to a child. Dweck warns that praising talent can be a curse that lets young naturals fall into a fixed mindset, while pointing children to learnable skills supports the growth mindset that builds real improvement.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Liam, who usually dominates his rec-league games, was held to four points and benched for part of the fourth quarter. His mother Dana wants to say the right thing on the drive home. The goal is to avoid the praise-for-talent trap and support learning; the stakes are Liam's willingness to keep playing and improving.","dialogue":["Liam: I only scored four points. The coach sat me for half the fourth quarter.","Dana: You looked frustrated out there. What happened?","Liam: The tall kid blocked me every time. I am not good at basketball anymore.","Dana: Sweetheart, you are naturally talented. You were born with athletic ability, and that does not just disappear.","Liam: Then why could I not get past him?","Dana: Because he was bigger. You cannot change that."],"choices":["Sweetheart, you are a natural athlete. Your talent will come through again, I promise.","If he is taller, then basketball may not be your sport. Let's not push it.","What did you try the first time he blocked you? Let's think about what you can practice this week so you beat him next time.","You need to try harder. A real competitor does not give up."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Process-focused feedback instead of talent praise; the chapter warns that lavish praise for talent lets naturals fall into a fixed mindset.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 2 works because it treats the block as information and points to a skill to develop, the growth-mindset route to improvement. Choice 0 is the classic talent praise that, as the chapter notes about praised naturals, leaves children needing to protect their label and vulnerable when it is tested. Choice 1 treats ability as fixed and steers him away from challenge. Choice 3 piles on shame without any strategy, the fixed-mindset message that failure is a character verdict."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Star Who Is Asked to Change Positions","visual_frame_description":"In a front-office conference room overlooking a practice court, franchise center Marcus, in a tailored suit, sits across from general manager Dani. A whiteboard behind them shows a new offensive alignment with Malik at the high post. Outside the glass, players run drills, and Marcus's chair is turned slightly away from the board as the dialogue options appear.","player_mission":"Play general manager Dani and convince franchise center Marcus to move to power forward for the team. Choose the reply that offers development and role-filling instead of validating his fixed superstar identity.","key_concepts":["Patrick Ewing was asked to play power forward for the Knicks, was not happy because center is the star position, and his Knicks never won a championship.","Alex Rodriguez agreed to retrain as third base when he joined the Yankees, even though he was then the best shortstop in baseball.","John Wooden said he could have made Kareem the greatest scorer in college history, but the team would not have won three national championships.","Michael Jordan: a superstar's talent can win games, but it is teamwork that wins championships.","Keyshawn Johnson declared he was a team player but an individual first, and was traded by the Jets and deactivated by the Buccaneers.","In the fixed mindset, athletes want to validate their talent, which means acting like a superstar rather than just a team member.","Ewing may not have been sure he could hit the outside shots a power forward has to hit, so he never gave his all to learning the position.","The 2004 Red Sox put their prima donnas on notice with the message: this is a team, not a bunch of stars.","Coach Wooden got players to fill roles as part of a team, which is a main reason he won ten national championships.","Diana Nyad's solo record swim actually took fifty-one other people, showing that every sport is a team sport."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests whether a star can adopt the growth mindset's willingness to develop new skills and fill a role, versus the fixed mindset's need to validate a superstar identity. Dweck contrasts Ewing, who refused to learn power forward and whose Knicks never won, with Alex Rodriguez, who retrained at third base, and with Wooden's handling of Kareem.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Dani, general manager of a professional basketball franchise, asks her franchise center Marcus to move to power forward so the team can run a new system around guard Malik. Marcus has built his brand as the star center. The goal is to help him choose development and the team over validating his position; the stakes are the championship window and his willingness to retrain.","dialogue":["Dani: Marcus, you are the best low-post scorer in the league, so I will be straight with you. Next season we want you at the four.","Marcus: Power forward? Center is the star position. I have built my whole game and my brand on being the franchise big man.","Dani: And you would still be the franchise. But at the four, Malik runs the high post, and our analytics say we win twelve more games.","Marcus: I am not sure I can hit those outside shots. What if I fail and everyone sees I am not what I was?","Dani: Then we work the outside shots every day until you are confident. Remember how Alex Rodriguez agreed to play third base when he joined the Yankees?","Marcus: He was a shortstop, not a franchise center. The whole city expects me to dominate in the paint."],"choices":["Then we work the outside shots every day until you are confident. Remember how Alex Rodriguez agreed to play third base when he joined the Yankees?","Fine. You will stay center for the TV contracts, and I will quietly run Malik at the four in practice.","Honestly, if you do not think you can do it, maybe you are right to be scared. We will keep you at center.","I cannot promise you playing time. Prove you are still worth your contract."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The growth mindset's willingness to develop new skills and fill a role for the team, versus the fixed mindset's need to validate a fixed, superstar identity.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 works because it offers development instead of a verdict and invokes Alex Rodriguez, who retrained and succeeded by giving up his old position for the team, unlike Patrick Ewing, whose refusal to learn power forward cost his Knicks a championship. Choice 1 lets ego rule under a dishonest cover and would fracture the team. Choice 2 validates the fixed belief that he cannot change, guaranteeing the failure he fears. Choice 3 is a threat that reads as a judgment of his worth rather than an invitation to grow."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Coming Back From Injury","visual_frame_description":"In an empty training gym at dusk, professional guard Priya, in a brace and practice jersey, stands at the free-throw line gripping a basketball. Her trainer Sam sits on a weight bench a few feet away holding a clipboard. Loose tape and resistance bands lie on the floor, and the scoreboard reads months since surgery as the dialogue options appear.","player_mission":"Play trainer Sam and help Priya take charge of her comeback from ACL surgery. Choose the reply that frames her recovery as a controllable process of daily work rather than a test of whether her talent survived the injury.","key_concepts":["When Candace Parker returned from knee surgery, she knew she needed to work on her timing, nerves, and wind.","When Parker's three-point shot went bad, she asked her father to come to the gym to work on it with her.","Candace Parker says whether in basketball or everyday life, nothing is promised.","Parker's father taught her that if you work hard at something, you get out what you put in.","Jackie Joyner-Kersee talked herself through an asthma attack by instructing herself to keep pumping her arms until she won.","Joyner-Kersee's sixth-jump bronze came because she had collected all her pains and turned them into one mighty performance.","Finding #3 in the chapter: people with a growth mindset take charge of the processes that bring success and maintain it.","Michael Jordan, losing stamina with age, compensated by working even harder on conditioning and his moves.","Tiger Woods managed his motivation by making his practice into fun and proving to himself that he could hit a certain shot on command.","The chapter says great athletes do not stay on top by trusting talent alone; character is what keeps you there."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the growth mindset's take-charge approach to rebuilding after injury, the chapter's third finding. Dweck shows it through Candace Parker, who returned from knee surgery knowing she had to rebuild her timing, nerves, and wind, and through Joyner-Kersee, who turned accumulated pain into performance rather than treating setbacks as verdicts.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Priya, a professional basketball guard, is cleared for contact practice eight months after ACL surgery. In the empty gym with her trainer Sam, she worries about her timing, nerves, and wind and about whether she will come back the same player. The goal is to help her take charge of her comeback instead of treating her talent as something the injury either preserved or destroyed.","dialogue":["Priya: Coach cleared me for contact next week. But honestly, Sam, I am not the same player. My first step is gone.","Sam: Let's not decide that right now. What did you need to work on before, when you were at your best?","Priya: Timing, mostly. And nerves. My wind used to be fine, but it is gone.","Sam: Good. That is a list. Candace Parker came back from knee surgery with exactly that list, timing, nerves, and wind, and she worked each one.","Priya: What if I come back and I am just average? Everyone will say I was never that good.","Sam: Nothing is promised. That is exactly why you put the work in."],"choices":["Everyone will say I was never that good. I do not know if I can handle that.","Nothing is promised. That is exactly why you put the work in.","You were a natural, and injury cannot take that away. Just trust your talent.","If you cannot come back at full strength, maybe it is time to think about retiring."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Taking charge of the process of success, the chapter's third finding, which Joyner-Kersee and Candace Parker embody.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 works because it reframes the comeback as a controllable process of daily work, echoing Candace Parker's belief that nothing is promised and that you get out what you put in. Choice 0 is the fixed-mindset fear that a poor comeback would prove she was never good, a verdict-style view of ability. Choice 2 relies on preserved talent to carry her, the natural's trap that keeps athletes from rebuilding. Choice 3 abandons the process entirely and treats her current form as a permanent label."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Defeating the Fixed Mindset of a Natural","visual_frame_description":"The battle scene opens in a minor-league dugout at dusk. Marco, a 21-year-old phenom, slams his batting helmet into the bench after striking out three times. A scout behind the screen mutters to a coach, while a grinning veteran shrugs off his own bad at-bat. Storm clouds roll over the outfield as the stage-one question locks onto the screen.","player_mission":"First diagnose the fixed-mindset belief driving Marco's meltdown, then deliver the single intervention that breaks the trap the way watching Lenny Dykstra broke it for Billy Beane. Survive the battle by choosing the growth-mindset read and the growth-mindset intervention.","key_concepts":["Billy Beane was a natural that everyone agreed was the next Babe Ruth, but he lacked the mindset of a champion.","When things went wrong, Beane searched for something to break; as Michael Lewis wrote, it was as if he did not know how to fail.","In the fixed mindset, natural talent does not ask for help because help is an admission of weakness.","Beane never analyzed his deficiencies to coach or practice them away, because the very idea of deficiencies was terrifying.","Lenny Dykstra had no concept of failure, and watching him showed Beane that mindset matters more than talent.","As general manager of the 2002 Oakland Athletics, Beane bought mindset rather than talent and led the team to 103 victories.","As it was for Beane and McEnroe, praise for talent can be a curse that pushes naturals into a fixed mindset.","Pedro Martinez, the dazzling but over-pampered pitcher, could not tolerate frustration or admit his faults.","The chapter says the fixed mindset leaves athletes enmeshed in the idea of their own talent and specialness.","The 2004 Red Sox showed that character, which grows out of mindset, can be learned."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the chapter's central lesson that talent alone does not make a champion and that the fixed mindset, with its belief that natural talent should not need effort, traps even the most gifted. Dweck's evidence is Billy Beane, trapped by his talent until he realized mindset matters more than talent, and the praise-for-talent curse that lets naturals fall into fixed thinking.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is the fixed mindset itself, wearing the face of a gifted young athlete's meltdown. The player must first identify the fixed-mindset belief that makes each strikeout feel like humiliation rather than information, then intervene the way the growth mindset would, using the chapter's Beane-and-Dykstra dynamic as the model.","stage_1_observation":"In a dugout at a minor-league ballpark, Marco, a 21-year-old phenom the organization is calling the next big thing, has just struck out three times. He hunts for something to throw, kicks the dugout bench, and mutters that he should never make an out. Beside him, a veteran teammate shrugs and grins after a bad at-bat, apparently unbothered. A scout behind the screen says to a coach that Marco was of the opinion that he should never make an out, and the player watches Marco's face harden with every botched at-bat.","stage_1_question":"What is the fixed-mindset belief that is driving Marco's meltdown after his strikeouts?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The fixed-mindset belief that natural talent should not need effort, so analyzing his deficiencies or asking for help would be an admission that he is not special; the very idea of a deficiency is terrifying to him, which turns each strikeout into humiliation rather than information.","stage_2_intervention":"After the game, Marco hurls his batting helmet at the dugout wall and tells the coach he is quitting because everyone was wrong about him. You have one conversation in the tunnel under the stands to break the trap the way watching Lenny Dykstra broke it for Billy Beane. What do you say?","stage_2_correct_answer":"You tell Marco the truth of what you watched: that a teammate with a fraction of his gifts has no concept of failure and shrugs off bad at-bats, that mindset matters more than talent, and that players who make it treat each strikeout as information and work on the specific weakness behind it; then you offer to start tomorrow by breaking down the at-bats he choked on, so he learns to see the game as a process rather than a test of his specialness."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Mind of a Champion in the Clutch","visual_frame_description":"Centre Court at Wimbledon renders in muted green light: Pete Sampras sits on the changeover bench down 4-1 in the second-set tiebreaker, towel over his head, clearly searching his memory. Across the net, a younger Patrick Rafter paces. The stage-one question overlays the scene, and a half-time locker room fades in behind it for the second stage.","player_mission":"First read the champion's strategy: identify the frame of reference Sampras uses on the changeover and why it rescues him. Then apply the same growth-mindset logic to a live team crisis by choosing the intervention that turns do-or-die pressure into a recoverable process.","key_concepts":["At Wimbledon 2000, Pete Sampras lost the first set to Patrick Rafter and was down 4-1 in the second-set tiebreaker.","Sampras searched for a frame of reference that could carry him through, remembering past matches where he lost the first set and won the next three.","Billie Jean King's Eureka: a champion is someone who raises their level of play when they need to, getting around three times tougher.","Jackie Joyner-Kersee talked herself through an asthma attack, instructing herself to keep pumping her arms until she won.","Jack Nicklaus, in do-or-die situations across his career, missed only one must-have shot.","The sportswriters defined character as the ability to dig down and find the strength even when things are going against you.","Character grows out of mindset, and the growth mindset helps people cope well with setbacks.","Mia Hamm says mental toughness is not innate but is one of the most difficult aspects of soccer to learn.","John Wooden said ability can get you to the top, but it takes character to keep you there.","The chapter says champions do not think they were born with the right to win; they work hard and stretch beyond ordinary abilities."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the mind of a champion, the growth-mindset ability to dig down and win when things are not quite right by treating setbacks as information and past comebacks as a frame of reference. Dweck grounds it in Sampras's comeback at Wimbledon 2000, King's discovery that champions get around three times tougher, and Nicklaus's single missed must-have shot.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The boss is do-or-die pressure. The battle tests whether the player understands that the growth mindset's reliability in the clutch comes from using setbacks as information and past experience as a frame of reference, the strategy that carried Sampras through and that King identified when she learned what a champion really is.","stage_1_observation":"Centre Court, Wimbledon 2000. Pete Sampras has lost the first set to the younger, harder-hitting Patrick Rafter and is down 4-1 in the second-set tiebreaker. On the changeover he sits with his towel, and instead of cursing his own bad shots he is clearly searching his memory, flipping through past matches. Even he had said he really felt like it was slipping away. William Rhoden notes that Sampras searched for a frame of reference that could carry him through. He stands up calm, wins a five-point run, then two more, and takes the set.","stage_1_question":"What frame of reference does Sampras deliberately search for on the changeover, and why does it help him?","stage_1_correct_answer":"He searches his memory for past matches where he lost the first set and came back to win the next three, telling himself there is time and that he has gotten through exactly this situation before; the frame of reference carries him because it treats the bad set as a recoverable stage rather than a verdict on his ability.","stage_2_intervention":"Two nights later, your own team is in the finals and down big at half-time. Your star guard keeps repeating that the game is over, and a couple of players are arguing about whose fault the first half was. Billie Jean King's Eureka was that a champion is someone who raises their level of play when they need to, someone who gets around three times tougher when the match is on the line. What do you do in the huddle?","stage_2_correct_answer":"You cut off the blame by reframing the deficit as information, recall a past comeback your team managed from a similar hole to show there is time, and set one concrete adjustment for the very next possession, telling the team that the mark of a champion is raising your level when things are not right rather than getting three times softer."}]},{"id":"ch10","title":"Chapter 5: Business: Mindset and Leadership","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Fixed vs. Growth Leadership Behaviors","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled executive boardroom at dusk, with a long walnut table and seven executives seated around it. A stern CEO at the head gestures dismissively at a rejected product sketch while two engineers exchange nervous glances. Along the right edge of the screen sit three labeled columns: Fixed-mindset leader, Growth-mindset leader, and Groupthink dynamic.","player_mission":"Read each behavior card that slides onto the table and sort it into the column it best reflects. Sort all six behaviors, then confirm which column represents the leadership pattern a good-to-great leader would model.","key_concepts":["Fixed-mindset leaders must repeatedly affirm their superiority and treat the company as a platform for proving it.","Growth-mindset leaders constantly try to improve, surround themselves with the most able people, and look squarely at their own mistakes.","Jim Collins's good-to-great leaders were self-effacing, asked questions, and could confront the most brutal answers while keeping faith they would succeed.","Alan Wurtzel of Circuit City held boardroom debates, pressing with 'Why, why, why?' until he understood the full picture.","Jack Welch went directly to front-line employees in factories like the Louisville refrigerator plant to find out what was really going on.","Lee Iacocca silenced critics, declaring the rounder cars looked like flying potatoes, and that ended the debate.","Irving Janis popularized groupthink: everyone in a group starts thinking alike and no one takes a critical stance.","Groupthink can arise when people put unlimited faith in a talented leader who is seen as a genius that never errs.","In Robert Wood's group study, growth-mindset groups stated honest opinions and disagreements while fixed-mindset groups fell into groupthink.","A company that cannot self-correct cannot thrive, and the fixed mindset blocks self-correction.","Rich Kinder, the only top Enron executive asking 'Are we smoking our own dope?', had his days numbered.","Morgan McCall noted people often like the things that work against their growth, avoiding criticism and new skills."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the fixed-versus-growth leadership distinction: fixed-mindset leaders suppress dissent and defend superiority, while growth-mindset leaders invite questions, feedback, and self-correction. Dweck grounds this in Jim Collins's Good to Great findings and the Wood group study, where growth-mindset groups openly disagreed while fixed-mindset groups slipped into groupthink.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Sort each executive behavior into the column it reflects, then choose the column that a good-to-great leader would model.","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset leader","column_b_label":"Growth-mindset leader","column_c_label":"Groupthink dynamic","rows":[{"label":"Rejects the new car design the team proposed, declaring the rounder cars 'look like flying potatoes' and closing the debate.","col":"a"},{"label":"Runs boardroom debates, pressing with 'Why, why, why?' until the full picture emerges, as Alan Wurtzel did at Circuit City.","col":"b"},{"label":"Advisors suspend their judgment because they believe the leader has the Midas touch and cannot lose.","col":"c"},{"label":"Visits the factory floor and asks assembly-line workers directly what is going on, as Jack Welch did in Louisville.","col":"b"},{"label":"Fires executives who question whether the company is fooling itself.","col":"a"},{"label":"Assigns someone to play devil's advocate so the team can see the holes in its own plan.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Sorting Wurtzel's boardroom debates into the fixed column because a prosecutor is aggressive.","Sorting the devil's advocate row into the groupthink column because any assigned role signals conformity.","Sorting Welch's factory visits into the fixed column because he was a larger-than-life figure with an ego he held in check."],"explanation":"The rows capture the chapter's contrast: fixed-mindset leaders suppress dissent and claim superiority (Iacocca's 'flying potatoes' and the firing of dissenting executives), while growth-mindset leaders like Wurtzel and Welch question, listen, and invite challenge to improve. Groupthink is the collective silence that results when faith in a genius leader or fear of disapproval suppresses independent judgment, which the Wood group study linked to fixed mindsets. The growth behaviors are the hallmark of Jim Collins's good-to-great leaders."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Organizational Mindsets: Culture of Genius vs. Culture of Development","visual_frame_description":"A muted office lobby where a wall screen cycles through anonymized employee-survey responses from a study of large corporations. On the left is a column labeled Culture of genius, on the right Culture of development, and in the center Groupthink posture. A row of employee avatars watches the player sort each survey statement into its home column.","player_mission":"Sort each employee-survey statement into the organizational mindset it reveals, then identify which column Dweck's study shows produces trust, innovation, and ethical behavior.","key_concepts":["An organization as a whole can hold a pervasive belief that talent is just fixed or that talent can be developed in all employees.","Dweck's team labeled fixed-mindset companies a 'culture of genius' and growth-mindset companies a 'culture of development.'","Employees in growth-mindset companies report far more trust in the company and a stronger sense of empowerment and ownership.","Employees in fixed-mindset companies express a greater interest in leaving for another company.","Employees in growth-mindset companies say their organization supports reasonable risk-taking, innovation, and creativity.","Employees in fixed-mindset companies report more cheating, taking shortcuts, cutting corners, and hiding information.","Supervisors in growth-mindset companies rate employees as more collaborative, committed to learning, innovative, and higher in management potential.","Dweck notes the irony that fixed-mindset companies hire and reward talent, then find themselves asking 'Where's the talent?' because it is not flourishing.","An organization's mindset shapes ethical behavior, not just performance.","Presenting skills as learnable and managers as resources for learning are elements of a growth-mindset environment."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests whether organizational mindset, a culture of genius versus a culture of development, shapes employee trust, commitment, innovation, and ethics. Dweck found that employees converge on a shared belief about talent that predicts risk-taking, ethical behavior, and whether talent flourishes.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Sort each employee-survey statement into the organizational mindset it reveals, then choose the column that Dweck's study shows produces trust, innovation, and ethical behavior.","column_a_label":"Culture of genius (fixed)","column_b_label":"Culture of development (growth)","column_c_label":"Groupthink posture","rows":[{"label":"'When it comes to being successful, this company seems to believe that people have a certain amount of talent and they can't really do much to change it.'","col":"a"},{"label":"'This company genuinely values the personal development and growth of its employees.'","col":"b"},{"label":"'In this company there is a lot of cheating, taking shortcuts, and cutting corners.'","col":"a"},{"label":"'This company genuinely supports risk-taking and will support me even if I fail.'","col":"b"},{"label":"'We got to the point where we thought we were bulletproof.'","col":"c"},{"label":"'This company values natural intelligence and business talent more than any other characteristics.'","col":"a"},{"label":"'People are encouraged to be innovative in this company; creativity is welcomed.'","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Classifying the 'cheating, taking shortcuts' statement as growth because growth implies flexibility.","Treating 'we thought we were bulletproof' as a sign of healthy confidence rather than groupthink.","Placing 'values natural intelligence and business talent' in the development column because talent is a plus."],"explanation":"Dweck asked diverse employee samples whether their company believed talent was fixed or developable and found strong within-company consensus forming a culture of genius or a culture of development. Employees in growth-mindset companies reported more trust, ownership, commitment, and support for risk-taking and innovation, while employees in fixed-mindset companies reported more cheating, secret-keeping, and a desire to leave. The 'we were bulletproof' row captures the Enron-style belief that follows when a group gets carried away with its brilliance."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Groupthink and the Dissent That Prevents It","visual_frame_description":"A dim decision room modeled on the Bay of Pigs era: advisers seated in a semicircle around a confident leader while a ticker of historical episodes scrolls along the top. Three columns are open beneath: Groupthink symptom, Anti-groupthink mechanism, and Fixed-mindset suppression of dissent. A clock in the corner suggests a catastrophic decision is imminent.","player_mission":"Sort each decision-making episode into the dynamic it illustrates, then identify which dynamic Dweck warns can lead to catastrophic decisions.","key_concepts":["Irving Janis popularized groupthink in the early 1970s: everyone in a group starts thinking alike and no one takes a critical stance.","Groupthink can lead to catastrophic decisions, as in the disastrous Bay of Pigs invasion.","Kennedy's advisers suspended their judgment because they had unbounded faith in his ability and luck.","Arthur Schlesinger noted that had one senior advisor opposed the adventure, Kennedy would likely have canceled it.","Churchill created a special department whose job was to give him all the worst news so he would not be groupthinked into false security.","Alfred P. Sloan, CEO of General Motors, proposed postponing a unanimous decision to give the group time to develop disagreement.","Herodotus reported that the ancient Persians reconsidered their sober decisions while intoxicated to prevent groupthink.","Groupthink can happen when a fixed-mindset leader punishes dissent; people may not stop thinking critically but they stop speaking up.","David Packard gave an engineer a medal for 'extraordinary contempt and defiance beyond the normal call of engineering duty.'","Robert Wood's study showed growth-mindset groups stated honest opinions and disagreements, leading to fuller discussion and better decisions."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests groupthink, the failure of a group to take a critical stance because of faith in a genius leader, collective brilliance, or fear of dissent. Dweck argues the fixed mindset breeds groupthink, while the growth mindset, by relieving people of fixed-ability burdens, leads to open discussion and enhanced decision making.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Sort each decision-making episode into the dynamic it illustrates, then identify which dynamic Dweck warns can lead to catastrophic decisions.","column_a_label":"Groupthink symptom","column_b_label":"Anti-groupthink mechanism","column_c_label":"Fixed-mindset suppression of dissent","rows":[{"label":"Kennedy's advisers suspended their judgment because they believed he had the Midas touch (Bay of Pigs).","col":"a"},{"label":"Churchill set up a special department whose job was to give him all the worst news.","col":"b"},{"label":"Alfred P. Sloan proposed postponing a unanimous decision to 'develop disagreement.'","col":"b"},{"label":"Iacocca insisted the rounder cars looked like flying potatoes and allowed no difference of opinion.","col":"c"},{"label":"An outside consultant asked Enron executives where they were vulnerable; nobody answered and nobody even understood the question.","col":"a"},{"label":"David Packard gave an engineer a medal for defying orders and persisting with his display monitor.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Calling Churchill's worst-news department a groupthink symptom because it sounds morbid.","Sorting Packard's medal into the fixed column because defiance broke the chain of command.","Reading the Bay of Pigs silence as sound loyalty rather than suspended judgment."],"explanation":"Groupthink, popularized by Irving Janis, is everyone thinking alike with no critical stance, and the Wood study linked it to fixed mindsets. The Bay of Pigs and Enron's 'bulletproof' belief are classic symptoms, while Sloan's postponed consensus, Churchill's worst-news department, and Packard's medal for defiance are deliberate mechanisms to force dissent. Iacocca's punishing of critics shows fixed-mindset suppression, the third column."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Talent Praise vs. Process Praise in the Workplace","visual_frame_description":"A bright open-plan office on a Monday morning, with a corkboard of award certificates labeled Employee of the Day and a sticky-note wall of thank-you messages. A manager's review screen shows three columns: Talent praise, Process praise, and Overpraise dependence. Speech bubbles drift from employee avatars as the player sorts each piece of feedback.","player_mission":"Sort each piece of workplace feedback into the kind of praise it represents, then choose the kind of praise Dweck's research shows builds hardiness and a growth mindset.","key_concepts":["The children of the praise-for-intelligence era have entered the workforce needing a sticker for every move.","Some companies give quarterly or monthly bonuses and 'employee of the day' awards to lavish rewards on the overpraised generation.","Constant reassurance and an inability to take criticism are not a recipe for success in business, where persistence and correcting mistakes are essential.","Praising a brilliant performance or the smartest idea reinforces the fixed mindset.","The right kind of praise rewards initiative, seeing a difficult task through, struggling and learning something new, and being undaunted by setbacks.","With the right kinds of feedback, even adults can be motivated to choose challenging tasks and confront their mistakes.","Praise for acting on criticism builds hardiness rather than fragility.","Dweck suggests it could even be praise for not needing constant praise.","The wrong kinds of praise lead children down a path of entitlement, dependence, and fragility.","Businesses may need to help develop a more mature and growth-minded workforce if the leaders of the future are to emerge."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the distinction between talent praise and process praise in the workplace. Dweck's research shows praising intelligence pushes people into the fixed mindset, making them dependent on reassurance and afraid of criticism, while praise for process and for acting on feedback builds the hardiness that business success requires.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Sort each piece of feedback into the kind of praise it represents, then choose the kind of praise Dweck's research shows builds hardiness and a growth mindset.","column_a_label":"Talent praise (fixed)","column_b_label":"Process praise (growth)","column_c_label":"Overpraise dependence","rows":[{"label":"'You're the smartest person on this team.'","col":"a"},{"label":"'You stuck with that difficult client through three setbacks and learned something new each time.'","col":"b"},{"label":"An employee who cannot function without a sticker or reward for every move.","col":"c"},{"label":"'Brilliant presentation. You're a natural.'","col":"a"},{"label":"'I admire how you acted on that tough criticism and revised the rollout plan.'","col":"b"},{"label":"'Employee of the day' awards introduced to keep constantly reassuring staff.","col":"c"},{"label":"'I'm proud of how you took the initiative on the stalled account.'","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Classifying 'you're a natural' as process praise because it is positive feedback.","Reading constant reassurance needs as a healthy sign of engagement.","Treating praise for acting on criticism as talent praise because it addresses the person directly."],"explanation":"The chapter warns that praise for intelligence and talent, the praise that created the praised generation, pushes people toward the fixed mindset where they need constant reassurance and cannot take criticism. Process praise for initiative, persistence, learning, and acting on criticism motivates people to choose challenging tasks and confront mistakes. The 'sticker for every move' pattern is the corporate extension of overpraising that firms now pay consultants to reproduce."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Mindset and Negotiation Performance","visual_frame_description":"A conference room with two negotiators across a table, a printed employment contract between them with eight highlighted issues from salary to benefits. Three columns hover beside the table: Fixed negotiation mindset, Growth negotiation mindset, and Integrative win-win outcome. A results ticker at the bottom tracks the negotiation's value for each side.","player_mission":"Sort each negotiation choice and outcome into the mindset it reflects, then identify which column best predicts negotiation success in Laura Kray and Michael Haselhuhn's studies.","key_concepts":["Laura Kray and Michael Haselhuhn showed that mindsets have an important impact on negotiation success.","Participants taught a fixed mindset about negotiation skills chose tasks that showed off their skills without teaching anything new.","Almost half, 47 percent, of fixed-mindset participants chose the show-off task, while only 12 percent of growth-mindset participants did.","In live employment negotiations, growth-mindset negotiators did almost twice as well as those with a fixed mindset.","Growth-mindset negotiators persevered through the rough spots and stalemates to gain more favorable outcomes.","Growth-mindset negotiators were able to construct deals that addressed both parties' underlying interests.","Growth-mindset negotiators came up with more creative solutions that conferred benefits all around.","The more of a growth mindset an MBA student endorsed, the better he or she did on the negotiation task.","MBA students with a growth mindset on the first day earned higher final grades in the negotiation course.","A fixed mindset about negotiation holds that the kind of negotiator someone is cannot be changed very much."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests how implicit theories of ability shape negotiation behavior. Kray and Haselhuhn showed that a growth mindset about negotiation leads people to choose learning tasks, persevere through stalemates, and reach more favorable and more creative agreements that serve both sides.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Sort each negotiation choice and outcome into the mindset it reflects, then choose the column that best predicts negotiation success in Kray and Haselhuhn's studies.","column_a_label":"Fixed negotiation mindset","column_b_label":"Growth negotiation mindset","column_c_label":"Integrative (win-win) outcome","rows":[{"label":"Choosing a task that shows off your negotiation skills but teaches you nothing new.","col":"a"},{"label":"Choosing a task where you might make mistakes but would learn useful negotiation skills.","col":"b"},{"label":"Persevering through stalemates to negotiate almost twice as favorable an employment deal.","col":"b"},{"label":"Believing that 'good negotiators are born that way.'","col":"a"},{"label":"Constructing a deal that addresses both parties' underlying interests after initial failure.","col":"c"},{"label":"An MBA student who endorses a growth mindset on day one of a negotiation course earning a higher final grade.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Calling the show-off task choice growth because it showcases confidence.","Treating an integrative deal as fixed because both sides compromised.","Reading perseverance through stalemates as stubbornness rather than learning."],"explanation":"Kray and Haselhuhn found that teaching a fixed mindset about negotiation, through an article titled 'Negotiation Ability, Like Plaster, Is Pretty Stable Over Time,' made 47 percent of participants choose the show-off task, versus only 12 percent after a growth mindset message. In live employment negotiations, growth-mindset negotiators did almost twice as well, persevered through stalemates, and in harder tasks constructed deals serving both parties' interests. Growth-endorsing MBA students also earned higher final grades."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Giving Process Praise in a Performance Review","visual_frame_description":"A bright, minimalist meeting room in a Seattle software office; Lena sits across from Marcus at a small table, her laptop closed and a spiral notebook between them. Marcus slouches slightly, running a finger along the edge of his notepad, while a window behind them shows the city skyline. The player sits in Lena's seat and must deliver the review.","player_mission":"As Lena, deliver feedback to Marcus about his salvaged rebranding campaign. Choose the response that rewards his learning process and prepares him for harder work, rather than praise that reinforces talent labels.","scenario_setup":"Lena, a 34-year-old product manager, is giving a quarterly performance review to Marcus, a 28-year-old marketing associate. Marcus just salvaged a rebranding campaign that failed twice in front of clients before he finally turned it around. Marcus has been praised his whole life as the smart one, and Lena can see he is anxious about being judged. Her goal is to keep him taking hard assignments and taking criticism well.","dialogue":["Lena: Before we start, Marcus, I want you to know this review is about what happened this quarter, not about who you are.","Marcus: Thanks. Honestly, I'm still a little raw about the two failed pitches. I feel like I let the team down.","Lena: Let's talk about them. What did you do after the first pitch failed?","Marcus: I went back to the clients' notes, redid the messaging, and asked Priya to role-play the objections with me.","Lena: And after the second failure, when leadership wanted to pull the campaign?","Marcus: I pushed to keep it alive for one more round and revised the whole rollout plan based on what we'd learned.","Lena: That is exactly the behavior I want to reward. You struggled, you learned something new each time, and you acted on the criticism. That tells me more than any smart-sounding first draft.","Marcus: So the two failures are not going to count against me?","Lena: They count, as evidence that you know how to get up and adjust. I'm putting you on the flagship account next quarter."],"choices":["You're clearly the most talented marketer on this team. You pulled that off on raw ability.","You're so smart, Marcus. You always figure it out eventually.","I want to reward how you handled the setbacks. You sought feedback, revised your strategy, and kept going, so I'm giving you the flagship account.","Good job. But don't let it happen again. We can't afford two more failed pitches."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Process praise (praising effort, strategies, learning, and response to criticism) versus talent praise","psychological_breakdown":"Option 2 is optimal because it praises the process: initiative, learning from failure, and acting on criticism, which Dweck's research shows builds a growth mindset and hardiness for future challenges. Options 0 and 1 are talent praise, which feels great but pushes Marcus into the fixed mindset, making him dependent on reassurance and afraid of future failure. Option 3 withholds credit for the learning and reinforces fear of judgment, the opposite of the growth-promoting feedback the chapter recommends.","key_concepts":["Praising intelligence and talent pushes people toward the fixed mindset.","Process praise, for initiative, persistence, and learning, builds a growth mindset.","Praise for acting on criticism helps employees confront their mistakes.","With the right kinds of feedback, even adults can be motivated to choose challenging tasks.","Employees who were overpraised as children often need constant reassurance and cannot take criticism.","A growth-promoting workplace rewards struggle and learning rather than ready-made genius.","Businesses may need to play a role in developing a more mature and growth-minded workforce.","Growth-minded managers act as guides, not judges.","Feedback that promotes learning and future success is a hallmark of a growth-mindset environment.","The praised generation's need for stickers on every move is not a recipe for business success."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests process praise versus talent praise in a performance review. Dweck's research shows that praising the process, the struggle, strategies, and acting on criticism, motivates adults to take on challenges and confront mistakes, while talent praise breeds dependence and fear of judgment.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Coaching a Struggling Performer: Fixed vs. Growth Managers","visual_frame_description":"A regional sales office with a whiteboard of quarterly quotas; Tom, 51, a sales director, leans against his desk while a younger account executive, Ravi, 29, sits across from him clutching a printout of his call notes. A window shows a freight yard below. The player is Tom and must decide how to treat a struggling performer.","player_mission":"As Tom, decide how to handle Ravi after three straight quarters below quota. Choose the response of a manager who believes people can develop, rather than a manager who has already judged Ravi unchangeable.","scenario_setup":"Tom's own manager, Priya, has suggested managing Ravi out after Ravi missed quota for three straight quarters. Tom read the chapter's research on managers who believe people can change and wonders whether coaching could turn Ravi around. His goal is to decide whether a struggling performer deserves development or dismissal.","dialogue":["Tom: Priya keeps telling me Ravi is not a salesperson and I should let him go.","Ravi (from memory): I know I keep missing quota, Tom. I feel like I'm just not cut out for this.","Tom: Let's look at your call notes from last month.","Ravi: I mostly talked about our features and pricing. When clients pushed back, I just dropped the price.","Tom: What would you do differently if the clients pushed back on value instead of price?","Ravi: I'm not sure. I've never really been trained on it.","Tom: That's the thing, you haven't. Let's set up role-play sessions with our top closer, and I'll observe your next five pitches and give you specific feedback.","Ravi: You're not going to fire me?","Tom: Not yet. Talent is just the starting point. I want to see what you do with training and feedback.","Ravi: I'll put in the work. Thank you."],"choices":["Ravi, you're not a natural salesperson. Let's be realistic about your future here.","I've already made up my mind about you from the first quarter. Let's talk severance.","Talent is just the starting point. I'll coach you, give you feedback, and we'll track whether you improve.","This is your last chance. Hit quota next quarter or you're out, with no handholding."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Implicit theory of ability applied to management: managers who believe people can change give developmental coaching","psychological_breakdown":"Option 2 reflects the growth-mindset manager in the Heslin, VandeWalle, and Latham studies: more developmental coaching, noticing improvement, and welcoming feedback. Options 0 and 1 are fixed-mindset stances, judging employees as competent or incompetent at the start and staying stuck in that initial impression. Option 3 demands results without offering a pathway to develop, treating improvement as a matter of pure will.","key_concepts":["Studies by Peter Heslin, Don VandeWalle, and Gary Latham show many managers do not believe in personal change.","Fixed-mindset managers judge employees as competent or incompetent at the start and remain stuck in that initial impression.","Fixed-mindset managers do relatively little developmental coaching.","Growth-mindset managers give a great deal more developmental coaching and notice improvement in employee performance.","Growth-mindset managers welcome critiques from their employees.","Fixed-mindset managers are less likely to seek or accept critical feedback from employees.","Growth-mindset managers see talent as nice to have but only the starting point.","A brief workshop based on well-established psychological principles can teach managers a growth mindset.","After the workshop, managers more readily detected improvement, were more willing to coach poor performers, and gave more and better coaching suggestions.","Training and experience can often draw out and develop the qualities required for successful performance."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests whether a manager treats ability as fixed or developable. Heslin, VandeWalle, and Latham found that managers with a growth mindset coach more, notice improvement, and welcome feedback, and that these beliefs can be taught in a short workshop.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Dissent and Defiance at Work","visual_frame_description":"A Silicon Valley hardware lab with a prototype monitor glowing on a workbench; Nina, 30, a display engineer, stands between her manager Greg and the prototype while a calendar reads 'Vacation.' In a corner, a framed black-and-white photo shows David Packard pinning a medal on an engineer. The player must decide how Nina responds to an order to shelve her project.","player_mission":"As Nina, decide what to do when your manager orders you to abandon a promising display monitor. Choose the path that mirrors the Hewlett-Packard engineer who earned Packard's medal for defiance.","scenario_setup":"Nina's manager, Greg, ordered the team to shelve a promising monitor project to focus on a cheaper product line. Nina believes the monitor has real demand, but she has seen colleagues who disagreed get sidelined. The chapter's story of David Packard's medal for defiance frames her dilemma: follow orders quietly or gather real customer evidence.","dialogue":["Greg: Nina, I'm killing the monitor project effective immediately. It's a distraction.","Nina: Greg, the customer feedback on the prototype is strong. We already have letters of intent.","Greg: I've decided. The board wants cost down this quarter, and the monitor is a cost sink.","Nina: (to herself) If I drop this, it dies. If I go on vacation and show it to customers, I might get fired, or I might prove the demand.","Greg: Do you understand? I need to hear you say you're done with it.","Nina: I understand you've made a decision. I'll take some time off to think about next steps.","Customer (later, at a trade visit): This monitor solves our exact problem. We'd order two hundred units if you'd build it.","Nina: (returning) Greg, I have purchase interest. Two hundred units from three customers who saw the prototype.","Greg: You went behind my back?!","Nina: I went in front of our customers. The demand is real. Can we reconsider?"],"choices":["Greg, I respect your authority. I'll shut the project down and reallocate the team.","I'll quietly keep working on it at home and present it to you again in six months.","I showed the monitor to potential customers, got real purchase interest, and I'm asking you to reconsider. I'll take responsibility for the risk.","Fine, you're the boss. But I'm documenting that this decision is wrong so history judges you."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"The value of open dissent against groupthink, illustrated by Packard's medal for 'extraordinary contempt and defiance beyond the normal call of engineering duty'","psychological_breakdown":"Option 2 mirrors the Hewlett-Packard engineer who defied the order, gathered real evidence, and brought the company more than seventeen thousand monitor sales and thirty-five million dollars in revenue. Option 0 surrenders to the groupthink pattern in which people stop speaking up even when they think critically. Option 1 hides the disagreement rather than forcing it into the open, and Option 3 turns the conflict into revenge, which serves the ego rather than the company.","key_concepts":["Groupthink can happen when a fixed-mindset leader punishes dissent.","People may not stop thinking critically, but they stop speaking up when dissent is punished.","David Packard gave an engineer a medal 'for extraordinary contempt and defiance beyond the normal call of engineering duty.'","The HP engineer, told to give up a display monitor, went on vacation and showed the prototype to potential customers.","The customers loved the monitor, and the company sold more than seventeen thousand units for thirty-five million dollars in revenue.","Hewlett-Packard's co-founder rewarded the engineer rather than punishing the defiance.","Leaders are sometimes seen as gods who never err, which suppresses dissent.","Workers seeking validation from leaders may fall into line behind them.","A growth mindset leads to a full and open discussion of information and enhanced decision making.","People can be independent thinkers and team players at the same time."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the value of dissent against a groupthink-producing leader. Dweck uses Packard's medal for defiance to show that open disagreement, even at personal risk, produces better results than the silence that fixed-mindset leadership enforces.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Persevering in a Negotiation Stalemate","visual_frame_description":"A modern HR office at a consumer electronics firm; Dana, 33, a senior product designer, sits across from Alex, a recruiter, with a printed offer letter between them and eight checkboxes from salary to benefits listed on a screen. Alex's arms are crossed after declaring the salary band firm. The player is Dana deciding how to respond to a stalemate.","player_mission":"As Dana, respond to the recruiter who says the salary band is firm. Choose the strategy of a growth-mindset negotiator who persists through the stalemate and searches the full set of issues for a deal that serves both sides.","scenario_setup":"Dana is negotiating her job offer with Alex, a recruiter at a large consumer electronics firm. The offer is good but below market, and Dana must negotiate on eight issues including salary, vacation, and benefits. Dana read about mindsets and negotiation and believes her skills can grow with practice, but she has already hit a stalemate on salary.","dialogue":["Alex: I appreciate you pushing back, Dana, but our salary band for this role is firm. Take it or leave it.","Dana: I hear that the number is firm, so help me understand the band. Is there flexibility in the role level or in the timing of performance reviews?","Alex: The level is set, but review timing could move up if that helps bridge the gap.","Dana: What about vacation and the signing bonus? I noticed your benefits package is light on parental leave.","Alex: The signing bonus has some room. Parental leave is standard across the company.","Dana: Then let's trade. I'll accept the salary band if the signing bonus covers the difference and the review moves to six months.","Alex: If I can get that approved, do we have a deal?","Dana: If we also confirm the vacation weeks in writing, yes. I want a deal that works for both of us, and I plan to be here for the long run.","Alex: Let me get the approvals. I think we can make this work."],"choices":["Fine. I'll take the original offer. I don't want to be difficult on my first day.","I'm walking. A recruiter who won't move on salary is wasting my time.","I'll hold firm on salary only. If you can't match my number, this deal is dead.","Let's explore the full set of issues. If salary is fixed, we can find value in the bonus, review timing, and benefits, and build a deal that serves both sides."],"optimal_choice":3,"theoretical_principle":"Growth mindset in negotiation: persevering through stalemates and constructing integrative deals that address both parties' underlying interests","psychological_breakdown":"Option 3 reflects the growth-mindset negotiator in Kray and Haselhuhn's studies: persist through the rough spots and seek creative solutions that benefit both parties rather than fixating on a single issue. Option 0 surrenders value, option 1 abandons the deal at the first obstacle, and option 2 collapses the negotiation onto one dimension, risking a deadlock instead of an integrated agreement.","key_concepts":["Kray and Haselhuhn taught participants either a fixed or a growth mindset about negotiation skills.","In live employment negotiations, growth-mindset negotiators did almost twice as well as fixed-mindset negotiators.","Growth-mindset negotiators persevered through the rough spots and stalemates.","In harder tasks, growth-mindset negotiators constructed deals that addressed both parties' underlying interests.","Growth-mindset negotiators came up with more creative solutions that conferred benefits all around.","MBA students who endorsed growth-mindset statements did better on the negotiation task.","Growth-mindset MBA students earned higher final grades in the negotiation course.","Negotiation skills can be cultivated and developed over a lifetime.","47 percent of fixed-mindset participants chose a show-off task, while only 12 percent of growth-mindset participants did.","A growth mindset in negotiation does not just mean getting one's own way; it also serves the other party's interests."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the growth mindset applied to negotiation. Kray and Haselhuhn showed that negotiators who believe the skill is developable persist through stalemates, win more favorable outcomes, and construct integrative deals that serve both sides.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Welcoming Bad News: CEO Disease vs. Honest Feedback","visual_frame_description":"A corner office at an energy company on the evening of a board review; Viktor, 58, the founder-CEO, stands by a window with the skyline behind him while his COO Maria holds a spreadsheet marked Revised Forecast Downward. A portrait of the CEO hangs on the wall, and a small plaque reads 'Are we smoking our own dope?' The player is Viktor facing genuinely bad news.","player_mission":"As Viktor, respond when your COO brings you genuinely bad news about missing earnings targets. Choose the response of a leader who welcomes the worst news rather than the one who silences critics.","scenario_setup":"Viktor has spent months surrounded by executives who confirm his brilliance, and his company is about to miss its earnings targets. His COO, Maria, is the only one who keeps asking hard questions. The scene parallels Rich Kinder at Enron asking whether the firm was fooling itself, and Churchill's special department that delivered only the worst news.","dialogue":["Maria: Viktor, I have to be direct. We are going to miss Q2 targets, and our pipeline math has been overstated.","Viktor: (coldly) That can't be right. My people told me we were ahead.","Maria: That's exactly why I'm here. Your people are telling you what you want to hear. I'm telling you what the numbers show.","Viktor: Are you sure you're not covering for your own mistakes?","Maria: I'm not covering anything up. I'm the only one in this room who asked if we were fooling ourselves, and the answer is yes.","Viktor: (pause) Tell me the worst part first. I'd rather hear the worst news now than a pleasant lie at the shareholder meeting.","Maria: If we restructure the two weakest divisions and renegotiate the debt, we can survive the quarter and rebuild.","Viktor: Then that's the plan. I want a full, honest accounting from every division head by Friday, and no one gets punished for bringing bad news.","Maria: I'll make the calls."],"choices":["Maria, you're undermining the team. You're dismissed. We'll find someone more loyal.","This is embarrassing. Let's revise the projections so we look on track while we find a fix.","I need to hear this. Give me the worst news first, get a full honest accounting, and never punish anyone for bringing bad news.","You caught this early, so fix it quietly. If the numbers don't work out, I'll distance myself from it."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"CEO disease and the value of honest feedback channels (Churchill's worst-news department and Gerstner's opening of communication)","psychological_breakdown":"Option 2 embodies the growth-minded leader who confronts brutal facts rather than flattery, like Churchill's department that delivered the worst news and Gerstner's memo opening channels of communication up and down IBM. Option 0 is Iacocca's silencing of critics and the fate Enron imposed on Rich Kinder, whose days were numbered precisely because he asked if they were fooling themselves. Option 1 is Enron's self-deception of recording profits before earning revenues, and option 3 hides responsibility while keeping the appearance of control.","key_concepts":["People with the fixed mindset do not admit and correct their deficiencies.","Enron forced its employees to look and act extraordinarily talented, pushing them into the fixed mindset.","Rich Kinder was the only person at the top who constantly asked, 'Are we smoking our own dope?'","Kinder's days were numbered, but he bought the valuable pipelines and built a company worth seven billion dollars.","CEO disease is the fixed-mindset peril where leaders surround themselves with good news despite warning signs.","A company that cannot self-correct cannot thrive.","Churchill set up a special department whose job was to give him all the worst news.","Gerstner sent a memo to every IBM worker six days after arriving, promising to meet and talk about strengthening the company.","Growth-minded leaders look squarely at their own mistakes and ask what skills they and the company will need in the future.","The fixed mindset lets CEOs create a world that caters to their need for validation."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests CEO disease, the fixed-mindset leader's tendency to surround himself with validating good news, against the growth-minded practice of demanding honest feedback. Dweck shows self-correcting companies like those led by Gerstner opened channels for bad news, while Enron silenced the one executive who asked if they were fooling themselves.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Fixed-Mindset CEO and the Growth Turnaround","visual_frame_description":"A fictionalized boardroom on the evening of a quarterly review: a charismatic autocrat stands before a rising stock chart while his top operations officer asks whether the revenue projections are real. Behind the CEO, a wall of Employee of the Day plaques and framed magazine covers signals a company obsessed with looking brilliant, while a finance director in the corner quietly holds a report labeled Revised Forecast Downward.","player_mission":"Diagnose the mindset pattern at work in this boardroom, then choose the signature first move of a new CEO that models what the chapter's growth-minded leaders did to turn companies around.","boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the fixed-mindset CEO's self-defeating pattern, CEO disease, gargantuan ego, blame and cover-up, and what a growth-minded turnaround requires. Dweck shows that Iacocca, Dunlap, Lay, Skilling, Case, and Levin sacrificed their companies to the need to prove superiority, while Welch, Gerstner, and Mulcahy rooted out the fixed mindset and built cultures of growth and teamwork.","stage_1_observation":"The CEO proudly points to a chart of rising stock while his operations officer asks whether the revenue projections are real. The boardroom walls are covered with Employee of the Day plaques and framed magazine covers, and a finance director quietly holds a report labeled Revised Forecast Downward. Everyone in the room except the operations officer seems to be agreeing with the CEO's story.","stage_1_question":"Which pattern is operating in this boardroom, and what is its root cause?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The company has been forced into a fixed mindset: everyone must look and act extraordinarily talented, so warning signs are suppressed and honest numbers are buried. This is the same dynamic that doomed Enron, which worshiped talent and made a flaw intolerable.","stage_2_intervention":"You are the new CEO brought in to save this company. In your first week you must choose a signature move. Which action best models what the chapter's growth-mindset leaders did?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Open the channels of communication: send a message to every employee promising honest discussions, disband the elitist inner circle, ask division heads for the worst news first, and protect rather than punish the officer who flagged the fake projections, exactly as Gerstner, Welch, and Churchill did. Attack the elitism and reward teamwork instead of individual genius.","key_concepts":["Fixed-mindset leaders live in a world where some people are superior and some are inferior, and the company is a platform to prove superiority.","In more than two-thirds of Jim Collins's comparison leaders, the researchers saw a 'gargantuan personal ego' that hastened the company's demise.","CEO disease is the peril where powerful leaders surround themselves with good news and ignore warning signs.","Iacocca resorted to the fixed mindset's weapons of blame, excuses, and the stifling of critics and rivals.","Albert Dunlap inflated revenues, fired people who questioned him, and covered up Sunbeam's dire straits.","Jeff Skilling believed his genius was profit and recorded millions in profits before a penny of revenue was generated.","Ken Lay wrote of honesty while Enron's ruthlessness thrived, because perception, not reality, was all-important.","Steve Case would rather let the company collapse than let another prince be crowned.","Jack Welch learned there is only a razor's edge between self-confidence and hubris, and that true self-confidence is the courage to be open.","Gerstner disbanded the management committee and attacked elitism, saying 'Hierarchy means very little to me.'","Anne Mulcahy took Balance Sheet 101, told the cold hard truth, cut the employee rolls by 30 percent, yet said 'I'm sorry' and preserved the culture.","Growth-minded leaders use the company as an engine of growth for themselves, the employees, and the company as a whole."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the self-defeating arc of the fixed-mindset CEO, ego, blame, cover-up, and the craving for validation, against the growth-minded turnaround practices of Gerstner, Welch, and Mulcahy. Dweck argues the fixed mindset explains where gargantuan egos come from and why they sacrifice their companies.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Building a Culture of Development","visual_frame_description":"A high-rise trading floor at a mid-sized financial firm, where a hallway monitor plays the CEO's speech declaring, 'We hire geniuses here.' On cubicle walls employees quietly track competitors' missteps, and a screen shows anonymized survey answers: 'There is a lot of cheating, taking shortcuts, and cutting corners,' and 'People here hide information and keep secrets.' A team lead whispers to a new hire, 'Never admit a mistake. You'll be labeled.'","player_mission":"Diagnose the organizational mindset of this company and the outcomes Dweck's research predicts for its employees, then choose the set of moves that would build a culture of development.","boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the organizational mindset, the pervasive, company-wide belief that talent is fixed, a culture of genius, or developable, a culture of development, and how leaders weave it into the fabric of the firm. Dweck's study of Fortune 500 and 1000 companies showed this belief predicts trust, commitment, innovation, and ethical behavior.","stage_1_observation":"The CEO's speech celebrates hiring geniuses, employees document competitors' missteps, and an internal survey reveals widespread corner-cutting and secret-keeping. A team lead tells a new hire never to admit a mistake, and the company's best performers avoid speaking up. The culture visibly revolves around proving brilliance rather than learning.","stage_1_question":"Which organizational mindset does this company embody, and what does the chapter's research predict about its employees?","stage_1_correct_answer":"A culture of genius, the fixed mindset: it worships natural talent, and Dweck's data predicts employees will distrust the company, hide information, avoid risk-taking, report more unethical behavior, and express a stronger desire to leave for another firm.","stage_2_intervention":"You are the new head of talent, and leadership wants to shift the culture. Which set of moves best creates a culture of development?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Present skills as learnable, reward learning and perseverance rather than ready-made genius, give feedback that promotes learning and future success, present managers as resources for coaching, and run a growth-mindset workshop for managers modeled on Heslin's so they notice improvement and coach poor performers instead of labeling them. These are the growth-mindset environment elements the chapter lists.","key_concepts":["An organization can have a pervasive belief that talent is just fixed or that talent can be developed in all employees.","Fixed-mindset companies are a 'culture of genius'; growth-mindset companies are a 'culture of development.'","Dweck found strong consensus within each company about whether it held fixed- or growth-mindset beliefs.","Employees in growth-mindset companies have far more trust in the company and a greater sense of empowerment and ownership.","Employees in fixed-mindset companies report more cheating, taking shortcuts, and hiding information.","Employees in growth-mindset companies report that their organization supports reasonable risk-taking and innovation.","Supervisors in growth-mindset companies rate employees as more collaborative, committed to learning, innovative, and higher in management potential.","Fixed-mindset companies search for talent, hire it, and reward it, then ask 'Where's the talent?' because it is not flourishing.","Creating a growth-mindset environment includes presenting skills as learnable and managers as resources for learning.","A growth-mindset workshop can teach managers to detect improvement and coach poor performers."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests how organizational mindset shapes behavior across a company. Dweck's survey of Fortune 500 and 1000 companies showed a culture of genius breeds distrust, secrecy, and corner-cutting, while a culture of development breeds trust, innovation, and flourishing talent.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."}}]},{"id":"ch11","title":"Chapter 6: Relationships: Mindsets in Love (or Not)","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Reaction to romantic rejection: revenge versus forgiveness","visual_frame_description":"A dimly lit apartment at night. On one side of the screen sits Maya, 29, scrolling her phone with tears on her face, a crumpled breakup note on the table. On the other side, a glowing timeline shows her a year later: she is laughing with friends at a cafe and later standing at a dance floor. Above each scene, a label hovers with a thought bubble revealing her inner goal. The player sorts six rejection reactions into the fixed-mindset, growth-mindset, or neutral column.","player_mission":"Read each reaction a person had to a terrible breakup and drag it into the correct column: fixed-mindset reaction, growth-mindset reaction, or neither. After sorting all six items, answer the question about which response pattern typifies the growth mindset.","key_concepts":["People with the fixed mindset felt judged and labeled by a rejection, as though a verdict of UNLOVABLE had been branded on their foreheads.","For people with the fixed mindset the number one goal after a breakup was revenge, not healing.","People with the growth mindset had rejection stories that were every bit as wrenching but their goal was understanding, forgiving, and moving on.","Growth-mindset people tried to learn something useful about themselves and relationships from a failed romance.","One man in the growth mindset said the relationship taught him the importance of communicating because love needs a lot of help.","A fixed-mindset friend told Dweck that given the choice between her own happiness and her ex-husband's misery, she would want him to be miserable.","Lydia, a fixed-mindset woman in the study, said she would get back at the man who left her and hurt him any way she could because he deserved it.","Growth-mindset people did not feel permanently branded by rejection and knew how to move on and embrace the future.","People in both mindsets told wrenching rejection stories; what differed dramatically was how they dealt with it.","The growth mindset's reaction to rejection included forgiving and wishing the other person well rather than seeking payback."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests how the fixed mindset frames rejection as a permanent verdict that demands revenge, whereas the growth mindset treats it as an event to learn from, forgive, and move past. Dweck's interview study of more than a hundred people found the two mindsets differed dramatically in how they handled heartbreak.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"After a terrible breakup, which response pattern typifies the growth mindset?","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset reaction","column_b_label":"Growth-mindset reaction","column_c_label":"Neither mindset (neutral)","rows":[{"label":"Feels a permanent verdict, UNLOVABLE, branded on the forehead","col":"a"},{"label":"Number one goal is revenge; wants the ex to pay","col":"a"},{"label":"Wants to understand, forgive, and move on","col":"b"},{"label":"Tries to learn something useful about relationships from the failure","col":"b"},{"label":"Says the relationship taught them who is right for them","col":"b"},{"label":"Lashes out and fantasizes about making the ex miserable","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The growth-mindset person tries to avoid all future relationships to stay safe","The fixed-mindset person quickly forgives and forgets because of low emotional investment","The growth-mindset person feels permanently branded but hides it well"],"explanation":"The rows about permanent branding, revenge as the number one goal, and fantasies of making the ex pay are fixed-mindset reactions because the fixed mindset sees rejection as a verdict on your worth. The rows about understanding, forgiveness, learning, and discovering who is right for you are growth-mindset reactions because that mindset treats the breakup as a source of learning and growth. Since the question asks which pattern typifies the growth mindset, the answer is column b."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Low-effort beliefs and mind reading in love","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen living room. In one frame, Charlene and Max sit at a candlelit dinner in heavy silence while a thought bubble reads 'If this were the right one, we wouldn't have to work so hard.' In the other frame, a couple talks openly at a kitchen table with speech bubbles connecting them. A floating legend at the top shows three columns and the player drags each belief statement into the column it matches.","player_mission":"Sort each belief about relationships into one of three columns: the fixed-mindset low-effort belief, the fixed-mindset mind-reading belief, or the growth-mindset belief in communication and effort. Then answer which column holds the core low-effort assumption.","key_concepts":["People with a fixed mindset believe that if you are compatible, everything in love should come naturally with no effort.","Charlene and Max felt that if the relationship were the right one, they would not have to work so hard.","The fixed mindset expects everything good to happen automatically, as if love will magically solve problems.","Aaron Beck calls 'If we need to work at it, there is something seriously wrong with our relationship' one of the most destructive beliefs.","John Gottman says every marriage demands an effort to keep it on the right track.","Part of the low-effort belief is the idea that couples should be able to read each other's minds.","The book states that mind reading instead of communicating inevitably backfires.","Dweck's own experience of hearing 'I need more space' shows that asking what a partner means beats assuming the worst.","Elayne Savage's couple Tom and Lucy illustrates how mind reading can misread a partner's intention.","A good, lasting relationship comes from effort and from working through inevitable differences."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix contrasts the fixed-mindset low-effort belief that love should be automatic with the mind-reading assumption that partners should just know each other's thoughts, against the growth-mindset view that lasting love is built through communication and effort. The book argues the low-effort belief robs couples of what they need to thrive.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Which column holds the core fixed-mindset assumption that love should require no effort?","column_a_label":"Low-effort compatibility belief","column_b_label":"Mind-reading belief","column_c_label":"Growth-mindset communication belief","rows":[{"label":"If you have to work at it, it wasn't meant to be","col":"a"},{"label":"My partner should just know what I think, feel, and need","col":"b"},{"label":"A good relationship comes from effort and working through differences","col":"c"},{"label":"Mind reading instead of communicating inevitably backfires","col":"c"},{"label":"If we were truly compatible, everything would come naturally","col":"a"},{"label":"Love should work the way Sleeping Beauty's prince worked on her coma","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The growth mindset expects instant, perfect, perpetual compatibility without friction","Mind reading is actually a reliable growth-mindset strategy","Relationship experts agree that needing to work at a relationship means it is doomed"],"explanation":"The statements about not having to work at it, natural compatibility, and magical love are all the low-effort compatibility belief because they claim a right relationship should be effortless. The statements about knowing what a partner thinks or feels without being told are the mind-reading belief, which the book says backfires. The statements that effort and communication build lasting love reflect the growth mindset and what experts like Beck and Gottman actually recommend. The low-effort assumption sits in column a, which is why it is the correct answer."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Blame and character flaws in relationship conflict","visual_frame_description":"A couples-therapy office with three observation panels. In the first, Ted and Karen glare at each other, labels flashing over their heads reading 'irresponsible airhead' and 'judgmental tyrant.' In the second, Brenda gestures excitedly while Jack visibly loses the thread of her long story. In the third, a calm couple asks each other what is the mature thing to do. The player drags each blame statement into the column it belongs to.","player_mission":"Read each statement that couples made during conflicts and sort it into the right column: blame on the partner's fixed traits, blame on your own fixed traits, or seeing a solvable situational problem. Then answer which pattern defines the fixed mindset's typical explanation of conflict.","key_concepts":["When people with a fixed mindset talk about conflicts, they assign blame, often to a trait, a character flaw.","Blaming a partner's personality for a problem produces anger and disgust toward that partner.","Since the problem is attributed to fixed traits, the fixed mindset concludes it cannot be solved.","Ted came to see Karen as an irresponsible airhead and she came to see him as a judgmental tyrant.","Daniel Wile's clients Brenda and Jack were each hurling traits: Brenda is boring, Jack is selfish, our relationship is no good.","The Brenda and Jack conflict was really a problem of communication, not of personality or character.","Charlie, Yvonne's husband, feared that confronting reality would force him to see himself as the bad person who drove her away.","A growth mindset does not mean confronting the partner; it means confronting the situation and thinking about what is wrong.","Ted saved his marriage by asking 'What is the mature thing to do?' and responding with a helpful action rather than a label.","People with a growth mindset can see their partners' imperfections and still think they have a fine relationship."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the attribution pattern of the fixed mindset, which converts relationship problems into verdicts about fixed character flaws in a partner or in oneself. The book shows that once a problem is blamed on a fixed trait, it is seen as unsolvable, whereas the growth mindset treats the same problem as a solvable situation or communication issue.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"When conflict arises, what is the fixed mindset's typical way of explaining the problem?","column_a_label":"Blame the partner's fixed traits","column_b_label":"Blame your own fixed traits","column_c_label":"See a solvable situational problem","rows":[{"label":"Ted calls Karen an irresponsible airhead","col":"a"},{"label":"Karen calls Ted a judgmental tyrant","col":"a"},{"label":"Charlie wonders if he is a bad person who drove his wife away","col":"b"},{"label":"Jack thinks Brenda is boring and the relationship is no good","col":"a"},{"label":"Ted asks what the mature thing to do is and cleans the house to help Karen","col":"c"},{"label":"The couple discusses the problem as a communication issue rather than a character flaw","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The fixed mindset typically blames random bad luck rather than a person's traits","The fixed mindset usually sees conflicts as solvable communication problems","People with a growth mindset routinely assign blame to fixed character traits"],"explanation":"The rows where one partner labels the other's character (airhead, tyrant, boring) are blame on the partner's fixed traits because the fixed mindset assigns blame to a trait rather than a situation. Charlie blaming himself reflects self-blame on fixed traits. The rows involving asking what the mature thing to do is, or discussing the issue as communication, reflect a growth mindset that sees the problem as solvable. The fixed mindset typically blames the partner's traits, making column a the correct answer."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Shyness and the growth mindset (Beer's study)","visual_frame_description":"A psychology lab filmed through one-way glass. Two strangers, Priya and Noah, sit facing each other over a small table for an ice-breaker. A live timer reads 5:00 in the corner. One observer panel shows Priya's behavior ticking down nervously with an avoiding-eye-contact icon, while Noah's panel shows him visibly relaxing and leaning in. The player sorts each described behavior into the mindset column it matches.","player_mission":"Jennifer Beer's study put shy people together two at a time to get acquainted. Read each behavior observed in the filmed interactions and sort it into the fixed-mindset shy pattern, the growth-mindset shy pattern, or the non-shy pattern. Then answer which pattern describes a shy person with a growth mindset.","key_concepts":["Shy people report their hearts racing, blushing, avoiding eye contact, and trying to end interactions as soon as possible.","Jennifer Beer measured people's mindsets and shyness and brought them together two at a time to get acquainted while filming.","Beer found that people with the fixed mindset were more likely to be shy.","Shyness harmed the social interactions of people with the fixed mindset but did not harm those of people with the growth mindset.","Both shy fixed- and growth-minded people looked very nervous for the first five minutes of the interaction.","After the first five minutes, shy growth-minded people showed greater social skills, were more likable, and created a more enjoyable interaction.","Shy growth-minded people looked on social situations as challenges and welcomed meeting someone new.","Shy fixed-minded people wanted to avoid meeting someone who might be more socially skilled than they were.","The shy growth-minded people felt less shy as the interaction wore on, while the shy fixed-minded people stayed nervous.","Shy fixed-minded people did more socially awkward things, such as avoiding eye contact or trying to avoid talking."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the interaction between shyness and mindset found in Jennifer Beer's filmed study: shyness only derailed the interactions of fixed-mindset people, while shy growth-minded people recovered and became likable. The book explains this through how the two mindsets frame the social situation as a challenge versus a risk of judgment.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"In Beer's study, which pattern describes a shy person with a growth mindset?","column_a_label":"Shy fixed-mindset pattern","column_b_label":"Shy growth-mindset pattern","column_c_label":"Non-shy pattern","rows":[{"label":"Avoids meeting someone who might be more socially skilled","col":"a"},{"label":"Looks on the social situation as a challenge to embrace","col":"b"},{"label":"After five minutes shows greater social skills and is more likable","col":"b"},{"label":"Stays nervous and avoids eye contact throughout the whole interaction","col":"a"},{"label":"Tries to end the interaction as soon as possible","col":"a"},{"label":"Actively welcomes the chance to meet someone new despite anxiety","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["A shy growth-minded person becomes even more anxious as the interaction goes on","Shy fixed-minded people recover and become more likable after five minutes","Only non-shy people ever enjoy meeting new people"],"explanation":"The behaviors of avoiding skilled people, staying nervous, avoiding eye contact, and ending the interaction quickly are fixed-mindset shy patterns, because fear of judgment keeps those people guarded and controlling the interaction. The behaviors of treating the encounter as a challenge, welcoming the new person, and growing more likable after five minutes are growth-mindset shy patterns, because those people reframe the situation as an opportunity rather than a judgment. Since the question asks about a shy person with a growth mindset, the answer is column b."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Bullying: fixed-mindset victims versus growth-mindset victims","visual_frame_description":"A school hallway rendered in three panels. In the left panel, a student clutches a locker, thought bubble reading 'I'm a nobody and nobody likes me.' In the center panel, another student stands tall and asks a taunting kid a question, bubble reading 'Why are you doing this?' In the right panel, a popular student smirks while the crowd laughs. The player sorts each response into the fixed-mindset victim, growth-mindset victim, or bully-motive column.","player_mission":"Dweck's lab gave eighth graders a bullying scenario and asked what they would think and want to do. Read each response and sort it into the fixed-mindset victim column, the growth-mindset victim column, or the bully's motive column. Then answer which pattern describes the growth-mindset victim.","key_concepts":["Fixed-mindset students took the bullying more personally and thought they were nobodies whom nobody liked.","Fixed-mindset students wanted violent revenge, saying they would explode with rage, punch the bullies' faces in, or run them over.","Fixed-mindset students strongly agreed that their number one goal would be to get revenge.","Growth-mindset students saw the bullying as a psychological problem of the bullies, a way for the bullies to gain status or charge their self-esteem.","Growth-mindset students planned to talk to the bullies and ask them why they were doing it.","Growth-mindset students said they would confront the person and try to help them see they are not funny.","Growth-mindset students strongly agreed they would want to forgive the bullies eventually.","Growth-mindset students strongly agreed that their number one goal would be to help the bullies become better people.","Bullies, like the boys in Sheri Levy's study, get a boost in self-esteem from judging and demeaning others.","Prolonged bullying can instill a fixed mindset even in victims who did not have one to begin with."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests how the fixed mindset converts being bullied into a verdict about the self and a demand for violent revenge, whereas the growth mindset attributes the bullying to the bully's own problems and aims to educate and forgive. The book reports this pattern directly from its eighth-grade study and links it to the Columbine shootings.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"According to the eighth-grade study, which response pattern describes a growth-mindset victim of bullying?","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset victim response","column_b_label":"Growth-mindset victim response","column_c_label":"Bully's motive","rows":[{"label":"Thinks 'I'm a nobody and nobody likes me'","col":"a"},{"label":"Plans to punch the bullies' faces in or run them over","col":"a"},{"label":"Sees the bullying as the bullies' way to gain status or charge their self-esteem","col":"b"},{"label":"Wants to forgive eventually and help the bullies become better people","col":"b"},{"label":"Asks the bullies questions to understand why they do it","col":"b"},{"label":"Judges others to get a boost in self-esteem","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The growth-mindset victim tries to become more popular to avoid being bullied","The growth-mindset victim agrees that their number one goal is revenge","The fixed-mindset victim treats the bullying as the bully's psychological problem"],"explanation":"The rows about feeling like a nobody and wanting to punch or run over the bullies are fixed-mindset victim responses, because the rejection is taken personally and demands violent payback. The rows about seeing the bullying as the bullies' problem, asking why they do it, forgiving, and helping them improve are growth-mindset victim responses. Judging others for a self-esteem boost describes the bully's own motive, as in Sheri Levy's study. The growth-mindset victim pattern is column b."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Revenge is sweet: helping a friend past a breakup","visual_frame_description":"A quiet bar after work. Tom, 34, an accountant, nurses a single glass and stares at his phone, which shows an old photo of him with Maya. His friend (the player) sits across from him. A framed clock on the wall reads 7:42 PM. Tom has just said that not a day goes by that he doesn't think about how to make his ex pay.","player_mission":"Tom was engaged for five years when Maya left him, and a year later he still fantasizes about revenge. As his friend, steer the conversation so Tom can understand his loss and move forward instead of staying trapped in bitterness. Choose the response that most helps him grow from the experience.","key_concepts":["People with the fixed mindset treated a rejection as a permanent verdict, feeling judged and labeled as though branded UNLOVABLE.","For people with the fixed mindset the number one goal after a breakup was revenge.","One fixed-mindset man said his ex took his worth with her when she left and that he thought every day about making her pay.","A fixed-mindset friend told Dweck that between her own happiness and her ex-husband's misery, she would want him to be miserable.","The idea that revenge brings redemption is a fixed-mindset notion; growth-mindset people have little taste for it.","Growth-mindset people wanted to understand, forgive, and move on after a breakup.","Growth-mindset people tried to learn something useful about themselves and relationships from a failed romance.","One growth-mindset man learned from his breakup the importance of communicating, because love needs a lot of help.","Growth-mindset people knew how to move on and embrace the future after being hurt.","Dweck's cousin Cathy, left after twenty-three years of marriage, refused to wallow and went out dancing, where she met her future husband."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the revenge-versus-forgiveness split after romantic rejection: the fixed mindset frames the breakup as a verdict that demands payback, while the growth mindset treats it as a source of learning and moving on. Dweck's interview study of more than a hundred people found the two mindsets differed dramatically in their goals after heartbreak.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Tom, a 34-year-old accountant, was left by his fiancée Maya after a five-year engagement. He tells you, his longtime friend, that she took his worth with her when she left and that he wants to make her pay. The stakes are Tom's emotional future: a year of revenge fantasies is keeping him stuck, and you want to help him heal without dismissing his pain.","dialogue":["Tom: She took my worth with her when she left. Not a day goes by I don't think about how to make her pay.","Player: That's a heavy thing to carry for a whole year.","Tom: Revenge is the only thing that would make me feel whole again. It's my redemption.","Player: What would revenge actually give you, besides a moment of satisfaction?","Tom: Proof that I matter. That she was wrong to leave me.","Player: What if there's a way to matter that doesn't depend on her at all?","Tom: Like what?","Player: Like what that relationship taught you about who you are and what you need in a partner.","Tom: I never thought of it that way. I just thought about what she did to me.","Player: You get to decide what this means for your life going forward. You're not branded by it."],"choices":["Agree that revenge will taste sweet and help Tom plot how to make Maya regret leaving.","Tell Tom to forgive and forget immediately, because dwelling on it is weak.","Ask Tom what the relationship taught him about himself and what he wants now, and encourage him to build that future.","Tell Tom the rejection proves he was fundamentally unlovable and he should accept that."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Revenge versus forgiveness after rejection: the fixed mindset treats a breakup as a permanent verdict that must be answered with payback, while the growth mindset seeks understanding, learning, and forgiveness.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice reframes the breakup as information rather than a verdict, matching how growth-mindset people in the study tried to learn something useful about themselves and relationships. Agreeing with revenge (choice 0) feeds the fixed-mindset conviction that the ex's misery is the goal. Forcing instant forgiveness (choice 1) dismisses his pain and gives no path to growth. Accepting the rejection as proof he is unlovable (choice 3) reinforces the permanent UNLOVABLE branding the fixed mindset produces."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Mind reading: when a partner says they need more space","visual_frame_description":"A cozy apartment living room at night. Priya sits on one end of the couch with a mug of tea, looking gentle but serious, while the player sits at the other end. Soft lamp light fills the room. Priya has just said, 'I need more space,' and the player's expression shows the room suddenly feels colder.","player_mission":"Your partner Priya just told you she needs more space. You feel the bottom drop out of your stomach. Decide how to respond in the moment instead of letting your mind read a catastrophe into her words.","key_concepts":["Part of the low-effort belief in love is the idea that couples should be able to read each other's minds.","The book states plainly that mind reading instead of communicating inevitably backfires.","People with a fixed mindset believe a couple should share all of each other's views, so they assume rather than communicate.","When Dweck's future husband said 'I need more space,' she assumed the worst about the relationship before asking what he meant.","He meant she should move over on the couch so he could have more room, not that he wanted to end things.","Elayne Savage's client Lucy misread the word 'imbalance' as her partner being less into the relationship when he meant he wanted to move it to the next level.","A no-effort relationship is a doomed relationship, not a great relationship.","It takes work to communicate accurately and to expose and resolve conflicting hopes and beliefs.","Couples can mistakenly believe they agree on each other's rights and duties without ever saying them aloud.","The fixed mindset's ideal of instant, perfect, perpetual compatibility makes any friction feel like a verdict."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the mind-reading belief, the fixed-mindset assumption that a partner should know what you think, feel, and need without being told. The book shows that assuming instead of asking backfires, and that simply asking what a partner means, as Dweck did when her husband said 'I need more space,' prevents a manufactured crisis.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"You have been dating Priya for several months and everything has felt right. One evening she says, 'I need more space.' Dweck describes nearly misreading this exact sentence when her future husband said it to her. The stakes are the relationship itself, because how you interpret the comment determines whether you spiral or stay connected.","dialogue":["Priya: There's something I need to tell you. I need more space.","Player: Wait, I... everything felt so right between us.","Priya: It is right. That's exactly why I want to say this clearly.","Player: Are you saying you want to end things?","Priya: No. I mean the apartment. You basically live here now, and I'm losing my quiet time.","Player: So 'space' meant... moving some of my things back to my place?","Priya: Exactly. Not 'us.' I should have said it better.","Player: I'm really glad I asked what you meant instead of assuming the worst."],"choices":["Assume it means she is pulling away and start preparing yourself for the breakup.","Ask Priya what she means by more space before deciding what it means.","Accuse her of keeping you at arm's length and demand a straight answer.","Match her distance by going cold and silent for the rest of the night."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Mind reading: the fixed-mindset belief that a partner should know what you think, feel, and need without being told, which the book warns inevitably backfires because it replaces communication with assumption.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice asks for clarification, exactly what Dweck did when her future husband said 'I need more space' and it turned out he meant 'move over on the couch.' Assuming the worst (choice 0) is mind reading that manufactures a breakup from ambiguity. Accusing Priya (choice 2) turns a misunderstanding into a conflict. Going cold (choice 3) is the retaliation pattern that lets a solvable situation curdle into distance."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"If you have to work at it, it wasn't meant to be","visual_frame_description":"A cozy cafe at lunchtime. Charlene, 31, a graphic designer, sits across from a friend (the player) stirring her coffee. A cello case leans against her chair, and her phone shows a photo of her and Max at a concert. She looks conflicted, and her voice wavers as she describes Max's moods and their growing distance.","player_mission":"Charlene and her boyfriend Max are drifting apart. Both believe that if the relationship were the right one, they would not have to work so hard. As her friend, help her see that friction is normal and workable rather than proof the relationship is doomed.","key_concepts":["People with a fixed mindset expect everything good in a relationship to happen automatically, as if by magic.","Charlene and Max both felt that if the relationship were the right one, they would not have to work so hard.","When Max was moody he wanted to be left alone, while Charlene felt shut out by his silence.","Friends urged Charlene and Max to work on the problem, but they believed a right relationship would need no effort.","The fixed mindset applies the idea that if you have ability you should not have to work hard to relationships: if you are compatible, everything should come naturally.","Aaron Beck calls the belief that needing to work at a relationship means something is seriously wrong one of the most destructive relationship beliefs.","John Gottman says every marriage demands an effort to keep it on the right track.","A good, lasting relationship comes from effort and from working through inevitable differences.","Believing success should not need effort robs people of what their relationship needs to thrive.","In the book, Charlene and Max grew apart and eventually broke up because of this low-effort belief."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the low-effort belief, the fixed-mindset assumption that a compatible relationship should require no work and that friction proves it was not meant to be. The book shows this belief doomed Charlene and Max and is contradicted by relationship experts Aaron Beck and John Gottman, who insist effort is what sustains love.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Charlene dated Max, a cellist, and at first they felt they understood each other deeply. Now Max turns moody and wants to be left alone, while Charlene feels shut out. Mutual friends have urged them to work on the problem, but both privately believe a right relationship should come naturally. The stakes are whether this promising couple breaks up over a fixable pattern.","dialogue":["Charlene: When he's in a bad mood, he wants to be left alone. When I try to talk, he says, 'Just leave me alone.'","Player: What does that feel like to you?","Charlene: Shut out. And at dinner, when I make light conversation, he says, 'I thought you understood me.'","Player: So you each want different things when he's down.","Charlene: Our friends say we should work on it. But if this were the right relationship, wouldn't it just work?","Player: Every relationship expert I've ever read says no. Effort is what makes a relationship work.","Charlene: So we're not doomed just because we have friction?","Player: Friction is normal. The real question is whether you'll talk it through together instead of deciding it wasn't meant to be."],"choices":["Tell Charlene that if they have to work this hard, the relationship clearly wasn't meant to be.","Tell Charlene a good relationship comes from effort and working through differences, and suggest they talk it out together.","Tell Charlene to accept that the relationship is fundamentally broken and start moving on.","Tell Charlene to ignore the problem and just enjoy the good days when they happen."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"The low-effort belief: the fixed-mindset assumption that if you are compatible everything should come naturally, which the book contrasts with the growth-mindset view that a lasting relationship is built through effort and working through inevitable differences.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice names the real mechanism: Charlene and Max broke up in the book because they believed the right relationship would need no effort, and this belief robs couples of the very thing they need to thrive. Telling her it wasn't meant to be (choice 0) is the exact fixed-mindset verdict that destroyed them. Declaring the relationship broken (choice 2) mirrors the same fatalism, and ignoring the problem (choice 3) is avoidance, not growth."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Shyness and being judged: George in the coffee shop","visual_frame_description":"A busy coffee shop with warm lighting. George, a shy engineer in his late twenties, stands at a table gripping his cup. Across from him, Jean, a friendly co-worker, smiles and has just suggested he ask her to join him. The player sees George's anxious inner monologue racing: be cool, don't seem overeager, don't get rejected.","player_mission":"You are George, a shy engineer whose co-worker Jean is clearly flirting. She has just suggested you ask her to join you. Choose how to respond in a way that builds a connection instead of protecting your ego from a feared rejection.","key_concepts":["Shy people report that their hearts race, they blush, they avoid eye contact, and they try to end interactions as soon as possible.","The fixed mindset makes people concerned about being judged, which can make them more self-conscious and anxious.","George was so eager to look cool, witty, and confident that he froze and acted cold instead.","George tried to protect himself from rejection by trying not to seem too interested.","In the book, when Jean suggested George ask her to join him, he replied, 'It doesn't matter to me if you sit down or not,' and she left fast.","Scott Wetzler slowly helped George stop focusing on being judged and instead focus on developing a relationship.","George eventually approached Jean, apologized for his rude behavior, and asked her to lunch, and she accepted.","Jean was not nearly as critical as George feared.","Shy growth-minded people look on social situations as challenges, while shy fixed-minded people fear making mistakes.","Jennifer Beer found that shyness harmed the social interactions of fixed-mindset people but not of growth-mindset people."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests how the fixed mindset's fear of being judged amplifies shyness and sabotages connection, as with George's cold 'It doesn't matter to me if you sit down or not.' Wetzler's clinical work and Beer's filmed study both show that reframing the interaction from judgment to relationship lets shy people connect despite their anxiety.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"George, a client of therapist Scott Wetzler, is painfully shy, especially with women, and freezes trying to look cool and witty. His attractive co-worker Jean has been flirting with him, and when he avoided her she approached him in a coffee shop and cutely suggested he ask her to join him. The stakes are a real chance at connection that George keeps sabotaging out of fear of judgment.","dialogue":["Jean: Hey George. Mind if I join you?","George: (inner voice: don't seem overeager, don't look inept, don't risk rejection)","Jean: I've actually been meaning to talk to you. You seem interesting.","George: ...","Jean: Okay, well, I can just go if you're busy.","George: (inner voice: if I seem too interested I lose all my power here)","Player: (choose how George responds out loud)","Jean: I'd really like to hear about that project you're working on."],"choices":["Say: 'It doesn't matter to me if you sit down or not.'","Say: 'Please, sit down. I actually get nervous meeting new people, but I'd like to talk to you.'","Say: 'I suppose you can. I'm usually the one who decides where we sit anyway.'","Say nothing at all and hope she stays despite the silence."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Shyness and the fixed mindset: the shy fixed-mindset person is controlled by fear of being judged and tries to end the interaction, whereas treating the moment as a chance to develop a relationship, as Wetzler eventually helped George do, allows connection despite anxiety.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice drops the performance and admits the nervousness, which is how Wetzler helped George get past his exclusive focus on being judged. Choice 0 is the exact line from the book that made Jean leave fast, because it was designed to protect George from seeming interested. Choice 2 is a hollow power play that still frames the moment as a contest. Choice 3 repeats the avoidance pattern that Beer's study showed keeps fixed-mindset shy people trapped."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The lower you are, the better I feel: a friendship under competition","visual_frame_description":"A rooftop party at dusk. Sam, 33, is beaming, holding up his phone showing a promotion email. His friend Rick, standing beside him, offers a tight smile. Over Rick's head a faint cartoon reads 'The lower you are, the better I feel.' Partygoers chatter in the background as the two men talk.","player_mission":"You are Sam, who just told your close friend Rick about your promotion. Rick's congratulations are laced with put-downs, a pattern you have started to notice. Decide how to respond to a friend who seems to need you to stay small.","key_concepts":["Sheri Levy found that adolescent boys who believed in the fixed mindset showed a boost in self-esteem when they endorsed negative stereotypes about girls.","Boys with the growth mindset were less likely to agree with the stereotypes, and agreeing gave them no ego boost.","The idea behind this mentality in friendships is 'the lower you are, the better I feel.'","Some friends, though brilliant and charming, leave you feeling diminished because they establish their superiority and your inferiority.","A person can be a vehicle for, and a casualty of, another's need to confirm their own worth.","Dweck describes a friend who punished her by saying, 'You can help me polish my work, but you could never be this creative.'","That friend needed to reduce her so he would not feel one-down.","It is difficult to realize when friends do not wish you well.","Your failures and misfortunes do not threaten other people's self-esteem; your successes do, for people who derive self-esteem from being superior.","Dweck admits that when she needed validation, she used people for it, treating one businessman as a mirror of her excellence."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the tendency, supported by Levy's study, for fixed-mindset people to prop up their own self-esteem by diminishing others, even friends. The book shows this dynamic converts friendships into competitions and that the growth-minded response is to recognize the pattern and stop being used as a casualty of someone else's need for superiority.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Sam just earned a senior role he worked five years for. His friend Rick, who has been leaning on Sam to polish his work for a year, responds by cutting Sam down, hinting that Sam could never be truly creative. Sam is beginning to see that Rick measures himself by keeping others low, and he must decide whether to accept this dynamic or draw the line.","dialogue":["Sam: I got the senior role I've been chasing. I wanted to tell you first.","Rick: Senior role. Huh. Must be nice to have things handed to you.","Sam: Handed to me? I've been grinding at this for five years.","Rick: Right. Anyway, my project needs a polish — you're good for that. Just remember, you could never do work this creative yourself.","Sam: You always do this. When something good happens to me, you find a way to cut it down.","Rick: I'm just being honest. Some of us earn what we have.","Sam: I think you're measuring yourself against me, and it's starting to hurt our friendship.","Rick: You're imagining things. I'm your friend. That's why I help you see things straight."],"choices":["Shrink your achievement: tell Rick he's right, you got lucky and you'll stay in your lane.","Prove your worth by listing every achievement you have accumulated over the years.","Name the pattern honestly: tell Rick that when he reduces you he props himself up, and that you won't be used that way anymore.","Silently stop sharing good news with Rick but keep helping him with his work."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Using others to prop up self-esteem: people with a fixed mindset can bolster themselves by diminishing others, even in friendships, and the book warns that being used as a mirror of another person's worth makes you a casualty of their need to feel superior.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice identifies the mechanism out loud, which is the growth-minded move of confronting the situation rather than accepting a verdict. Choice 0 keeps the friendship on the terms of Sam staying small, exactly the pattern Dweck describes where the friend punished her by saying she could never be creative. Choice 1 plays the proof game the fixed mindset thrives on. Choice 4, the silent retreat, avoids the conflict but leaves the damaging dynamic intact, the passive route that lets a relationship rot quietly."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The blame battle: from partner as enemy to teammate","visual_frame_description":"A tense living room. The wife's inner voice thunders 'It's not my fault!' while the husband stands with his arms crossed. Between them, a small cartoon man named Maurice holds a sign reading 'Blame me instead.' In a second window, the same couple years later sits calmly at a table talking, with the words 'What is the mature thing to do?' glowing above them.","player_mission":"Defeat the fixed mindset's urge to turn your partner into an enemy by shifting from blame to growth. Study the blame spiral in stage one, then intervene in stage two to replace labels with helpful action.","key_concepts":["In the fixed mindset, one moment your partner is the light of your life and the next they are your adversary.","When something goes wrong in a relationship it is easy to blame someone else.","In the fixed mindset you have a limited set of choices: blame your own permanent qualities or blame your partner's.","Dweck confesses an irresistible urge to defend herself and assign blame when something goes wrong in a relationship.","To deal with the blame habit, Dweck and her husband invented an imaginary man named Maurice to pin the blame on.","People with the fixed mindset find it hard to forgive because forgiving the partner would mean shouldering more blame themselves.","If my partner is a good guy, then I must be the bad guy, is the fixed-mindset logic that blocks forgiveness.","Dweck realized she controlled half of her relationship with her mother, her half, and could be the loving daughter she wanted to be.","Giving up blame and bitterness allowed Dweck to have a real relationship with her mother.","Ted saved his marriage by asking 'What is the mature thing to do?' and responding with a helpful action instead of an angry label."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This boss battle tests the blame dynamic of the fixed mindset, where problems are assigned to someone's permanent flaws and the partner becomes an enemy. The book's path out is the growth mindset's willingness to give up blame, own your half of the relationship, and respond to a partner with helpful action rather than labels, as Ted did.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is that in the fixed mindset, a relationship problem instantly triggers blame, and the partner is transformed from the light of your life into your adversary. The book describes this as a limited menu: blame your own permanent qualities or blame your partner's, and forgiveness feels impossible because forgiving the partner would force you to shoulder the blame yourself.","stage_1_observation":"You observe a married couple in conflict. The wife has an almost irresistible urge to defend herself and assign blame the moment something goes wrong, her inner voice repeating 'It's not my fault!' The husband, meanwhile, feels judged and pulls away. Notice how each person's fear of being the flawed one turns the shared problem into a contest over who is defective.","stage_1_question":"Why does the fixed mindset find it so tempting to foist the blame onto the partner instead of examining the problem?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because in a relationship it is easy to blame someone else, and the fixed mindset offers only two painful choices: blame your own permanent qualities or blame your partner's, so blaming the partner protects your sense of being okay.","stage_2_intervention":"The couple is mid-argument about a genuine conflict, and you must intervene. You can escalate by insisting on who is at fault, or you can shift the frame. Choose the growth-minded move that turns the partner from enemy into teammate.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Step out of blame entirely and ask what the mature thing to do is, then respond with a helpful action toward the partner, the way Ted asked himself this exact question and cleaned the house to support Karen, and the way Dweck's invented 'Maurice' lets a couple pin the blame on a third party so they can work on the real problem together."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The school mindset: from judgment to collaboration","visual_frame_description":"A middle school hallway splits into two visions. In the red-tinted vision, jocks push a quiet student into a locker while others laugh, and the victim's thought bubble reads 'I'm a nobody.' In the green-tinted vision, the same hallway is calm, a counselor shakes a former bully's hand, and a banner reads 'We work on getting along.' A scoreboard tracks the school's atmosphere of judgment versus collaboration.","player_mission":"Lead a school from an atmosphere of judgment to one of collaboration and self-improvement. Study why fixed-mindset victims of bullying turn to revenge in stage one, then intervene in stage two with the approach that Stan Davis used to slash bullying by more than 90 percent.","key_concepts":["Bullying is about judging who is more worthy or important, and bullies rub this judgment in on a daily basis.","Judging and demeaning others gives bullies a boost in self-esteem, like the boys in Sheri Levy's study.","In Dweck's eighth-grade study, fixed-mindset students took bullying personally and thought they were nobodies.","Fixed-mindset students in the study strongly agreed their number one goal would be to get revenge.","Growth-mindset students saw bullying as the bullies' psychological problem, a way to gain status or charge their self-esteem.","Growth-mindset students wanted to talk to, educate, and eventually forgive the bullies.","Prolonged bullying can instill a fixed mindset even in victims who did not have one to begin with.","Stan Davis's anti-bullying program cut physical bullying in his school by 93 percent and teasing by 53 percent.","Davis does not judge the bully as a person; he makes bullies feel liked and welcome and praises their effort, not their traits.","Brooks Brown, a bullying victim, rejected revenge and the right of people to judge others, believing people have the potential to change."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This boss battle tests the school-level application of the mindset insight: an atmosphere of judgment breeds fixed-mindset victims who fantasize about violent revenge, while an atmosphere of collaboration and self-improvement reduces bullying dramatically. The book cites Stan Davis's program, grounded in Dan Olweus's work, which cut physical bullying 93 percent and teasing 53 percent.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is that bullying is fundamentally about judging who is more worthy, and that a fixed mindset converts being judged into a permanent verdict that demands violent payback. The book shows that shifting a whole school's mindset, from judging to collaboration, dramatically reduces bullying, citing Stan Davis's program that cut physical bullying 93 percent and teasing 53 percent.","stage_1_observation":"You are reviewing data from the eighth-grade bullying scenario. Some students imagine being followed, taunted, and called a loser every day, and their written reactions split cleanly. One group writes 'I would think I was a nobody and that nobody likes me' and describes exploding with rage or running the bullies over. Another group writes that the bullies probably have problems at home and asks why they do it. Study these two response patterns.","stage_1_question":"According to Dweck's eighth-grade study, how do fixed-mindset students react when they imagine being bullied?","stage_1_correct_answer":"They take the bullying personally, believe they are stupid, weird, and misfits, and strongly agree that their number one goal would be to get revenge, even fantasizing about punching the bullies' faces in or running them over.","stage_2_intervention":"The principal wants to reduce the bullying that has been happening in the hallway out of sight of teachers. You can punish and label the bullies, ignore the problem as a normal part of school, or transform the school culture. Choose the intervention that matches Stan Davis's proven growth-mindset program.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Implement Stan Davis's approach: enforce consistent discipline while never judging the bully as a person, make bullies feel liked and welcome, praise their effort rather than their traits, support victims like Darla, and empower bystanders to come to a victim's aid, which cut physical bullying 93 percent and teasing 53 percent within a few years."}]},{"id":"ch12","title":"Chapter 7: Parents, Teachers, and Coaches: Where Do Mindsets Come From?","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Praise: Intelligence, Process, and Speed Messages","visual_frame_description":"A family dinner table in a bright Denver kitchen. Nine-year-old Maya beams as she tells her mom, aunts, and uncle that she got an A on a spelling test without studying. Adults in the scene respond with different kinds of praise. A split-panel classifier on the screen sorts each praise statement into one of three labeled columns as the player watches.","player_mission":"Listen to each praise statement the adults make at Maya's dinner and decide which column it belongs in: intelligence/talent praise, process/strategy praise, or speed/perfection praise. Place the statements correctly to expose how the same moment can send either a fixed-mindset or a growth-minded message.","key_concepts":["Praising children's intelligence or talent sends a fixed-mindset message that judges permanent traits rather than development.","Dweck's seven experiments with hundreds of children found that praising children's intelligence harms their motivation and harms their performance.","Children who are praised for being smart hear a hidden message: if I don't learn something quickly, I'm not smart.","Praise that focuses on speed and perfection tells children that fast, flawless work is what earns admiration.","Speed and perfection are the enemy of difficult learning, because children reason that if they are smart only when fast and perfect, they should avoid challenges.","Process praise focuses on what children accomplished through practice, study, persistence, and good strategies.","Praising intelligence gives children a boost only for the moment; the minute they hit a snag, their confidence goes out the window and their motivation hits rock bottom.","If success means you're smart, then failure means you're dumb, which is the fixed mindset.","The best gift parents can give children is to teach them to love challenges, be intrigued by mistakes, enjoy effort, seek new strategies, and keep on learning.","When a task is completed too quickly and perfectly, Dweck suggests saying the task was too easy and finding something the child can really learn from."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests how the content of praise communicates mindset: praising intelligence or talent signals that fixed traits are being judged, while process praise signals that abilities develop through effort and strategy. Dweck reports that after seven experiments with hundreds of children, praising children's intelligence harmed both their motivation and their performance.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"At dinner, Maya's mom says, \"You're so brilliant, you got an A without even studying!\" In which column does this statement belong, given how Dweck analyzes praise?","column_a_label":"Intelligence or Talent Praise (fixed-mindset message)","column_b_label":"Process or Strategy Praise (growth-minded message)","column_c_label":"Speed and Perfection Praise (undermines challenge)","rows":[{"label":"Maya's mom says, \"You're so brilliant, you got an A without even studying!\"","col":"a"},{"label":"Her aunt tells her cousin, \"You're such a natural athlete,\" during a backyard game.","col":"a"},{"label":"Her dad says, \"Wow, you did that so quickly!\" as she races through her math sheet.","col":"c"},{"label":"Her uncle says, \"Look, you didn't make any mistakes!\" inspecting her spelling test.","col":"c"},{"label":"Her teacher says, \"I like the way you tried all kinds of strategies on that math problem until you finally got it.\"","col":"b"},{"label":"Her grandmother says, \"You really studied for your test and your improvement shows it. You read the material over, outlined it, and tested yourself.\"","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["It belongs in the process column because it mentions how she did on the test.","It belongs in the speed column because she finished without studying.","It belongs in no column because praise is always supportive for children."],"explanation":"Dweck's praise research shows that \"You're so brilliant, you got an A without even studying!\" praises the child's intelligence and implies that effortless success proves a fixed trait. The child hears, \"If I don't learn something quickly, I'm not smart,\" which makes her avoid hard work. Process praise (\"you tried strategies,\" \"you studied and outlined and tested yourself\") and speed/perfection praise (\"so quickly,\" \"no mistakes\") are separate categories, with speed and perfection flagged as the enemy of difficult learning."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Process Praise After a Strong Test Performance","visual_frame_description":"A Friday evening in the Rivera family kitchen in Austin, Texas. Twelve-year-old Sofia bursts through the door waving a test paper and announces her score to her mother, Elena. Warm lamplight, a stack of dishes, and Sofia's glowing face set the scene as Elena decides how to respond to the news.","player_mission":"Choose Elena's response to Sofia's high test score. Your goal is to reinforce a growth mindset by praising the process that led to the result without deflating Sofia's excitement, and to avoid trait or speed praise that would make her afraid of hard tasks.","key_concepts":["Praising children's intelligence harms their motivation and their performance, according to Dweck's seven experiments with hundreds of children.","Process praise celebrates what a child accomplished through practice, study, persistence, and good strategies.","Asking children about their work shows interest in their efforts and choices and recognizes how they improved.","Children love praise, but the boost from intelligence praise lasts only until they hit a snag.","Growth-minded praise ties the process to the outcome, so children see that engaging in the process helped them learn.","Haim Ginott concluded that praise should deal not with the child's personality attributes but with his efforts and achievements.","Parents can appreciate their children enthusiastically as long as they keep away from praise that judges intelligence or talent.","A student in the chapter asked her 12-year-old sister about the effort she put into studying and how she improved from the year before.","Speed and perfection are the enemy of difficult learning, because children reason that if they are smart when fast and perfect, they'd better not take on anything challenging.","Parents who want to give children a gift should teach them to love challenges, be intrigued by mistakes, enjoy effort, seek new strategies, and keep on learning."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests process praise: children infer from praise whether their abilities are fixed traits being judged or skills that grow through effort and strategy. Dweck reports that process praise, naming the study, strategies, and persistence that led to learning, is what moves children toward a growth mindset, while intelligence praise makes their confidence and motivation fragile.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Sofia scored 102 on her social studies test and repeats the news about five times over the weekend. Last year her grades dropped lower and lower as the year went on. Elena remembers a lesson about praise and wants to respond in a way that builds a growth mindset. The stakes: how Elena reacts will shape whether Sofia studies to learn or to prove she is smart.","dialogue":"SOFIA: Mom! I got 102 on my social studies test. Did you hear me? One hundred and two!\nELENA: I heard you, honey, and I can see you're bursting about it. Tell me more.\nSOFIA: I studied the chapter like three times, made flashcards of the dates, and last year my grades kept dropping. This time it finally stuck!\nELENA: That's a real change from last year. What do you think you did differently this time?\nSOFIA: I didn't just read it once. I outlined it, and I quizzed myself out loud.\nELENA: So the outlining and quizzing really worked for you. How does it feel to see that pay off?\nSOFIA: It feels like I actually understand it now. Can I try that for science too?\nELENA: That sounds like a great plan. I'm proud of how you stuck with it and kept learning.","choices":["You're so brilliant! You got 102 without even trying hard. I knew you had it in you.","Wow, that was fast! You finished it so quickly, you must be a genius at social studies.","I'm so proud of how you studied, how you outlined it and quizzed yourself, and your improvement really shows it. What do you think helped the most?","A 102? Not bad. Now let's see you keep that up so you stay ahead of everyone else."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Process praise (praise for the process children engage in: hard work, strategies, focus, and perseverance) tied to the child's learning and improvement.","psychological_breakdown":"Option 2 follows Dweck's advice to praise the growth-oriented process and tie it to the child's learning and improvement; it is modeled on the chapter's student who asked her 12-year-old sister about the effort she put in and how she improved from the year before. Options 0 and 1 praise intelligence and speed respectively, and both signal that quick, effortless success is what earns admiration, making Sofia reluctant to take on hard tasks. Option 3 attaches approval to the grade itself, setting up the fixed-mindset trap of proving worth through performance."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Messages About Failure: Elizabeth's Gymnastics Meet","visual_frame_description":"In a Portland, Oregon gym, ten-year-old Becca sits slumped on the bleachers after the district meet, eyes red, clutching an empty gym bag. She competed first in floor exercises and lost as the scoring changed, finishing the night without a single ribbon. Her father, Marcus, crouches beside her as the crowd files out.","player_mission":"Choose Marcus's response to Becca's disappointment. Your goal is to sympathize with her feelings while telling her the truth and giving her a path to improve, instead of offering an insincere boost, blaming the judges, devaluing the sport, or promising that ability alone will win next time.","key_concepts":["The societal message that parents must protect children from failure can be harmful in the long run.","Telling a child she was the best when she was not is insincere and offers no recipe for recovery or improvement.","Blaming the judges teaches children to blame others for their deficiencies.","Devaluing an activity a child loses at teaches her to devalue things she doesn't do well in right away.","Reassuring a child that she has the ability to win next time is dangerous, because ability does not automatically take you where you want to go.","Elizabeth's growth-minded father sympathized with her disappointment, told her the truth, and taught her how to learn from her failures.","Elizabeth responded by spending much more time repeating and perfecting her routines, especially the ones she was weakest in.","At the next meet Elizabeth won five ribbons for the individual events and was the overall champion.","Children need honest and constructive feedback; if they are protected from it, they won't learn well.","Withholding constructive criticism does not help children's confidence; it harms their future."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests messages about failure: growth-minded parents sympathize with disappointment but give honest feedback plus a path to improvement, whereas fixed-minded responses either devalue the activity, blame others, or hand out a phony \"you have the ability\" boost. Dweck reports that Elizabeth's father's honest response taught her to learn from failure, and she went on to win five ribbons and the overall championship at her next meet.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Becca loves gymnastics and felt confident, even imagining where she would hang her first ribbon. After the meet she is devastated. Marcus has read about Elizabeth in Dweck's book and knows this is a messages-about-failure moment. The stakes: his words will either teach her how to recover from failure or teach her to avoid, blame, or devalue it.","dialogue":"BECCA: I didn't get any ribbons, Dad. Not one. I practiced the floor routine so much.\nMARCUS: I know, sweetheart. I know how it feels to have your hopes up, to give it your best, and still not win.\nBECCA: The judges changed the scoring after I went first. It wasn't fair.\nMARCUS: That part was out of your hands. But let's talk about what you can control.\nBECCA: I guess I could work on my weakest events. The beam was really shaky.\nMARCUS: If gymnastics is something you really want to excel in, that's what it will take, more work. If you want to do it just for fun, that's fine too.\nBECCA: I want to win next time. Will you help me practice more?\nMARCUS: I'd love to. Let's make a plan for the routines you struggled with.","choices":["You were robbed. Those judges had it wrong, that ribbon was rightfully yours.","You were the best out there, Becca. I don't care what the scores said.","Gymnastics isn't that important, honey. And you have the ability, so you'll surely win next time.","I know you're disappointed, and I'm sorry. But you haven't really earned it yet, some of those girls have trained longer and harder. If it's what you really want, you'll have to work for it, and I'll help you."],"optimal_choice":3,"theoretical_principle":"Honest, growth-minded feedback on failure: sympathize with the child's disappointment, tell the truth, and give a recipe for improvement instead of protecting the child from failure.","psychological_breakdown":"Option 3 mirrors what Elizabeth's growth-minded father actually said: he sympathized with her disappointment but told her the truth and taught her how to learn from the loss, and she responded by spending far more time perfecting her routines, especially her weakest ones, and winning five ribbons plus the overall title at her next meet. Option 0 places blame on the judges when the problem was mostly her performance. Option 1 is insincere, since she knows she was not the best, and offers no recipe for recovery. Option 2 devalues the sport and reassures her about ability, which is dangerous because ability does not automatically take you where you want to go."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Reassuring Test-Anxious Children: Kristina","visual_frame_description":"A quiet Chicago suburban living room the night before the College Board tests. Kristina, a bright high school senior, paces by the window, wringing her hands. Her parents, Dan and Priya, sit on the couch, watching her and deciding how to build her confidence for the morning.","player_mission":"Choose what Dan and Priya say to Kristina the night before the test. Your goal is to lower the stakes by removing the feeling that she is being evaluated, rather than reassuring her about her intelligence, which would raise the stakes even higher.","key_concepts":["Reassuring children about their intelligence or talent before a test backfires and makes them more afraid to show a deficiency.","Kristina studied and knew the material, but her anxiety made her mind go blank on tests and her grades suffered.","The supportive message \"you know how smart you are, you've got this nailed\" raises the stakes even higher.","The recommended message is: we are not evaluating you; we care about your learning, and we know that you've learned your stuff.","Praising or reassuring about intelligence turns every performance into a test of permanent worth.","Growth-minded reassurance emphasizes learning and the process, not the outcome.","Children are remarkably sensitive to the messages behind their parents' words.","Every word and action can send a fixed-mindset message (you have permanent traits and I'm judging them) or a growth-mindset message (you are a developing person and I'm committed to your development).","Fixed-mindset children interpret parental help as an attempt to measure how smart they are.","Growth-minded children interpret parental help as encouragement to learn and study better in the future."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the backfire effect of intelligence reassurance: telling a test-anxious child she is smart raises the stakes because it implies her fixed trait is being judged. Dweck's recommendation is non-evaluative reassurance that focuses on care for learning, which the book illustrates through the high school student Kristina whose mind went blank on tests.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Kristina always studies and always knows the material, but on test day her anxiety makes her mind go blank and her grades suffer. The schools she longs to attend prize these tests highly. Her parents want to help her feel secure. The stakes: their reassurance will either raise the stakes higher or remove the feeling that she is being evaluated.","dialogue":"KRISTINA: I know everything, but my mind always goes blank. What if it happens tomorrow?\nDAN: Look, you know how smart you are and we know how smart you are. You've got this nailed. Now stop worrying.\nKRISTINA: That's what you said before the last one, and I still froze.\nPRIYA: It must be a terrible thing to feel like everyone is evaluating you and you can't show what you know.\nKRISTINA: It is. I feel like my whole worth is on the line.\nPRIYA: We want you to know that we are not evaluating you. We care about your learning, and we know that you have learned your stuff.\nDAN: And we're proud that you've stuck to it and kept learning all year.\nKRISTINA: So even if I freeze tomorrow, you won't think less of me?\nPRIYA: No. We care about your learning, not a score. And let's make a plan for what to do if you feel yourself tensing up.","choices":["Stop worrying. You're one of the smartest kids in the school, you've got this nailed.","If you don't do well, it's the test's fault, not yours. The College Board is a rigged system anyway.","We are not evaluating you. We care about your learning, we know you've learned your material, and we're proud that you've stuck to it and kept learning. Now let's plan what to do if your mind goes blank.","Tests don't matter at all in the long run, honey. Just relax and get a good night's sleep."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Reassurance about intelligence or talent backfires; non-evaluative reassurance that focuses on care for learning and the process reduces the stakes.","psychological_breakdown":"Option 2 follows the book's recommended message: acknowledge her fear of evaluation, explicitly say you are not evaluating her, emphasize care for her learning, and move to a practical coping plan. Option 0 is trait reassurance, and it raises the stakes by implying her smartness is on trial, exactly what made Kristina's grades suffer. Option 1 removes all responsibility and gives her no way to manage the test. Option 3 devalues the test and dodges her real worry. Reassuring children about their intelligence or talent backfires because it makes them more afraid to show a deficiency."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Constructive Criticism of Sloppy Homework","visual_frame_description":"A Baltimore apartment kitchen table at 6 p.m. Twelve-year-old Billy hands his father, Tom, a homework sheet completed in a rush, with several questions skipped and the rest answered in short, sloppy sentences. Tom sets down his coffee and studies the page, visibly frustrated and disappointed.","player_mission":"Choose Tom's response to Billy's rushed homework. Your goal is to deliver constructive criticism that helps Billy fix the work and learn, not judgment that questions his intelligence and character.","key_concepts":["Constructive criticism means helping the child fix something, build a better product, or do a better job, not judging the child.","Feedback like \"you are either dense or irresponsible\" questions a child's intelligence and character and implies the defects are permanent.","An alternative response is: \"Son, it really makes me upset when you don't do a full job. When do you think you can complete this?\"","Another alternative is: \"Is there something you didn't understand in the assignment? Would you like me to go over it with you?\"","Ginott's story of Philip shows children can be taught to separate the event from a label: \"The nails spilled, I'll pick them up!\"","Children who are protected from constructive feedback won't learn well and will experience advice, coaching, and feedback as negative and undermining.","Withholding constructive criticism does not help children's confidence; it harms their future.","Children are ready to judge and label themselves, and adults can teach them not to.","Growth-minded discipline asks: what message am I sending, I will judge and punish you, or I will help you think and learn?","Growth-minded parents set high standards but teach children how to reach them; they say no as a fair, thoughtful, respectful no."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the difference between constructive and judgmental criticism: judgmental feedback labels the child's permanent traits, while constructive feedback tells the child how to fix the work. The book contrasts the father who called his son \"dense or irresponsible\" with process-oriented alternatives that express upset, check understanding, and schedule completion.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Billy rushed through his homework, skipping questions and answering the rest sloppily. Tom is frustrated, but he remembers the chapter's point that constructive means helping the child fix something, build a better product, or do a better job. The stakes: his words will either teach Billy how to do better or label him as permanently flawed.","dialogue":"TOM: Billy, can we look at this homework together?\nBILLY: I did it. Can I go play now?\nTOM: You rushed through it, skipped questions, and the answers are pretty short. It makes me upset when you don't do a full job.\nBILLY: I didn't like the assignment.\nTOM: Is there something you didn't understand in the assignment? Would you like me to go over it with you?\nBILLY: Maybe the third question. I guessed on that one.\nTOM: Let's look at it together, then. When do you think you can complete the rest, tonight or tomorrow?\nBILLY: Tonight, I guess. If we figure out the third one, the rest are easy.\nTOM: Good. I feel sad when I see you missing a chance to learn, so let's make sure this one counts.","choices":["This is your homework? Can't you ever get it right? You are either dense or irresponsible, which is it?","It's fine, don't worry about it. I'll just do the rest of it for you so you can go play.","Son, it really upsets me when you don't do a full job. When do you think you can complete this? And is there something you didn't understand that I could go over with you?","Your teacher gave you a boring assignment and way too much of it. I'll call the school about that."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Constructive criticism (feedback that helps the child fix something, build a better product, or do a better job) versus judgmental criticism that questions intelligence and character.","psychological_breakdown":"Option 2 uses the alternatives the book offers Billy's father: express the upset, ask when the work can be completed, and offer to go over anything he didn't understand. Option 0 is the book's example of what the angry father actually said, questioning the boy's intelligence and character and implying the defects were permanent. Option 1 removes all responsibility and teaches Billy nothing. Option 3 blames the teacher, mirroring parents who heap blame on coaches and referees instead of giving the child honest, constructive feedback."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Failure Response Taxonomy: Sympathy, Protection, Blame","visual_frame_description":"A classified dashboard displays a young gymnast on a podium that keeps moving out of reach. Beside the image, five parents' voices float in speech bubbles over the bleachers, each reacting to a child who just lost a meet. A three-column sorter awaits each reaction.","player_mission":"Sort each reaction to the child's loss into the correct column: growth-minded failure message, self-esteem-protective message, or blame-shifting message. Classify every reaction correctly to reveal which responses help a child learn from failure.","key_concepts":["The strongest societal message about boosting children's self-esteem is to protect them from failure, which can be harmful in the long run.","Telling a child she was the best is insincere when she was not, and it offers no recipe for how to recover or how to improve.","Telling a child she was robbed places blame on others when the problem was mostly her performance.","Reassuring a child that an activity isn't important teaches her to devalue things she doesn't do well in right away.","Telling a child she has the ability and will surely win next time is dangerous, because ability does not automatically take you where you want to go.","Elizabeth's growth-minded father told her the truth and taught her how to learn from her failures and do what it takes to succeed in the future.","Elizabeth responded by spending much more time repeating and perfecting her routines, especially the ones she was weakest in.","At the next meet Elizabeth won five ribbons for the individual events and was the overall champion of the competition.","Coaches report that athletes now grumble that corrective feedback undermines their confidence, while parents on the ride home heap blame on coaches and referees.","Withholding constructive criticism does not help children's confidence; it harms their future."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix sorts reactions to a child's failure into growth-minded honesty, protective self-esteem boosting, and blame-shifting responses. Dweck argues that the societal protect-them-from-failure message is harmful in the long run, and that honest, sympathetic feedback, as Elizabeth's father gave, teaches children how to recover and improve.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"After Becca loses her gymnastics meet, her father says, \"You haven't really earned it yet, some girls have trained longer and worked harder. If this is something you really want, you'll have to work for it.\" In which column does this response belong?","column_a_label":"Growth-minded failure message (sympathy plus honest feedback and a path to improve)","column_b_label":"Self-esteem-protective message (shield the child from failure)","column_c_label":"Blame-shifting message (assign fault to others)","rows":[{"label":"\"You haven't really earned it yet, you'll have to work for it,\" while sympathizing with the disappointment.","col":"a"},{"label":"Tell the child you thought she was the best, even though she was not.","col":"b"},{"label":"Reassure her that gymnastics is not that important.","col":"b"},{"label":"Tell her she has the ability and will surely win next time.","col":"b"},{"label":"Tell her she was robbed of a ribbon that was rightfully hers.","col":"c"},{"label":"Let her know that if she wants gymnastics purely for fun that is fine, but if she wants to excel in competitions, more is required.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["It belongs in the blame column because the father is blaming the other girls for winning.","It belongs in the protective column because the father is sparing the child's feelings.","It belongs in no column because parents should never mention a child's failure."],"explanation":"Elizabeth's father sympathized with her disappointment but told her the truth and gave her a recipe for improvement, which is the growth-minded failure message in column a. \"She was the best\" and \"she has the ability to win next time\" are protective messages that are insincere or imply ability alone guarantees success. \"She was robbed\" places blame on the judges when the problem was mostly her performance. Elizabeth acted on her father's honest feedback, spent far more time perfecting her weakest routines, and became the overall champion at her next meet."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"False Growth Mindset: Effort Praise as a Consolation Prize","visual_frame_description":"A fifth-grade math classroom in a Newark school, after a worksheet is collected. Marcus, a student who has tried hard all month on fractions, stares at a paper full of wrong answers while Ms. Okonkwo, who attended a growth-mindset workshop, considers how to respond without discouraging him.","player_mission":"Choose Ms. Okonkwo's response to Marcus, who worked hard but shows almost no progress on fractions. Your goal is to respect his genuine effort while refusing to settle for effort that is not producing learning, and to steer him toward new strategies and help.","key_concepts":["A false growth mindset can appear when adults praise effort that is not there, so the process praise has nothing real to reward.","Effort praise used as a consolation prize when kids are not learning is a major concern for Dweck.","We should never be content with effort that is not yielding further benefits; we need to figure out why the effort is not effective.","The process to be appreciated includes more than effort: hard work, trying new strategies, and seeking input from others.","We don't want children to just try harder with the same ineffective strategy.","Dweck fears the mindset concept will be used to make kids feel good when they're not learning, like the failed self-esteem movement.","\"You can do anything\" is empty reassurance unless children are given the skills and resources to make progress.","Growth-minded teaching means telling the truth and giving tools to close the gap, as Marva Collins told a student his reading score so they could work on it.","When math teachers teach for conceptual understanding, give feedback that deepens understanding, and allow revision, their students move toward a growth mindset in math.","Teachers can say \"growth mindset\" while using methods that do not foster it; it is their actions that matter."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the false growth mindset: real growth-minded teaching does not settle for effort praise when learning is not happening, but diagnoses why effort fails and guides students toward new strategies and help. Dweck distinguishes the genuine process, hard work, trying new strategies, and seeking input, from effort used as a consolation prize, warning that the latter repeats the failures of the self-esteem movement.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Marcus has tried hard on fractions for a month with almost no progress, and today's worksheet is mostly wrong. Ms. Okonkwo is tempted to praise his effort so he won't feel bad, but she has also read the chapter's warning about the false growth mindset. The stakes: whether she treats effort as the goal or digs into why his effort is not producing learning.","dialogue":"MS. OKONKWO: Marcus, I can see you really put in the time on this worksheet. That's real effort.\nMARCUS: I tried, but I still don't get fractions. I guess I'm just not a math kid.\nMS. OKONKWO: Let's not decide that today. Let's figure out where it's breaking down.\nMARCUS: I keep doing what you showed me, the same way, and it comes out wrong.\nMS. OKONKWO: So the strategy you're using isn't working. We don't want to just try harder with the same strategy. What did you do on this problem here?\nMARCUS: I subtracted the denominators, like I always do.\nMS. OKONKWO: That's the piece to fix. Let's try drawing the pieces on paper instead, and I'll check in with you before you move on.\nMARCUS: Okay. If I use pictures, will it count?\nMS. OKONKWO: It will count, because it will help you learn it. Effort is great, but our goal is for you to understand fractions. When you get it, we'll know the process worked.","choices":["Nice effort, Marcus. That's what matters most, keep trying hard and you'll get it eventually.","You're just not a math person yet, but don't worry, everyone has their own strengths.","Your effort is real, but it's not getting you to learning yet, and I won't pretend otherwise. Let's change strategies, I'll show you how to draw the fractions, and we'll check that you understand before moving on.","You can do anything you set your mind to, Marcus. Believe in yourself and it will happen."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Effort alone is not the growth mindset: the process includes trying new strategies and seeking help, and effort praise must never become a consolation prize when kids are not learning.","psychological_breakdown":"Option 2 follows the chapter's core correction to the false growth mindset: appreciate real effort, but never be content with effort that yields no further benefits, and instead figure out why the effort is ineffective and guide the student toward new strategies and resources. Option 0 is effort praise as a consolation prize, which Dweck says is like the failed self-esteem movement, making kids feel good when they are not learning. Option 1 labels Marcus with a fixed trait. Option 3 is the empty \"you can do anything\" reassurance that puts the onus on the student without giving skills or resources."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Coaching Philosophies: Knight, Wooden, and Summitt","visual_frame_description":"A sports-documentary control room with three large screens: one shows a furious coach berating a player in a locker room, one shows a quiet coach turning out gym lights, and one shows a champion team being routed on its home court. A three-column sorter labels each scene by coaching philosophy.","player_mission":"Sort each coaching behavior into the correct column: fixed-minded intimidation coaching, growth-minded preparation coaching, or success-infected complacency. Classify every scene correctly to distinguish how mindsets play out in the coaches' decisions.","key_concepts":["Bobby Knight motivated his players not through respect for them but through intimidation, through fear of his judgments and explosions.","Knight's Holy Grail was the mistake-free game, and he was incapable of accepting failure because coaching gave him his identity.","Knight had a fixed mindset about himself and his coaching ability, so his team had to prove his ability every time out.","Knight won three championship teams, but at other times players and the team as a whole broke down, and players escaped by transferring or going early to the pros.","John Wooden asked not \"Did I win? Did I lose?\" but \"Did I make my best effort?\", and he coached that if so, \"You may be outscored but you will never lose.\"","Wooden did not tolerate coasting; when players coasted in practice he turned out the lights and said, \"Gentlemen, practice is over.\"","Wooden gave equal time and attention to all players regardless of initial skills, and unheralded recruits blossomed into starters.","Wooden refused to have any player's number retired while he coached, because the jersey and the number never belong to just one single player.","Pat Summitt found that success was the enemy: \"Success lulls you. It makes the most ambitious of us complacent and sloppy.\"","Pat Riley called the same complacency the \"disease of me,\" thinking you are the success and chucking the discipline that got you there."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix contrasts three coaching stances: fixed-minded intimidation that demands mistake-free games (Knight), growth-minded coaching that asks for full effort and develops every player (Wooden), and success-infection complacency that lulls teams into sloppiness (Summitt and Riley). Dweck argues that the fixed coach's judgment and fear can produce championships but also collapse and escape, while the growth coach's preparation and equal treatment develops players for life.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"A coach's players ask him, \"Coach, did we win tonight?\" He replies, \"Did I make my best effort? That's the correct question.\" To which column does this coach belong?","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset coach (judgment, intimidation, mistake-free games)","column_b_label":"Growth-mindset coach (full preparation, full effort, equal treatment)","column_c_label":"Success-infected coach (complacency, disease of me)","rows":[{"label":"A coach's Holy Grail is the mistake-free game and players are demeaned for losing (Bobby Knight).","col":"a"},{"label":"A coach turns out the practice lights when players coast and says, \"Gentlemen, practice is over\" (John Wooden).","col":"b"},{"label":"A coach says, \"You may be outscored but you will never lose,\" if you made your best effort (John Wooden).","col":"b"},{"label":"A team that just won the championship stops working and gets crushed at home by Stanford (Tennessee after 1996).","col":"c"},{"label":"A coach leaves a Tampax in a player's locker to make a point about toughness (Bobby Knight).","col":"a"},{"label":"A coach promises a backup to Bill Walton a pro contract, develops him, and he becomes rookie of the year (John Wooden).","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The best-effort question belongs to the fixed coach who demands mistake-free perfection.","It belongs to the success-infected coach who lulls players into complacency.","No real coach would ever ask this question; it is only a slogan."],"explanation":"The \"Did I make my best effort?\" question is John Wooden's, the straight-ahead growth-minded coach who asked for full preparation and full effort rather than mistake-free games. Knight's Holy Grail was the mistake-free game and he motivated through intimidation and fear, while Tennessee's post-championship complacency, what Pat Summitt called \"success lulls you\" and Pat Riley called the \"disease of me,\" is the success-infection pattern. Wooden also gave equal time to all players and developed them, exemplified by the backup who became rookie of the year."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Boss Battle: Bobby Knight and the Fixed-Mindset Coach","visual_frame_description":"The scene is Assembly Hall, Indiana, during the \"season on the brink.\" A dark locker room door swings open and Coach Knight, red-faced, berates Daryl Thomas, a big, fast player coaches call a \"million dollar body,\" delivering a three-year assessment in front of the team. Steve Alford sits silently; the air is heavy with fear and judgment.","player_mission":"Defeat the fixed-mindset boss by first diagnosing why Knight explodes at his own players after a loss, then choosing a coaching intervention that can reach a sensitive, talented player without humiliation. Correct diagnosis and intervention win the stage.","key_concepts":["Bobby Knight was incapable of accepting failure; every defeat was personal because coaching gave him his identity.","Knight had a fixed mindset about himself and his coaching ability, so his team had to prove his ability every time out.","Knight motivated players through intimidation and fear of his judgments and explosions.","Knight's demeaning of Daryl Thomas and the Tampax in a player's locker came from the fixed mindset.","Knight's Holy Grail was the mistake-free game, which made mistakes intolerable.","Intimidation sometimes worked, Knight had three championship teams, but at other times players and the team broke down.","In the \"season on the brink,\" the team collapsed at the end of the season, and players escaped by transferring, breaking rules, or going early to the pros.","Steve Alford could use Knight's basketball wisdom but described the poisonous atmosphere drowning his love of the game.","Knight never seemed to analyze his motivational strategies when they were not working, never asking whether a player like Daryl Thomas needed another kind of incentive besides ridicule.","Isiah Thomas said there were times he wanted to shoot Knight and other times he wanted to hug him, and Dweck says she would not count as an unqualified success if her best student had considered shooting her."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the fixed-mindset coach: Knight's motivation through intimidation and his demand for mistake-free games flow from a fixed mindset about his own coaching identity, making every loss a personal verdict. Dweck notes the approach can win championships but also produces player breakdown, escape, and lost love of the game.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests whether you can recognize the fixed mindset operating inside a highly successful coach: Knight genuinely believed his players could develop, but his fixed mindset about himself made every loss a personal failure, so he motivated through judgment and intimidation and demanded mistake-free games.","stage_1_observation":"After a loss, the locker room door swings open and you watch Coach Knight single out Daryl Thomas and berate him in front of the team. The next day a Tampax appears in a player's locker. Steve Alford says the atmosphere is poisonous and that Coach's Holy Grail is the mistake-free game.","stage_1_question":"Why does Knight explode at his own players after a defeat, according to John Feinstein's account in the book?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because Knight was incapable of accepting failure: every defeat was personal, the team he had selected and coached was his product and proved his ability, so a loss obliterated his identity and he judged the players who let him down.","stage_2_intervention":"The season collapses and players sit around fantasizing about the schools they should have chosen. You must advise a young assistant coach on how to reach a sensitive, talented player like Daryl Thomas. Knight's methods, the humiliation, the locker gift, and the jersey yank, have broken the player down on the basketball court. Choose the intervention.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Give the player honest, respectful, process-focused coaching instead of humiliation: separate the performance from the person, analyze what went wrong in terms of effort and preparation, and build a plan for improvement. The chapter shows Knight never analyzed his motivational strategies when they failed, never asking whether a player like Thomas needed a different incentive besides ridicule, so the growth-minded alternative is to diagnose and teach rather than to judge and punish."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Boss Battle: John Wooden and Full Effort Coaching","visual_frame_description":"The scene is the B.O. Barn, UCLA's crowded, dark, poorly ventilated practice gym in Wooden's early years. Practice has barely begun when players start coasting through drills. Coach Wooden stops, switches off the lights, and says, \"Gentlemen, practice is over.\" Across the court, a recruit Wooden privately thought would never contribute is getting the same coaching as a future star.","player_mission":"Defeat the growth-minded boss scenario by first identifying what Wooden demanded from his players, then choosing the right move after he pulls Fred Slaughter in the national championship game. Correct identification and intervention win the stage.","key_concepts":["John Wooden led UCLA to ten NCAA championships and an eighty-eight-game winning streak.","Wooden's rule: apply yourself each day to becoming a little better, and over time you will become a lot better.","Wooden did not ask for mistake-free games; he asked for full preparation and full effort.","Wooden's correct question was \"Did I make my best effort?\", and if so, \"You may be outscored but you will never lose.\"","Wooden did not tolerate coasting; when players coasted in practice he turned out the lights and said, \"Gentlemen, practice is over.\"","Wooden gave equal time and attention to all players regardless of initial skill, and his unheralded recruits became starters.","Wooden refused to have any player's number retired because the jersey and the number never belong to just one single player.","When Wooden pulled Fred Slaughter in the championship game, Fred understood and said he was disappointed but not upset, and was happy for Doug.","Wooden's philosophy: \"For me, concern, compassion, and consideration were always priorities of the highest order.\"","Wooden was average in basketball tactics but exceptional at analyzing and motivating players, preparing them for life as well as basketball."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the growth-minded coaching principle that effort and preparation, not mistake-free perfection, are the measure of a player and the guarantee of development. Dweck contrasts Wooden's full preparation and full effort and his equal treatment of all players with the fixed coach's demand for mistake-free games, noting that Wooden's players credited him with preparing them for life.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the growth-minded coaching alternative: John Wooden's rule was to apply yourself each day to becoming a little better, to demand full preparation and full effort rather than mistake-free games, and to give all players equal coaching regardless of initial talent.","stage_1_observation":"Practice has barely begun when the players start coasting through drills. Coach Wooden stops, switches off the lights, and says, \"Gentlemen, practice is over.\" Later you watch him give equal time to two unheralded recruits he privately thought would never contribute, both of whom become starters.","stage_1_question":"What was John Wooden's coaching Holy Grail, the thing he demanded from his players?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Full preparation and full effort. Wooden rejected the question \"Did I win? Did I lose?\" in favor of \"Did I make my best effort?\", and he did not tolerate coasting because it wasted the day's opportunity to become better.","stage_2_intervention":"UCLA has just won its first national championship, beating Duke to finish the season thirty wins and zero losses. But Wooden is troubled: he pulled Fred Slaughter, who started every game, when the game went badly, and left in the replacement, Doug, who played well. As Wooden leaves the press conference, he heads to find Fred. You must advise Wooden's next move.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Go to Fred at once and show him that being pulled from one game does not erase his season or his worth. Wooden rushed to find Fred in the dressing room, where Fred said he understood, was disappointed but not upset, and was happy for Doug. For Wooden, concern, compassion, and consideration were always priorities of the highest order, and it is exactly this move that contrasts with a fixed coach like Knight, who would never have rushed to console Daryl Thomas."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"True vs. False Growth Mindset: Sorting Misunderstandings","visual_frame_description":"A school hallway in a district that has adopted the mindset message. Speech bubbles drift from teachers and parents: \"I'm open-minded, so I have a growth mindset,\" \"I tell my students they can do anything,\" and \"I praised his effort all year.\" A three-column sorter awaits each claim.","player_mission":"Sort each mindset claim into the correct column: true growth mindset, false growth mindset, or fixed mindset. Classify every claim correctly to show that using growth language is not the same as embodying growth-minded deeds.","key_concepts":["A growth mindset at its core is about believing people can develop their abilities.","Many people take what they like about themselves, like being open-minded or flexible, and call it a growth mindset.","Many people believe a growth mindset is only about effort, especially praising effort.","The process to be appreciated includes more than effort: hard work, trying new strategies, and seeking input from others.","We should never praise a process that is not there; the first question when effort praise fails is whether the child actually tried hard.","Effort praise used as a consolation prize when kids are not learning is a concern of even greater concern to Dweck than praise of absent effort.","A growth mindset does not mean telling kids they can do anything without helping them gain the skills and resources to make progress.","Blaming kids for having a fixed mindset absolves adults of responsibility for creating growth-minded environments.","In truth we are all a mixture of fixed and growth mindsets, and the task is to understand what triggers the fixed mindset.","Adults' mindsets are in their heads and are not directly visible to children; overt actions speak far louder and are what children pick up on."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix sorts mindset claims into true growth mindset, false growth mindset, and fixed mindset, testing the chapter's catalogue of misunderstandings: equating open-mindedness with growth, reducing growth to effort, praising effort as a consolation prize, and empty \"you can do anything\" proclamations. Dweck argues that genuine growth-mindedness is passed on through deeds, process praise tied to learning, treating setbacks as opportunities, and teaching for understanding, not through a belief sitting in an adult's head.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Principal Osei overhears a teacher say, \"I've always had a growth mindset. I always tell my students, You can do anything!\" In which column does this statement belong?","column_a_label":"True growth mindset (developing abilities through hard work, new strategies, and help)","column_b_label":"False growth mindset (a misunderstanding that borrows the growth language)","column_c_label":"Fixed mindset (traits are permanent and are being judged)","rows":[{"label":"\"You can do anything!\" said with no skills, strategies, or resources offered.","col":"b"},{"label":"\"I praise my child's effort but it's not working,\" when the child was not really trying.","col":"b"},{"label":"Effort praise handed out as a consolation prize to a child who tried hard but is not learning.","col":"b"},{"label":"\"I won because I have talent, therefore I will keep winning.\"","col":"c"},{"label":"Calling yourself open-minded or flexible and treating that as having a growth mindset.","col":"b"},{"label":"\"Show me what you've done, let's try to understand how you're thinking, then let's figure out what you should try next.\"","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["It belongs in the fixed column because it labels children as permanently capable.","It belongs in the true growth column because it expresses strong belief in children's potential.","It belongs in no column because any talk of growth is automatically effective."],"explanation":"The \"you can do anything\" proclamation is a false growth mindset: belief in children's potential only helps when adults give them the skills and resources to make progress, and otherwise it is an empty reassurance that puts the onus on the student. The false-growth category also covers calling flexibility a growth mindset, praising effort that is not there, effort praise as a consolation prize, and process praise tied to no outcome. \"I won because I have talent\" is a fixed-mindset attribution, while \"show me what you've done, let's understand how you're thinking\" is genuine growth-minded teaching for understanding."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Parental Ideals and Conditional Love","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen collage of three households: a father holding a picture of an ideal son while his teenage boy looks away, a high school senior being groomed toward one university, and a young tennis star standing beside a smiling parent. A three-column sorter labels each scene.","player_mission":"Sort each parental behavior into the correct column: growth-minded ideal, fixed-mindset ideal, or conditional love. Classify every scene correctly to distinguish a mold a child can never fit from love granted only on the parent's terms.","key_concepts":["Fixed-mindset students described ideals that could not be worked toward, like the ideal student who comes in with innate talent.","These fixed ideals disrupted students' thinking, made them procrastinate and give up, and stressed them out because they could never measure up.","Growth-minded students described ideals like expanding their knowledge and ways of thinking, with grades as a means to continue to grow.","Growth-minded students felt they were working toward their ideal and found it inspiring.","A fixed-mindset ideal asks children to fit the mold of the brilliant, talented child or be deemed unworthy, with no room for error or for their individuality.","Nicholas felt his father compared him to a picture of an ideal son and was deeply disappointed, a message that leaked out in tone, words, and silence and left a permanent feeling of failure.","Sandy's parents wanted the label of a Harvard admission, not learning, and her record filled with A's and F's after she failed to get in.","Mark's parents refused to let him attend Stuyvesant High School because they had heard it was hard to get into Harvard from there.","John McEnroe's father lived for his son's junior career, and McEnroe says he never felt he loved tennis the way other athletes truly love their sport.","Tiger Woods's father was ambitious but made being a good person the goal: \"If Tiger had wanted to be a plumber, I wouldn't have minded, as long as he was a hell of a plumber.\""],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix sorts parental ideals and love messages into growth-minded ideals, fixed-mindset ideals, and conditional love, distinguishing a mold a child can never fit from love granted only on the parent's terms. Dweck contrasts fixed-minded parents like McEnroe's father and Sandy's Harvard-obsessed parents with Tiger Woods's father, who made being a good person the goal and left his son's love of the sport free to grow.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"In Ginott's account, seventeen-year-old Nicholas says, \"In my father's mind there is a picture of an ideal son. When he compares him to me, he is deeply disappointed.\" To which column does Nicholas's father's ideal belong?","column_a_label":"Growth-minded ideal (something to strive toward and grow into)","column_b_label":"Fixed-mindset ideal (an innate-talent mold you either fit or not)","column_c_label":"Conditional love (we love you only if you succeed on our terms)","rows":[{"label":"The ideal successful student \"comes in with innate talent\" (fixed-mindset college students).","col":"b"},{"label":"A successful student \"whose primary goal is to expand their knowledge and their ways of thinking\" (growth-minded students).","col":"a"},{"label":"Sandy's parents: \"We will love and respect you only if you go to Harvard.\"","col":"c"},{"label":"McEnroe's father lived for his son's junior career, and his love depended on the success he craved.","col":"c"},{"label":"John McEnroe says he never truly felt he loved tennis the way other athletes love their sport.","col":"b"},{"label":"Growth-minded parents say, \"I am amazed at the incredible person my child has become.\"","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["It belongs in the growth column because the father had high hopes for Nicholas.","It belongs in the conditional-love column because the father loved Nicholas.","It belongs in no column because ideals are always helpful to children."],"explanation":"Nicholas's father held a fixed-mindset ideal, a picture of an ideal son that Nicholas could not live up to, revealed in his tone, his words, and his silence, and it left a permanent feeling of failure. Fixed-mindset ideals are things you either have or you don't, and they demoralize rather than guide. Conditional love is the distinct message \"we love you only if you succeed on my terms,\" seen with Sandy's Harvard-or-nothing parents and McEnroe's father, while growth-minded parents give children an ideal they can strive toward and say, \"I am amazed at the incredible person my child has become.\""}]},{"id":"ch13","title":"Chapter 8: Changing Mindsets","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Judging Monologue vs. the Learning Monologue","visual_frame_description":"Three labeled columns float over a split-screen classroom scene. On the left, a student named Alana sits under a red banner reading 'Judging Monologue,' a thought bubble above her head flashing 'This means I'm a loser.' On the right, a green banner reads 'Learning Monologue' above a student jotting notes. A third column shows a therapist's office where a clinician holds a clipboard beside a couch. A stream of speech-bubble cards drifts down the middle of the screen for the player to drag into the columns.","player_mission":"Drag each thought, reaction, and strategy card into the column it belongs to: the fixed-mindset judging monologue, the growth-mindset learning monologue, or the cognitive-therapy evidence reframe. Judge each card on its own using the book's descriptions of the two internal monologues and of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy.","key_concepts":["The fixed mindset creates an internal monologue focused on judging, such as 'This means I'm a loser' or 'This means my partner is selfish.'","People with a growth mindset constantly monitor what is going on, but their monologue asks 'What can I learn from this?' and 'How can I improve?'","Aaron Beck realized in the 1960s that his clients' automatic beliefs flashed through their minds just before waves of anxiety or depression.","Beck found he could teach people to hear and work with these beliefs, and this gave birth to cognitive therapy.","Cognitive therapy teaches people to rein in extreme judgments by asking 'What is the evidence for and against your conclusion?'","Cognitive therapy makes judgments more realistic and optimistic but does not confront the assumption that traits are fixed.","Mindsets frame the running account taking place in people's heads and guide the whole interpretation process.","The growth mindset is attuned to implications for learning and constructive action, such as 'How can I help my partner do this better?'","Cognitive therapy does not escort people out of the framework of judgment and into the framework of growth.","Chapter 8 is about changing the internal monologue from a judging one to a growth-oriented one.","When things go wrong, young children with the fixed mindset feel powerless and incapable.","Some people put extreme interpretations on events and then react with exaggerated feelings of anxiety, depression, anger, or superiority."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the shift in internal monologue: the fixed mindset evaluates every event as a verdict about the self, while the growth mindset interprets the same events for their learning value. The book argues that cognitive therapy can temper extreme judgments but cannot change the underlying belief that traits are fixed.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Alana, a community-college student, failed her first chemistry exam and flashed the automatic thought 'I'm stupid.' She asks you to sort a stream of her thoughts, reactions, and strategies into the correct columns. Which column does the thought 'I'm stupid' belong to?","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset judging monologue","column_b_label":"Growth-mindset learning monologue","column_c_label":"Cognitive-therapy evidence reframe","rows":[{"label":"This means I'm a loser.","col":"a"},{"label":"What can I learn from this? How can I improve?","col":"b"},{"label":"List the evidence for and against the conclusion that I'm stupid.","col":"c"},{"label":"This means my partner is selfish.","col":"a"},{"label":"How can I help my partner do this better?","col":"b"},{"label":"Recount past successes to refute the negative judgment.","col":"c"},{"label":"I'm a better person than they are.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["It belongs to the growth monologue because Alana is reflecting on her performance.","It belongs to cognitive therapy because the thought is automatic and unconscious.","It belongs to all three columns since every mindset judges outcomes."],"explanation":"The fixed mindset's monologue is a judging one, so a global self-label like 'I'm stupid' sorts to column a. Learning-monologue statements ask what can be learned or improved, while cognitive-therapy moves like examining the evidence temper the judgment without leaving the framework of judgment; the book stresses that only mindset change completes the escape from judging."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Willpower: A Fixed Trait or a Skill That Needs Help","visual_frame_description":"A kitchen table in the days before a high school reunion. Nathan, a middle-aged man, stares at a half-eaten plate of lasagna while a golden scale icon hovers nearby. Three columns label his possible approaches: 'Fixed Willpower,' 'Growth Self-Control Strategies,' and 'Concrete Plans (Gollwitzer).' Meal cards, strategy cards, and plan cards float across the screen for sorting.","player_mission":"Sort each statement and tactic into the column that best matches the book's account of willpower: the fixed belief that you either have willpower or you don't, the growth-mindset approach of learning and practicing strategies, or Gollwitzer-style concrete when-where-how plans.","key_concepts":["Some people think about dieting, smoking, or anger in a fixed-mindset way: if you are strong you can do it, and if you are weak you can't.","Fixed-mindset dieters make firm resolutions but take no special measures to make sure they succeed.","Nathan's 'I have to be strong' approach failed because he refused to use simple strategies like putting food aside at the start of a meal.","Nathan ended up on a liquid crash diet, lost weight for the reunion, and put back more than he lost afterward.","People in a growth mindset realize that to succeed they will need to learn and practice strategies that work for them.","Growth-minded dieters keep desserts out of the house, decide in advance what to order in restaurants, schedule a weekly splurge, and consider exercising more.","Peter Gollwitzer's research shows that vowing, even intense vowing, is often useless for follow-through.","Concrete plans that specify when, where, and how you will do something lead to very high levels of follow-through.","Growth-minded people think actively about maintenance and about what habits will continue the gains they have achieved.","When growth-minded people slip, they ask what they can learn and what they will do next time rather than beating themselves up.","The book concludes that willpower is not just a thing you have or don't have; willpower needs help.","A lapse in a growth mindset is a reminder that you are an unfinished human being, not a verdict on your character."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the attribution of self-control: fixed-mindset dieters attribute success to an all-or-nothing trait called willpower and therefore take no protective measures, while growth-minded dieters treat self-control as a skill that needs strategies. The book pairs this with Gollwitzer's finding that vivid concrete plans, not vows, drive follow-through.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Nathan wants to lose weight before his twenty-fifth high school reunion and keeps insisting, 'No, I have to be strong.' In the book, which view does Nathan's insistence reflect?","column_a_label":"Fixed-mindset willpower belief","column_b_label":"Growth-mindset self-control strategy","column_c_label":"Gollwitzer-style concrete plan","rows":[{"label":"If I'm strong and have willpower, I can do it; if I'm weak, I can't.","col":"a"},{"label":"Put part of the meal aside at the beginning or have the restaurant wrap it up.","col":"b"},{"label":"Tomorrow during my break, I'll get a cup of tea, close my office door, and call the gym.","col":"c"},{"label":"After every slip, ordering dessert either to seal the failure or to lift the mood.","col":"a"},{"label":"Keep desserts out of the house and think in advance about what to order in restaurants.","col":"b"},{"label":"On Wednesday morning right after I brush my teeth, I'll sit down and start my report.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["It reflects a growth mindset because he is making a serious resolution to change.","It reflects a Gollwitzer plan because he has committed to a weight-loss goal.","It reflects denial because he is hiding his weight problem from himself."],"explanation":"Nathan's refusal to use strategies reveals the fixed belief that willpower is an all-or-nothing trait, so his approach sorts to column a. Growth-minded people adopt protective strategies (column b), while Gollwitzer's work shows that only vivid, concrete when-where-how plans (column c) reliably produce follow-through."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Entitlement, Denial, and the Growth Step in Career Setbacks","visual_frame_description":"An office cubicle on a low floor of a high-rise. A young professional named Elena, arms crossed, scowls at a promotions board that skips her name. Three columns hover beside her: 'Entitlement,' 'Denial,' and 'Growth-Mindset Step.' A Groundhog Day movie poster hangs on the wall to hint at the book's Bill Murray reference, and a stack of company training manuals sits at the edge of her desk.","player_mission":"Sort each thought and action into the column it belongs to: entitlement (the fixed mindset that the world owes you), denial (running from flaws), or the growth-mindset step (effort, learning, and helping others).","key_concepts":["Many people with a fixed mindset think the world needs to change, not them, and feel entitled to a better job, house, or spouse.","The fixed mindset tells people that because of who they are, they should automatically be thrust into the upper levels of the business.","Entitled workers believe people should see their talents and reward them, and when they don't, it's not fair.","A growth-minded worker considers working harder and being more helpful to people at work.","Growth-minded workers use their time to learn more about the business instead of resenting their low status.","All of the top growth-mindset CEOs knew their companies from top to bottom, inside out, and upside down.","Growth-minded people begin to see colleagues as collaborators toward a common goal rather than adversaries out to deny them what they deserve.","Denial is a fixed-mindset pattern of running away from problems because if their life is flawed, then they are flawed.","The book compares the entitled worker's reorientation to Bill Murray's Groundhog Day experience of reliving and gradually improving the same day.","The cloak of specialness is really a suit of armor people build to feel safe, strong, and worthy, and it later constricts their growth.","Giving up the idea of being superior can be frightening because people dread becoming an ordinary, run-of-the-mill human being.","As Elena becomes more growth-minded, she is amazed at how people start to help and support her."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests two fixed-mindset defenses against feedback, entitlement and denial, and contrasts them with the growth-mindset step of learning, effort, and relationship-building. The book argues that the cloak of specialness protects the self early on but later sends people into self-defeating battles and cuts them off from mutual relationships.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Elena believes her boss 'is threatened by me' and that she should automatically be thrust into the upper levels of the business. Which fixed-mindset pattern does this belief belong to?","column_a_label":"Entitlement (the world owes you)","column_b_label":"Denial (running from flaws)","column_c_label":"Growth-mindset step","rows":[{"label":"With my talent, I shouldn't have to work like this; I should be up there with the big boys.","col":"a"},{"label":"If my life were flawed, then I'd be flawed, so it's easier to make believe everything's all right.","col":"b"},{"label":"Learn the nuts and bolts of the company that could help me on my rise to the top.","col":"c"},{"label":"My boss is threatened by me; people should see my talents and reward me.","col":"a"},{"label":"Choosing to misinterpret a partner's signs that the marriage is ending.","col":"b"},{"label":"Help colleagues develop in ways they value and turn them into collaborators.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["It belongs to denial because Elena refuses to admit her performance is weak.","It belongs to the growth-mindset step because she is questioning her boss's judgment.","It belongs to all three because every fixed mindset blames others for setbacks."],"explanation":"Elena's complaint that the world should recognize her talents is the entitlement pattern, so it sorts to column a. Denial (column b) is the fixed-mindset habit of fleeing from flaws, while the growth-mindset step (column c) redirects effort toward learning the business, helping colleagues, and building the relationships that actually produce advancement."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Four Steps of the Journey to a Growth Mindset","visual_frame_description":"A winding mountain path with four glowing waypoint stones labeled 'Embrace,' 'Trigger,' 'Name,' and 'Educate.' A hiker in a yellow jacket stands at the trailhead. Above the path, three columns sort statements: 'Step 1 Embrace,' 'Step 2 Triggers,' and 'Steps 3-4 Name and Educate.' Journal pages from Stanford freshmen drift down as sorting cards.","player_mission":"Sort each statement into the correct step of the mindset journey: embracing your fixed mindset, becoming aware of your triggers, or naming and educating your fixed-mindset persona.","key_concepts":["Step 1 of the journey is to embrace your fixed mindset, because everyone is a mixture of growth and fixed mindsets.","Accepting that some fixed mindset dwells within is 'welcome to the human race,' not a shameful admission.","Step 2 is to become aware of your fixed-mindset triggers, such as big new challenges, dead ends, decisive failure, or encountering someone better than you.","Under pressure, one person's fixed-mindset persona fills his head with noise and keeps him from paying attention to the work.","Step 3 is to give your fixed-mindset persona a name, like Gertrude, Duane, Ianni, Sugardaddy, Henrietta, Z, or Dale Denton.","Susan Mackie's financial executives gave their personas names and then talked about how Duane brings out Ianni.","Once the executives understood each other's triggers and personas, the morale in their unit went up by leaps and bounds.","Step 4 is to educate the persona: thank it for its input, then ask it to come along with you on the journey.","When you hit a setback, don't suppress or ban the persona; let it do its song and dance, then talk about how you plan to learn and go forward.","The fixed-mindset persona was born to protect you and keep you safe, but it has developed some very limiting ways of doing that.","A grade-school boy who wouldn't participate finally named his persona Dumping Dan, and after that his growth was tremendous.","Not one of the sixteen Stanford freshmen in Dweck's seminar claimed to have no triggers or persona."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the four-step journey to a true growth mindset: embracing the fixed mindset, mapping triggers, naming the fixed-mindset persona, and educating it. The book's evidence is qualitative, drawn from Dweck's Stanford freshman seminar and Susan Mackie's work with executives, so the steps are a clinical procedure rather than an experimentally validated sequence.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"A Stanford freshman writes about her fixed-mindset persona, Gertrude, who appears at 'any whiff of failure or imperfection.' In the book's four-step journey, which step does giving the persona a name belong to?","column_a_label":"Step 1: Embrace your fixed mindset","column_b_label":"Step 2: Become aware of triggers","column_c_label":"Steps 3-4: Name and educate the persona","rows":[{"label":"Welcome to the human race; we all have some fixed mindset within us.","col":"a"},{"label":"My persona shows up when I'm under pressure, when I procrastinate, or when I'm too shy at a party.","col":"b"},{"label":"Meet Gertrude, my cagey, histrionic, self-aggrandizing fixed-mindset persona.","col":"c"},{"label":"It's not a shameful admission to acknowledge that we're a mixture of growth and fixed mindsets.","col":"a"},{"label":"Public failure, criticism, and deadlines summon my persona and I become a harping perfectionist.","col":"b"},{"label":"Thank the persona for its input, then ask, 'Can I count on you to bear with me?'","col":"c"},{"label":"Understanding that everyone has a fixed-mindset persona gives us more compassion for people.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Naming belongs to Step 1 because it is an act of embracing your fixed mindset.","Naming belongs to Step 2 because the persona is defined by its triggers.","Naming belongs to all four steps because the persona is present throughout the journey."],"explanation":"Giving the persona a name is Step 3 of the journey, so the statement sorts to column c, and educating the persona is Step 4. Step 1 statements (column a) accept that everyone carries some fixed mindset, while Step 2 statements (column b) identify the specific triggers that summon the persona."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"False Growth Mindset vs. the Sustained Journey","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen scene. On the left, a manager named Wes sits behind a desk with a smirk, having just stopped using the strategies that improved his son's behavior, while a red 'REVERT' stamp slams down on his report. On the right, a couple, Marlene and Scott, sits on a couch as a conversation bubble about rewarding effort pops over their heads. Three columns sort behaviors: 'False Growth Mindset,' 'True Growth Mindset Journey,' and 'Fixed Mindset.'","player_mission":"Sort each behavior into the column it belongs to: the false growth mindset (using growth strategies without real belief), the true growth-mindset journey (sustained learn-and-help-learn change), or plain fixed-mindset behavior.","key_concepts":["Once a problem improves, people often stop doing what caused it to improve, so changes have to be supported or they go away faster than they appeared.","Alcoholics Anonymous tells members they will always be alcoholics so they won't become complacent and stop doing what they need to do to stay sober.","If someone stays inside a fixed mindset and uses the growth strategies, it can backfire.","Wes praised Mickey's strategies and efforts, but as soon as the turnaround took place he stopped using the strategies and expected the improvement to continue.","When Mickey's behavior lapsed, Wes became even angrier and more punitive than before.","Marlene and Scott stopped jumping on negatives and started rewarding the thoughtful things their partner did, and the love and tenderness they thought were dead returned.","Once the love returned, Marlene and Scott reverted, because in the fixed mindset good people should just act good and good relationships should just unfold.","When the bickering resumed, it was fiercer than ever because it reflected all of their disappointed hopes.","Susan Mackie encountered people who claimed to have a growth mindset but who, upon closer inspection, did not, which Dweck calls the false growth mindset.","Everyone wants to seem enlightened and in the know, which fuels the false growth mindset.","Mindset change moves people from a judge-and-be-judged framework to a learn-and-help-learn framework.","Growth takes plenty of time, effort, and mutual support to achieve and maintain."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the false growth mindset: using growth strategies while still holding fixed beliefs, which backfires when the gains require maintenance. The book argues that true mindset change is not picking up a few pointers but adopting a learn-and-help-learn framework that must be actively maintained.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"Wes tried growth-oriented strategies with his son Mickey, and when the turnaround happened he stopped using them, growing angrier and more punitive than before. In the book, what is Wes's pattern called?","column_a_label":"False growth mindset","column_b_label":"True growth-mindset journey","column_c_label":"Fixed-mindset behavior","rows":[{"label":"Wes used growth strategies only until Mickey improved, then stopped and expected it to continue.","col":"a"},{"label":"A manager claims a total, long-standing growth mindset yet reports having no fixed-mindset triggers.","col":"a"},{"label":"After a setback, a runner asks 'What can I learn from this?' and forms a new concrete plan.","col":"b"},{"label":"Marlene and Scott reverted to bickering once love returned because good relationships should just unfold.","col":"a"},{"label":"A parent shifts family conversation to 'What did you learn today?' and keeps it up nightly for months.","col":"b"},{"label":"A fixed-minded manager punishes an employee rather than helping him work through his difficulties.","col":"c"},{"label":"An employee lets every lapse remind her that she is an unfinished human being.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Wes shows a true growth mindset because he genuinely praised his son's efforts.","Wes shows fixed-mindset behavior from the start because he never tried to change.","Wes's pattern is denial because he refused to admit Mickey had problems."],"explanation":"Wes's case is the book's example of using growth strategies from inside a fixed mindset, which backfires, so it sorts to column a. Sustained learn-and-help-learn behaviors (column b) define the true journey, while actions like punishing rather than helping (column c) are straightforward fixed-mindset behavior."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Maggie the Aspiring Writer Faces Criticism","visual_frame_description":"A sunlit campus corridor outside the registrar's office. Maggie, a 22-year-old undergraduate, clutches an open course catalog showing a creative writing class, her knuckles white. Her friend Priya leans against the wall, arms crossed and smiling. A thought bubble over Maggie's head reads 'If they criticize my work, I was never a real writer.' The scene is calm and ordinary, with students passing in the background.","player_mission":"Advise Priya as she helps Maggie decide whether to register for the creative writing class next term. Choose the response that moves Maggie from her fixed belief about creativity toward the growth mindset the book describes, so she can pursue her long-hidden dream.","scenario_setup":"Maggie has always wanted to be a writer but believes people are inherently artistic or creative and that you cannot improve through effort. She has never taken a writing class or shared her writing because any negative criticism would mean she 'is not a writer inherently.' After hearing her psychology lecture on mindsets, she hesitates outside the registrar's office, torn between protecting her dream and testing it. The stakes are her secret dream of writing and her fear of exposing herself to the possibility that she might not be a natural.","dialogue":["Priya: Maggie, you've been standing here ten minutes with the course catalog open to creative writing. What's stopping you?","Maggie: If I take the class and someone criticizes my work, it just proves I was never a real writer. I'd rather protect the dream.","Priya: So the dream only survives if it stays untested?","Maggie: Basically, yes. Either you're born creative or you're not. I don't think practice changes that.","Priya: What did your psych lecture say about the brain and learning?","Maggie: That the brain is more like a muscle, and it changes and gets stronger when you use it. But writing feels different from math.","Priya: Is it, though? Or is that just the fixed mindset talking?","Maggie: When you put it that way, I hear myself saying 'Don't do it. Don't share your writing. Your dream could be destroyed.'","Priya: And what would it sound like if the other voice answered?","Maggie: Go for it. Make it happen. Develop your skills. I think I'll register."],"choices":["Reassure Maggie that her writing is genuinely talented and that critics are just wrong.","Tell Maggie to register for the class and to share her writing, because the brain grows with practice and effort.","Advise Maggie to keep her writing private until she is sure it is good enough to survive criticism.","Tell Maggie to take the class but to ignore any negative feedback from the teacher."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Belief in the growth mindset: the brain changes and grows like a muscle when you use it, so skill and achievement come through practice and effort.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice echoes the book's Mindset Lectures, where merely learning the growth mindset lets students like Maggie reframe their internal monologue from 'Don't do it' to 'Go for it,' because they stop equating criticism with a verdict on their fixed identity. Choice 0 keeps Maggie dependent on talent labels and external validation; choice 2 preserves the fixed belief that the dream must be protected rather than tested; and choice 3 keeps the judgment framework intact by dodging criticism instead of learning from it.","key_concepts":["Maggie believed people were inherently artistic or creative and that you could not improve through effort.","Her fear was that any negative criticism would mean she was not a writer inherently.","She was too scared to expose herself to the possibility that she might not be a natural.","After listening to the mindset lectures, Maggie decided to register for a creative writing class next term.","Maggie's internal monologue changed from 'Don't do it, protect your dream' to 'Go for it, develop your skills.'","The book reports that students describe how the mindset ideas changed them in all areas of their lives.","Just learning about the growth mindset can cause a big shift in the way people think about themselves and their lives.","The brain is more like a muscle; it changes and gets stronger when you use it.","Scientists have been able to show just how the brain grows and gets stronger when you learn.","Sticking to the growth mindset may not always be easy, but just knowing it gave people another way to be.","The growth mindset freed Maggie from being held captive by an intimidating fantasy about the Great Writer."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"theory_summary":"This scenario tests the power of merely learning about the growth mindset to shift self-attributions about creativity from fixed trait to developable skill. The book presents Maggie's story from Dweck's undergraduate course as evidence that a single idea can reorient a person's internal monologue."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Rejected Graduate Applicant Calls for Feedback","visual_frame_description":"A modest dorm room. Laura Chen, a 26-year-old applicant, sits on the edge of her bed gripping a phone, a rejected admission letter face-down on her desk. A calendar on the wall shows weeks passing. The scene is tense but hopeful as she dials the graduate department she once believed would accept her original and exciting work.","player_mission":"Advise Laura as she makes the call her advisor recommended after her rejection. Choose the response that applies the book's growth-mindset step, so she gathers information that will let her learn from the experience and improve her next application.","scenario_setup":"Laura was rejected by the only graduate school she applied to, a top program she believed her original work would get her into. Days after the rejection letter, her fixed-mindset voice is telling her the admissions committee found her work mediocre and the verdict is in. Now she has steeled herself to call the department and ask how she could improve her application if she applied again. The stakes are her research career and her ability to turn a rejection into learning.","dialogue":["Laura: Hello, my name is Laura Chen, and I applied to your doctoral program this year. I'm not calling to dispute your decision.","Admissions officer: I appreciate that. How can I help you?","Laura: I want to know, if I apply again in the future, how I can improve my application. I would be very grateful if you could give me some feedback along those lines.","Admissions officer: That's an unusual request. Most people either argue or disappear. Give me a couple of days to look your file over again.","Laura: Thank you. Anything at all, what experiences you value or what you look for in letters, would help me target my preparation.","Admissions officer: I'll pull your file and call you back. If I may say so, this call says a lot about your maturity.","Laura: I've been learning that feedback is information, not a verdict. I'd rather act on it than hide from it.","Admissions officer: Well, Laura, after reviewing your file, I think there's room for reconsideration. Let me make a call to the committee."],"choices":["Tell Laura the call was pointless because the committee's verdict is final and she should accept it and move on.","Encourage Laura to insist that the school admit her, since her work was clearly original and exciting.","Urge Laura to make the honest plea for feedback and to use whatever information she gathers to strengthen her next application.","Advise Laura to apologize for calling and reassure her that she was simply a victim of a very competitive year."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"The growth-mindset step: after a setback, gather information that lets you learn from experience and improve for the future.","psychological_breakdown":"Laura's call embodies the book's growth-mindset step after rejection: instead of merely restoring self-esteem with reassuring talk, she reaches out for the information she needs to learn and improve, and the officer is receptive to an honest plea for helpful feedback. Choice 0 abandons learning at the fixed mindset's verdict; choice 1 fights the verdict with entitlement; and choice 3 returns to the rationalizing reassurance that fixes self-esteem without producing growth.","key_concepts":["The rejected applicant made a growth-mindset phone call asking how she could improve her application if she applied again.","The growth-mindset step after rejection is to think about the goal and what steps would keep you on track toward achieving it.","Gathering information about what makes a good application turns rejection into learning.","Nobody scoffs at an honest plea for helpful feedback.","The applicant's call led to her admission because it had been a close call and the department liked her initiative.","In the fixed mindset, restoring self-esteem with reassuring self-talk is the end of the matter; in the growth mindset it is only the first step.","The book notes that the rejected applicant could feel miserable and still reach out for information that would help her improve.","Concrete plans, not vows, drive follow-through: she had to plan when and how to make the call.","The growth-mindset advice changed her reaction from rationalizing to acting on her own development.","Every day people plan to do difficult things but don't do them; vowing, even intense vowing, is often useless.","What works is a vivid, concrete plan about when, where, and how you will act."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"theory_summary":"This scenario tests the growth-mindset response to rejection: rather than ending the episode by restoring self-esteem, the person seeks feedback that will enable learning and improvement. The book's true-story example shows the plea for feedback winning over both rationalization and resentment."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Overflowing Garbage: Controlling Anger in a Partnership","visual_frame_description":"A warm suburban kitchen in the evening. Marcus, 41, stands near the back door with his shoulders slumped, while his wife Dana sits stiffly on the porch bench outside, arms wrapped around herself. An overflowing trash can sits by the counter between them. The scene is quiet and strained, with soft kitchen light spilling out onto the porch.","player_mission":"Advise Marcus as he tries to repair the blowup and break the cycle. Choose the response that applies the book's growth-mindset approach to anger and self-control, turning the next episode into a learning process instead of a carbon copy.","scenario_setup":"Marcus loves his wife Dana but has a hot trigger: when she lets the garbage overflow, he feels personally betrayed, moves from 'I've told you a thousand times' to 'You never do anything right,' and escalates until Dana has to leave the house to get out of range of his fury. Tonight the escalation already happened, Dana is sitting stiffly on the back porch, and Marcus wants to break the cycle using the book's growth-mindset approach to self-control. The stakes are the couple's peace and whether the next blowup will repeat exactly.","dialogue":["Marcus: Dana, can I sit with you?","Dana: Depends. Are you here to apologize, or to tell me what's wrong with me again?","Marcus: I am sorry. When I saw the trash overflowing, something in me flipped. I treated you like a villain.","Dana: I brace for it every time. I go quiet and you take the silence as proof I feel superior, and then it gets worse.","Marcus: You're right. I read your quiet as 'you don't matter.' That's what sets me off, feeling devalued.","Dana: I never meant it that way. The trash was just Tuesday. I didn't know it was a test.","Marcus: It wasn't supposed to be. I've been treating my own rules as tests of whether you respect me. I want to change that.","Dana: What would that look like?","Marcus: Next time, I tell you matter-of-factly that it makes me feel unimportant, instead of attacking your character. And I need you to tell me you care how I feel and will try to be more watchful.","Dana: I can do that. I do care, Marcus."],"choices":["Tell Marcus he should simply resolve to stay calm next time, since a strong person can control anger on willpower alone.","Advise Marcus to demand that Dana acknowledge she is at fault so he won't feel disrespected again.","Teach Marcus concrete strategies: state how it makes you feel, leave the room and write down your ugliest thoughts followed by what is probably really happening, and loosen some of his rules.","Tell Marcus to bottle up the feeling until the urge to criticize passes."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"The growth mindset and self-control: self-control is learned through strategies, not all-or-nothing willpower.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice follows the book's anger guidance: name the devaluation ('I feel unimportant'), communicate it matter-of-factly, use the write-down-ugly-thoughts technique, and loosen rules that are really tests of respect, turning the next episode into a learning process. Choice 0 is the fixed-mindset willpower error; choice 1 turns the conflict into a blame verdict; and choice 3 suppresses the feeling without strategies, guaranteeing another explosion.","key_concepts":["Anger control fails when people vow to be different next time but plan no strategies.","In the fixed mindset, people believe that simply keeping the good person in the forefront will prevent a flare-up.","When the spouse shirks tasks, the angry partner may feel devalued and disrespected, as though told 'You're not important.'","The angry partner takes the spouse's silence as evidence of superiority, which fuels the escalation.","Spouses can't read your mind, so you have to matter-of-factly tell them how their action makes you feel.","You can request directly: 'Please tell me that you care how I feel and you'll try to be more watchful.'","A strategy is to leave the room and write down your ugliest thoughts, followed by what is probably really happening.","People can learn to loosen up on some of their rules, once each rule is no longer a test of the partner's respect.","Dropping good-bad, strong-weak thinking makes people better able to learn useful strategies that help with self-control.","Every lapse doesn't spell doom; it's a reminder that you're an unfinished human being.","Growth-minded people ask 'What can I learn from this?' after setbacks instead of beating themselves up."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"theory_summary":"This scenario tests the growth-mindset approach to anger control: self-control is a set of learnable strategies rather than an all-or-nothing trait, so preventing flare-ups requires planning rather than mere good intentions. The book argues that dropping good-bad thinking lets people learn the specific techniques that stop the escalation loop."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Duane in Full Bloom: A Team Lead Educates His Persona","visual_frame_description":"A dim product-launch war room at 9 PM. Andre, the team lead, looms over a whiteboard covered in sticky notes, his hands on his hips. His team member Iris sits at a desk with her shoulders hunched, looking down. Two small thought bubbles float over their heads, one labeled 'Duane' and one labeled 'Ianni.' The pressure of the deadline is visible in every face.","player_mission":"Advise Andre as he manages his fixed-mindset persona during the deadline crunch. Choose the response that applies the book's naming-and-educating technique, turning a judgment spiral into a moment of mutual support.","scenario_setup":"It is late in product-launch week. Andre feels his fixed-mindset persona 'Duane' taking over: he is supercritical of everyone, bossy, and demanding rather than supportive, and morale is cratering. His team member Iris is about to respond the way she always does, with her own persona 'Ianni' making her cowering and anxious, which infuriates Duane further. Andre has just learned from Susan Mackie's executive work that naming the persona and talking about it can break the loop. The stakes are the launch, the team's morale, and whether Andre can turn a pressure spiral into a learning collaboration.","dialogue":["Iris: Andre, you've rewritten my section three times tonight. Nothing I do seems right.","Andre: That's because we have a deadline, and right now it doesn't look like this team can hit it. Where are the breakthrough ideas?","Iris: I feel like I can't do anything right. I'm starting to shut down.","Andre: Hold on. That's Duane talking, isn't it? When we're in a crunch, Duane shows up and I get supercritical and demanding.","Iris: Duane? You mean the persona from the workshop? Then I should tell you my Ianni is roaring right now. He makes me feel incompetent.","Andre: So Duane brings out Ianni, and Ianni makes me angrier, which makes Duane worse. I'm sorry. This isn't about your work.","Iris: What do you need from us to do your best work?","Andre: I need to hear where you're blocked and what support you need, instead of me judging. Let's walk through your section together."],"choices":["Tell Andre to push harder and demand the team step up, because pressure is when real leaders show their mettle.","Advise Andre to apologize for the criticism and then quietly take over the section himself so the launch won't fail.","Help Andre name what is happening, tell Iris that Duane is in full bloom, ask what the team needs from him, and keep talking to Duane so he can calm down and cut the team some slack.","Suggest Andre set aside the launch concerns and schedule a team-building retreat next month."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Naming and educating the fixed-mindset persona: when pressure triggers the persona, talk about it openly and use it as a cue to ask for and give support.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice follows Steps 3-4 of the journey and the book's Duane-and-Ianni story: acknowledging that Duane is in full bloom, asking the team what they need, and keeping the dialogue with the persona going transforms the pressure spiral into mutual support and restores morale. Choice 0 feeds Duane's judging; choice 1 still distrusts the team and burns Andre out; and choice 3 avoids the immediate trigger rather than educating the persona.","key_concepts":["A financial executive reported that in a crunch 'Duane shows up' and makes him supercritical, bossy, and demanding.","A female team member replied that when Duane shows up, her persona Ianni makes her cowering and anxious, which infuriates Duane.","Once the executives understood each other's triggers and personas, the morale in their unit went up by leaps and bounds.","Step 2 of the journey is to become aware of what triggers your fixed-mindset persona.","Under a deadline, one team lead's persona sits in judgment instead of empowering the team.","The judging persona leads the lead to take over and do much of the work himself, which does not do wonders for team morale.","Step 4 is to educate the persona: when it shows up, don't suppress it, but talk with it about your plan to move forward.","You can tell the team that Duane is in full bloom and ask them what they need from you to do their best work.","The fixed-mindset persona was born to protect you and keep you safe, but it has developed limiting ways of doing that.","Understanding that everyone has a fixed-mindset persona gives us more compassion for people.","Educating the persona teaches it new ways to support you: taking on challenges, bouncing back from failure, and helping others grow."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"theory_summary":"This scenario tests naming and educating the fixed-mindset persona under deadline pressure, based on Dweck's account of Susan Mackie's work with executives. When the persona is triggered, openly acknowledging it and asking for what the team needs converts judgment into support and reverses the morale spiral."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Devastating Journal Reviews: It's Not About You","visual_frame_description":"A quiet home office late at night. Dr. Priya Nair, a young researcher, sits at a desk with a thick stack of single-spaced review pages face-down, her head in her hands. A phone on speaker shows a call from her former advisor. The paper's rejection is written across her face, but the advisor's words on the screen begin to reframe the scene.","player_mission":"Advise the professor as she helps Priya change her mindset about the journal reviews. Choose the response that reframes harsh criticism as information for improvement and drives immediate, concrete revision rather than retreat.","scenario_setup":"Priya has just received the reviews of her thesis research from the top journal in her field: ten or so pages of single-spaced criticism. She felt judged, as though the flawed work meant she was flawed too, and for weeks she has not been able to go near the reviews or work on the paper. Her former advisor phones to help her change her mindset. The stakes are the paper, which represents years of work, and Priya's ability to keep revising instead of retreating.","dialogue":["Professor: Priya, I heard the reviews came back. How are you doing?","Priya: Honestly? I've been avoiding them for three weeks. I read them once and felt like the work was flawed and so was I.","Professor: That's the fixed mindset doing the reading. Look, it's not about you.","Priya: Not about me? They took apart every section of my thesis.","Professor: That's their job. Their job is to find every possible flaw. Your job is to learn from the critique and make the paper even better.","Priya: So when they say the analysis is weak, that's not a verdict on my intelligence?","Professor: It's information. Ten pages of information. Every single criticism tells you what to fix. Nothing in those pages says you are a failure.","Priya: When you frame it that way, I almost feel ready to open the file again.","Professor: Good. Start by listing the criticisms you can actually act on, and revise one section tonight. The plan matters more than the feeling.","Priya: I'll do that. And next time I get a critique, I'll remind myself: oh, that's their job."],"choices":["Tell Priya to take a few more weeks off until she feels ready to face the reviews.","Agree with Priya that the reviewers' criticism means her research was not good enough and she should consider a new field.","Reframe the reviews as the reviewers' job and teach Priya that her job is to learn from the critique and revise, making a concrete plan to act immediately.","Advise Priya to write a rebuttal demanding the reviewers justify each criticism."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Reframing feedback as information for improvement rather than a verdict on the self, combined with the growth-mindset step of forming a concrete plan and acting on it regardless of mood.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice mirrors the book's mentoring moment: 'It's not about you. That's their job. Your job is to learn from the critique and make your paper even better,' which freed the student to revise within hours and stop feeling judged. Choice 0 delays action and waits for feeling better; choice 1 accepts the reviewer's verdict as identity; and choice 3 treats the critique as a battle rather than learning material.","key_concepts":["Research reviews are often ten or so pages of single-spaced criticism, and a paper often represents years of work.","The student was devastated because she felt the flawed work meant she, by extension, was flawed.","The professor reframed the reviews: 'It's not about you. That's their job. Your job is to learn from the critique and make your paper even better.'","Within hours of the reframe, the student was revising her paper, which was warmly accepted.","She reported: 'I never felt judged again. Every time I get that critique, I tell myself, oh, that's their job.'","Even if you feel miserable, you can still take the growth-mindset step and reach out for information that helps you improve.","The plan is the plan: it might be easier to mobilize for action if you felt better, but it doesn't matter.","Depressed students with a growth mindset did more of the constructive thing the worse they felt.","Vivid, concrete plans about when, where, and how lead to high levels of follow-through.","In the fixed mindset, regaining self-esteem ends the episode; in the growth mindset it is only the first step.","The growth mindset allows people to carry forth not judgments and bitterness but new understanding and new skills."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the growth-mindset reframe of harsh feedback: treating criticism as the reviewer's job and a source of information for improvement rather than a verdict on the self. The book's anecdote shows the reframe enabling the student to revise within hours and never feel judged again.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Brainology: The Brain Is a Muscle","visual_frame_description":"A high school classroom during the first workshop session. A projector shows side-by-side pictures of how the density of brain connections changes during the first years of life. Jimmy, the hard-core turned-off student, leans forward with tears in his eyes as the images change; a caption reads 'You can help it grow by using it in the right way.' The other students are quiet, watching him.","player_mission":"Win the battle for Jimmy's mindset. First identify the belief that broke through his fixed mindset, then choose the intervention that keeps him on the growth path, matching how the book says the workshop and Brainology program actually worked.","boss_battle_explanation":"The mindset workshop and the Brainology program teach that the brain is like a muscle: it changes and gets stronger with use, because learning multiplies and strengthens the tiny connections between brain cells. This belief, not study-skill training alone, is what turned students like Jimmy around.","stage_1_observation":"In a high school classroom during the first workshop session, Jimmy, the hard-core turned-off student from chapter 3, leans forward with tears in his eyes after seeing pictures of how the density of brain connections changes during the first years of life. The projector shows how new connections multiply and strengthen when people practice and learn, and how a challenge that once seemed impossible, like speaking a foreign language or doing algebra, becomes easy.","stage_1_question":"What single belief, delivered by the workshop, broke through Jimmy's fixed mindset and produced his tearful outburst?","stage_1_correct_answer":"That intelligence is not fixed at birth: the brain is more like a muscle that grows and gets stronger when you use it, so he doesn't have to be 'dumb.'","stage_2_intervention":"A month later, Jimmy's teacher describes him as a student who never put in extra effort and rarely turned in homework on time. You must choose the intervention that keeps Jimmy on the growth path, matching how the book says the workshop and Brainology worked.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Tell Jimmy 'You're in charge of your mind. You can help it grow by using it in the right way,' and teach him study strategies, because the workshop combined the growth message with application to schoolwork and produced real grade gains.","key_concepts":["The workshop tells students that many people believe a person is born smart, average, or dumb and stays that way for life.","New research shows the brain is more like a muscle; it changes and gets stronger when you use it.","When you learn new things, the tiny connections in the brain actually multiply and get stronger.","The more you challenge your mind to learn, the more your brain cells grow, and hard things become easier.","Nobody laughs at babies and says how dumb they are because they can't talk; they just haven't learned yet.","Jimmy reacted to the workshop with tears and the words 'You mean I don't have to be dumb?'","The workshop told Jimmy he is in charge of his mind and can help it grow by using it in the right way.","Jimmy then stayed up late working for hours to finish an assignment early and earned a B+ after getting C's and lower.","The control workshop taught even more study skills but no mindset message, and its students showed no gains.","Students in the growth-mindset workshop showed a jump in their math grades compared with the control workshop.","The Brainology computer program, with Chris, Dahlia, and Dr. Cerebrus, teaches the same brain lessons through interactive modules.","Students transferred the message into their own words: 'Every time I thought about not doing work I remembered that my neurons could grow.'"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the brain-like-a-muscle message that fuels the mindset workshop: learning physically strengthens the brain through multiplying connections. The book's evidence is that only the growth-mindset workshop, not the extra study-skills control, produced jumps in students' math grades.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"The Precocious Fixed Mindsetter at the Dinner Table","visual_frame_description":"A family dinner table at dusk. A seven-year-old boy sits with his chin lifted, announcing 'I'm a chess champion' while a chessboard lies untouched on a shelf behind him. His parents exchange a knowing glance across the table. Three question cards labeled 'What did you learn today?' float above the scene as the family begins its new ritual.","player_mission":"Win the long battle for your son's mindset. First identify the fixed belief driving his retreat from chess and his cheating at games, then choose the family strategy the book says can actually shift a precocious fixed mindset.","boss_battle_explanation":"The precocious fixed-mindset child is invested in his brain's praises, not in making it grow: he believes losing would prove he is no longer a champion, so he retires from chess and starts cheating at games. You cannot talk him out of the fixed mindset; the family must live the growth mindset instead.","stage_1_observation":"Your seven-year-old son announced 'I'm a chess champion' and retired from the game after watching Searching for Bobby Fischer, because he learned that a champion can lose. He has started cheating at Candy Land and Chutes and Ladders, and he boasts about all the things he can do that other children can't. At the dinner table he tells you he doesn't have to practice, because the teacher shows him something new and he can already do it better than the other kids.","stage_1_question":"Your son's behavior, retiring from chess, cheating at games, boasting, and refusing to practice, is driven by which fixed-mindset belief?","stage_1_correct_answer":"He believes being smart is a fixed gift that must be proven, so losing would expose him as not really a champion; he is invested in singing his brain's praises rather than making it grow.","stage_2_intervention":"Instead of trying to talk your son out of the fixed mindset, you restructure the family dinner conversation. You must pick the approach the book says actually shifts the child's mindset over time.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Go around the dinner table each evening asking every family member, adults included, 'What did you learn today?' 'What mistake did you make that taught you something?' and 'What did you try hard at today?' and answer your son's boasting with 'Yeah, but what did you learn?'","key_concepts":["Some children become truly passionate fixed-mindset believers much earlier than others.","The precocious son concluded that some kids are smart and some are dumb because they have a worse brain.","After watching Searching for Bobby Fischer, the son learned that you could lose and not be a champion anymore, so he retired.","To avoid losing, he started cheating at Candy Land, Chutes and Ladders, and other games.","He was invested in his brain not in making it grow but in singing its praises.","The book's advice is to live the growth mindset rather than try to talk the child out of the fixed mindset.","Dinner-table questions: 'What did you learn today?' 'What mistake did you make that taught you something?' 'What did you try hard at today?'","When the son boasts, the family answers, 'Yeah, but what did you learn?'","When he says everything is easy, the family says, 'That's too bad. You're not learning. Find something harder to do.'","The family dramatizes mistakes that held the key to the solution, telling them like mystery stories.","Eventually the son becomes the mindset watchdog, delighting in catching family members who slip into fixed thinking.","The fixed mindset tempts children with a lifetime of worth and admiration for just sitting there and being who they are."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests how to counter a precocious fixed mindset: since talking the child out of the belief fails, the family must embody the growth mindset through daily practices like the learning-focused dinner table. The book's account is prescriptive rather than experimentally tested, so the outcome rests on the logic of modeling rather than controlled evidence.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."}}]},{"id":"ch15","title":"Recommended Books","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Groupthink in Fixed-Mindset Teams (Wood's Management Study)","visual_frame_description":"A conference room at a fictional office, split-screen view of two three-person management teams huddled over laptops running a simulated furniture company. On the left, the fixed-mindset team members sit stiffly, glancing sideways before anyone speaks, and the chat log is almost silent. On the right, the growth-mindset team leans in, pointing at figures and debating openly. A banner across the top reads 'The Wood Group-Process Study.'","player_mission":"Study the two teams' behavior in the management simulation and classify each observed pattern into the correct column: groupthink, growth-minded 'we-think', or individual skill display. Sort all seven evidence cards into their correct category by clicking the matching column, then confirm the verdict on the fixed-mindset group.","key_concepts":["Irving Janis popularized the term groupthink, defined as everyone in a group starting to think alike, with no one disagreeing and no one taking a critical stance.","Dweck argues groupthink 'often can come right out of a fixed mindset,' citing Robert Wood's study of management groups.","In Wood's study, growth-mindset groups openly stated their honest opinions and expressed their disagreements, while fixed-mindset groups produced 'more like groupthink' than open discussion.","The longer Wood's growth-mindset groups worked, the larger their performance advantage over the fixed-mindset groups became.","Unlimited faith in a talented leader is one route to groupthink, as when President Kennedy's advisers suspended their judgment before the Bay of Pigs.","A group can fall into groupthink when it gets carried away with its own brilliance, as Enron executives did in believing that because they were brilliant, all of their ideas were brilliant and they were 'bulletproof.'","A fixed-mindset leader who punishes dissent creates groupthink: people stop speaking up even if they keep thinking critically.","Winston Churchill set up a special department whose job was to bring him all the worst news so he would not be groupthinked into a false sense of security.","Alfred P. Sloan of General Motors postponed a decision to 'give ourselves time to develop disagreement,' a technique he used to prevent premature consensus.","David Packard gave a Hewlett-Packard engineer a medal 'for extraordinary contempt and defiance beyond the normal call of engineering duty.'","The book's reading list recommends Irving Janis's Groupthink, 2nd ed., and Jim Collins's Good to Great for understanding how teams decide.","A growth mindset, by relieving people of the illusions or burdens of fixed ability, leads to a full and open discussion of the information and to enhanced decision making."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the book's claim that the fixed mindset produces groupthink: when people believe ability is fixed, groups suppress dissent, put unlimited faith in a leader, or get carried away by their own brilliance, leading to catastrophic decisions. Dweck contrasts this with the growth-minded 'we-think' observed in Robert Wood's groups, where open disagreement and shared learning improved decision making over time.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"question":"In the book's account of Robert Wood's management-group study, the fixed-mindset groups gradually behaved more like a single-minded crowd than a working team. Which column best captures how a fixed-mindset group functions during a joint decision task?","column_a_label":"Groupthink: suppressed dissent and blind consensus","column_b_label":"We-think: open disagreement and shared learning","column_c_label":"Individual display: proving personal smartness","rows":[{"label":"Group members held back disagreement out of fear their ideas would be judged as dumb or disapproved of.","col":"a"},{"label":"Growth-mindset group members stated their honest opinions and openly expressed disagreements as they discussed management decisions.","col":"b"},{"label":"Enron executives believed that because they were brilliant, all of their ideas were brilliant and nothing would ever go wrong.","col":"a"},{"label":"Wood's growth-mindset groups profited from their mistakes and feedback, and the longer they worked the more they outperformed the fixed groups.","col":"b"},{"label":"Chrysler employees stopped speaking up after Lee Iacocca dismissed criticism of his square-car designs, calling them flying potatoes.","col":"a"},{"label":"David Packard gave an engineer a medal for defying him, after the engineer's contested display monitor earned Hewlett-Packard thirty-five million dollars.","col":"b"},{"label":"Wood's fixed-mindset groups worried about who was smart or dumb and about disapproval of their ideas instead of exchanging information openly.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Groupthink is any group that fails to reach unanimous agreement, so a team with lively debate is also groupthinking.","Groupthink only occurs inside corporate boardrooms and never in research settings like Wood's simulated management task.","Groupthink is best cured by trusting one brilliant leader whose judgment the whole group accepts without question."],"explanation":"The target pattern (hesitation, fear of being judged, suppressed disagreement) sorts to column a because Janis defined groupthink as everyone thinking alike with no critical stance, and the book argues fixed-mindset groups drift into it naturally: members worry about who is smart or dumb, avoid stating honest opinions, and fall silent. Column b holds the contrast shown in Wood's study, where growth-mindset groups profited from open disagreement; column c describes a motive (proving yourself) that can feed groupthink but is not the defining behavior of the fixed groups in the study."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"Emotional Intelligence and the Growth Mindset in a Marriage","visual_frame_description":"Priya and Marcus, a married couple in their early thirties, stand on opposite sides of a kitchen island on a Sunday evening in their apartment in Austin. Priya has just set down a mug of tea and looks hurt; Marcus, still in his travel jacket, is avoiding eye contact. Between them a small notepad reads 'Sundays: reconnect.' The player acts as Priya's voice in the conversation.","player_mission":"Guide Priya through the conversation with Marcus, who just returned from a week-long business trip. Choose responses that name her own feelings without blame, drop the expectation that he should read her mind, and treat the conflict as a skill the two of them can develop together rather than as a verdict on the relationship.","key_concepts":["Dweck writes that Daniel Goleman's Emotional Intelligence 'struck such a responsive chord' because it said there are social-emotional skills and they can be identified and learned.","The book argues that as a society we often do not understand relationship skills, even though 'everything is at stake' in people's relationships.","A growth mindset about relationships holds that you, your partner, and the relationship itself are all capable of growth and change.","A fixed mindset about relationships expects 'instant, perfect, and perpetual compatibility,' as if it were meant to be from the start.","Part of the low-effort belief is mind reading: fixed-mindset partners assume the other person should simply understand without being told.","In the book's example, Charlene and Max drifted apart because both felt that if the relationship were the right one, they would not have to work so hard.","People with a fixed mindset feel judged and permanently labeled by rejection, as though a verdict had been handed down about them.","In the growth mindset, a good, lasting relationship comes from effort and from working through inevitable differences.","The book's reading list recommends Daniel Goleman's Emotional Intelligence, John Gottman's Why Marriages Succeed or Fail, and Scott Wetzler's Is It You or Is It Me? for relationship skills.","The book recommends Haim Ginott's Between Parent & Child and Teacher and Child as guides to communicating in ways that build development rather than judgment.","Aaron Beck's Love Is Never Enough is recommended for understanding how distorted thinking can corrode intimate relationships.","Levy's study found that adolescent boys' beliefs in negative stereotypes about girls tracked with their own self-esteem, not with fixed facts about girls."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests Goleman's social-emotional skills as the book frames them, combined with a growth mindset about the relationship: partners who believe love requires no work expect automatic, mind-reading compatibility, while those who believe the relationship can develop treat conflicts as skills to build. Dweck grounds this in her relationship chapter, where effort and working through differences, rather than instant perfection, produce lasting partnerships.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"scenario_setup":"Priya and Marcus have been married for three years. Marcus just returned from a week-long business trip, and Priya is hurt that he never called on the one day she was overwhelmed at work. She has been cold all evening; Marcus feels criticized and keeps retreating into silence. Both are sliding into the patterns the book warns about: the mind-reading expectation ('a partner should just know') and the fixed-mindset belief that if the relationship were right, it would not need work. The stakes are high because a similar blowup last month ended in three days of silence, and the player must keep this conversation from becoming the blame game.","dialogue":["Priya: \"You were gone all week, and the one day I really needed to talk, you never picked up. What am I supposed to think?\"","Marcus: \"I was in back-to-back meetings, Priya. It had nothing to do with you. Why does everything have to be a test?\"","Priya: \"It's not a test. It's that you don't seem to notice when I'm struggling. A partner should just know.\"","Marcus: \"See, that's the thing, you expect me to read your mind. I can't win.\"","Priya: \"So we just spend the whole night not talking, like last time?\"","Marcus: \"I don't know what you want from me. I feel like no matter what I do, I've already failed.\"","Priya: \"Maybe I should just stop bringing things up, then nothing has to be a fight.\""],"choices":["I'm not asking you to read my mind, Marcus. I felt alone that day, and I want us to figure out how we handle days like that together, not against each other.","If you really cared about me, you would have called. This really isn't that complicated.","Fine. Forget it. I shouldn't have said anything, clearly you don't care about my feelings at all.","You're right, I'm being too sensitive. I'll just stop expecting anything from you and we won't talk about it."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"Emotional intelligence as teachable social-emotional skills (Goleman), applied through a growth mindset about the relationship, which treats you, your partner, and the relationship as capable of development.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 works because Priya names her own feeling ('I felt alone') without blaming Marcus, explicitly rejects the mind-reading demand ('I'm not asking you to read my mind'), and reframes the conflict as a shared skill to develop ('figure out how we handle days like that together'). That is the growth-mindset move: effort and working through differences are the path, not proof the relationship was never meant to be. Choice 1 escalates the blame game and keeps the mind-reading assumption, which the book shows fuels retreat and sullen silence. Choice 2 punishes and withdraws, repeating the fixed-mindset pattern of no-responsibility, high-denial fights. Choice 4 buries Priya's genuine need and labels herself as the problem, which develops no skill and guarantees the resentment resurfaces."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Growth vs. Fixed Mindset | Attribution Theory","topic":"From Magic Talent to Optimal Experience (Csikszentmihalyi's Flow)","visual_frame_description":"Nina's sunlit music room in a Portland community school. Eleven-year-old Amir finishes a hard passage and beams, saying it 'just flowed.' Eleven-year-old Lena stands at the door with her bow lowered, watching him with a discouraged expression. On the wall, a shelf of books is lit up, with Csikszentmihalyi's Flow and Sand's Teaching Genius glowing among the titles. A progress bar at the bottom reads 'Boss: The Fixed-Mindset Myth.'","player_mission":"Help Nina dismantle Lena's fixed-mindset belief that musical skill simply flows from a natural gift she lacks. First observe the scene and identify the core belief behind Lena's discouragement, then choose the intervention that steers her toward the growth-minded view that mastery comes from committed, effortful engagement.","key_concepts":["The book's reading list recommends Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi's Flow: The Psychology of Optimal Experience (Harper & Row, 1990).","The book warns that children's learning 'does not just magically flow from some innate ability'; it comes through commitment, effort, and strategy.","In the growth mindset, the hand you're dealt is just the starting point for development.","The book observes that natural talent 'does not need effort' only in the fixed mindset, where 'effort is for the others, the less endowed.'","Barbara Sand's Teaching Genius, the story of Dorothy DeLay and the making of a musician, is recommended as a portrait of how teaching builds ability.","Marva Collins' Way and Benjamin Bloom's Developing Talent in Young People are recommended as accounts of how environments and teaching grow talent.","Effort praise given as a consolation prize when a student has not learned is criticized: process praise must be paired with real learning and new strategies.","Dweck writes that great contributions to society are born of curiosity and deep understanding, not of fixed brilliance alone.","The fixed mindset creates an urgency to prove yourself over and over, while the growth mindset creates an urgency to learn.","Coaches and teachers who embody a growth mindset praise effort, strategy, and progress rather than labeling children as naturals or non-naturals.","The book recommends John Wooden's Wooden and Twyla Tharp's The Creative Habit as examples of how sustained, deliberate practice builds mastery.","Dweck notes that athletes who receive constant praise for their talent, rather than their effort, can easily fall into a fixed mindset."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction between the fixed-mindset myth that ability 'just flows' from innate talent and the growth-minded view that mastery comes from effortful engagement with challenge. The book grounds this in its own caution that learning does not magically flow from innate ability, and in its recommendation of Csikszentmihalyi's Flow (the psychology of optimal experience); Dweck does not summarize flow's internal research in the text, so that specific content is cited but not elaborated.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Empirical education psychology (Dweck mindset research; replication nuance on effect sizes)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The reading list recommends Csikszentmihalyi's Flow: The Psychology of Optimal Experience. The battle tests whether the player can tell the growth-mindset view (skill and optimal experience come from committed, effortful engagement) from the fixed-mindset fantasy that achievement simply flows from a natural gift, which Dweck explicitly warns against when she says learning does not just magically flow from some innate ability.","stage_1_observation":"In Nina's music room, Amir, eleven, finishes a difficult passage at his lesson and beams, saying, 'It just flowed today.' Across the room, Lena, also eleven, stops playing and quietly watches him, gripping her bow. Lena recently told Nina she 'doesn't have the gift,' because Amir never seems to practice yet always sounds better. Nina watches Lena's shoulders slump and recognizes the fixed-mindset belief at work: talent is something that either flows naturally into you or is absent for good.","stage_1_question":"What is the core belief Nina needs to address in Lena?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Lena believes ability is fixed: she sees Amir's skill as flowing from a natural gift he has and she lacks, so she concludes that no amount of practice can close the gap, making effort feel pointless.","stage_2_intervention":"Nina puts down her tuning fork and walks over to Lena, holding the school's reading list. She has to choose how to respond. Option A: tell Lena that some children are simply naturals and that she should focus on the pieces that come easily to her. Option B: praise Lena for how hard she tried today and hand her Sand's Teaching Genius, pointing out how Dorothy DeLay built musicians' skills through relentless, structured practice. Option C: reassure Lena that she is talented too, without mentioning the difficult passage she struggled with. Which intervention best moves Lena toward a growth mindset?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Option B: Nina praises Lena's process rather than her talent, acknowledges the struggle she had with the difficult passage as part of learning, and lends her Sand's Teaching Genius so she can see how mastery is built through practice, reframing 'flow' as the reward of deep, effortful engagement rather than a magical gift."}]}]};
